window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-00"]=[{"id":"preface","q":"preface","s":0,"surah":"Author’s Preface","title":"Author’s preface","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 49–51","cairo":"I:49–51","source_page_first":49,"source_page_last":51,"ar":"بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم (وصلى الله على سيدنا محمد وعلى آله وصحبه وسلّم) قال الشيخ الإمام الحبر الهمام، العارف الرباني والقطب الصمداني، قدوة السالكين ومنار الواصلين، بحر العرفان، ومشرق شمس العيان، مهيع الطريقة، الجامع بين الشريعة وبحر الحقيقة، أبو العباس أحمد بن محمد بن عجيبة الحسنى- رضى الله عنه وأرضاه- آمين:\nنحمدك يا من تجلّى لعباده فى كلامه، بكمال بهائه وجماله، وفتق ألسنة العلماء النحارير لاستخراج درره ولآلئه، وفجّر قلوبهم بينابيع الحكم المؤيدة بأصوله ومبانيه، واستفادوا عند غوصهم فى تياره من فرائده ومثانيه، فدحضوا بآياته الباهرة، وحججه الظاهرة القاهرة شبه من يناويه ويعانيه، والكلّ معترف بالتقصير، مغترف على حسب الفهم والتيسير، من بحر أسراره ومعانيه، فهو البحر الطام الذي لا يدرك له قعر، والروض المونق الذي لا يعدم منه زهر ولا نور، وكيف لا، وهو كلام مولانا العالم بالخفيات، وبما كان وما هو الآن وما هو آت؟!.\nوالصلاة والسلام على سيدنا ومولانا محمد، مظهر الرحمات، المبعوث بخوارق العادات ولوامع البينات، وعلى آله وأصحابه أولى النّدى والسماحة، وجبال اليقين فى اشتداد الأزمات وتفاقم المعضلات.\nوبعد ... فإن علم تفسير القرآن من أجلّ العلوم، وأفضل ما ينفق فيه نتائج الأفكار وقرائح الفهوم، ولكن لا يتقدم لهذا الخطر الكبير إلا العالم النّحرير، الذي رسخت أقدامه فى العلوم الظاهرة، وجالت أفكاره فى معانى القرآن الباهرة، بعد أن تضلّع من العلم الظاهر، عربية وتصريفا ولغة وبيانا، وفقها وحديثا وتاريخا، يكون أخذ ذلك من أفواه الرجال، ثم غاص فى علوم التصوف ذوقا وحالا ومقاما، بصحبة أهل الأذواق من أهل الكمال، وإلا فسكوته عن هذا الأمر العظيم أسلم، واشتغاله بما يقدر عليه من علم الشريعة الظاهرة أتم.\nواعلم أن القرآن العظيم له ظاهر لأهل الظاهر، وباطن لأهل الباطن، وتفسير أهل الباطن لا يذوقه إلا أهل الباطن، لا يفهمه غيرهم ولا يذوقه سواهم، ولا يصح ذكره إلا بعد تقرير الظاهر، ثم يشير إلى علم الباطن بعبارة رقيقة وإشارة دقيقة، فمن لم يبلغ فهمه لذوق تلك الأسرار فليسلّم، ولا يبادر بالإنكار فإنّ علم الأذواق من وراء طور العقول، ولا يدرك بتواتر النقول.\nقال في لطائف المنن: اعلم أن تفسير هذه الطائفة- يعنى الصوفية- لكلام الله وكلام رسوله صلى الله عليه وسلّم بالمعاني الغريبة ليس إحالة للظاهر عن ظاهره، ولكن ظاهر الآية مفهوم منه ما جاءت الآية له ودلت عليه فى حرف اللسان،\nوثمّ أفهام باطنة تفهم عند الآية والحديث لمن فتح الله قلبه، وقد جاء أنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- قال: «لِكلِّ آيةٍ ظَاهرٌ وبَاطِنٌ وحدّ ومُطلع» . فلا يصدّنك عن تلقى المعاني الغريبة منهم أن يقول لك ذو جدل ومعارضة: هذا إحالة لكلام الله- عزّ وجل- وكلام رسوله صلى الله عليه وسلّم فليس بإحالة، وإنما يكون إحالة لكلام الله لو قالوا: لا معنى للآية إلا هذا، وهم لا يقولون ذلك. بل يقرّون الظواهر على ظواهرها ومراداتها وموضوعاتها، ويفهمون عن الله ما أفهمهم. هـ.\nوقال سعد الدين فى شرح عقائد النسفي- بعد إبطال الإلحاد-: (وأمّا ما يذهب إليه بعض المحققين من أن النصوص على ظواهرها، ومع ذلك ففيها إشارات خفية إلى حقائق تنكشف لأرباب السلوك، يمكن التطبيق بينها وبين الظواهر المرادة، فهو من كمال الإيمان ومحض العرفان) وقوله: يمكن التطبيق ... إلخ، أي: يمكن أن يشار إليها فى باطن الخطاب بحيث لا ينبو عنها سرّ الخطاب، ولا يبعد اللفظ عنها كل البعد حتى يكون تحريفا.\nوقال الشيخ زرّوق رضى الله عنه: نظر الصوفي أخص من نظر المفسر وصاحب فقه الحديث، لأن كلا منهما يعتبر الحكم والمعنى ليس إلا وهو يزيد بطلب الإشارة بعد إثبات ما أثبتاه. وإلّا فهو باطني خارج عن الشريعة فضلا عن المتصوفة. والله أعلم. هـ.\nوقوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لِكلِّ آيةٍ ظَاهرٌ وبَاطِنٌ وحدّ ومُطلع» فالظاهر لمن اعتنى بظاهر اللفظ، كالنحاة وأهل اللغة والتصريف، والباطن لمن اعتنى بمعنى اللفظ، وما دلّ عليه الكلام من الأمر والنهى والقصص والأخبار والتوحيد، وغير ذلك من علوم القرآن، وهو نظر المفسرين. والحدّ لمن اعتنى باستنباط الأحكام منه، وهم الفقهاء، فهم ينتهون إلى ما يدل عليه اللفظ وسيق لأجله، دون زيادة عليه. والمطّلع لأهل الحقائق من أكابر الصوفية، لأنهم يطلعون من ظاهر الآية إلى باطنها، فيكشف لهم عن أسرار وعلوم وغوامض، تتجلى لهم عند استعمال الفكرة فيها.\nقال فى الصّحاح: فى الحديث: «من هول المطّلع» ، شبّه ما أشرف عليه من أمر الآخرة بالمطّلع وهو المأتى.\nيقال: أين مطلع هذا الأمر؟ أي: مأتاه، وهو موضع الاطلاع من إشراف إلى انحدار. هـ لأن أهل الحقائق يشرفون من ظاهر الآية إلى أسرار باطنها، ويغوصون فى لجج بحرها. والله تعالى أعلم.\nهذا ... وقد ندبنى شيخى العارف الرباني سيدي محمد البوزيدي الحسني، وكذلك شيخه القطب الجامع شيخ المشايخ مولاي العربي الدرقاوي الحسني، أن أضع تفسيرا يكون جامعا بين تفسير أهل الظاهر وإشارة أهل الباطن، فأجبت سؤالهم وأسعفت طلبتهم، رجاء أن يعمّ به الانتفاع، ويكون ممتعا للقلوب والأسماع. مقدّما في كل آية ما يتعلق بمهمّ العربية واللغة، ثم بمعاني الألفاظ الظاهرة، ثم بالإشارات الباطنة. متوسطا فى ذلك بين الإطناب\nوالاختصار. منتظرا فى ذلك كله ما يفتح علىّ من خزائن الكريم الغفار.. وسميته (البحر المديد في تفسير القرآن المجيد) نسأل الله أن يكسوه جلباب القبول، وأن يبلغ فيه القصد المأمول، إنه على ما يشاء قدير، وبالإجابة جدير، ولا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله العلي العظيم. وصلى الله على سيدنا محمد وآله وصحبه وسلم.\nوقد ذكرت في تفسير الفاتحة الكبير عشر مقدمات تتعلق بأصول العلوم وتفاريعها، وعلوم القرآن وأصل منابعها، فلينظرها من أرادها. وبالله التوفيق.","quran_ar":"","quran_en":"","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The transmitted opening","html":"<p>In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful. God bless our master Muḥammad, his family and his Companions, and grant them peace.<\/p><p>The shaykh, imam, eminent and resolute scholar, God-knowing master and divinely sustained pole, exemplar of wayfarers and beacon of those who have arrived, ocean of gnosis and dawning-place of the sun of direct vision, broad highway of the Path, who unites the Sacred Law with the ocean of Reality—Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAjība al-Ḥasanī, may God be pleased with him and grant him His good pleasure, amen—said:<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-1\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[P01]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful. God bless our master Muḥammad, his family and his Companions, and grant them peace. The shaykh, imam, eminent and resolute scholar, God-knowing master and divinely sustained pole, exemplar of wayfarers and beacon of those who have arrived, ocean of gnosis and dawning-place of the sun of direct vision, broad highway of the Path, who unites the Sacred Law with the ocean of Reality—Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAjība al-Ḥasanī, may God be pleased with him and grant him His good pleasure, amen—said: [P01]"},{"type":"theology","title":"The divine speech and the sciences of interpretation","html":"<p>We praise You, O You who disclose Yourself to Your servants in Your speech, in the perfection of Your splendour and beauty; who loose the tongues of consummate scholars to bring forth its pearls and precious gems, and cause springs of wisdom to gush from their hearts, sustained by its principles and foundations. As they dive into its current, they draw benefit from its unique treasures and reiterated teachings. With its dazzling signs and its manifest, overwhelming proofs, they refute the doubts of those who oppose and resist it. Yet all acknowledge their inadequacy, each drawing from the ocean of its mysteries and meanings according to the understanding and facility granted him. It is the surging ocean whose bottom cannot be reached, the delightful garden never without blossom or flower. And how could it be otherwise, when it is the speech of our Master, who knows hidden things, what has been, what now is, and what is to come?<\/p><p>Blessings and peace be upon our master and patron Muḥammad, the manifestation of mercies, sent with wonders that breach the customary order (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خوارق العادات<\/bdi>) and the radiance of clear proofs; and upon his family and Companions, the possessors of generosity and open-handedness, mountains of certainty amid intensifying crises and ever more difficult perplexities.<\/p><p>To proceed: the science of interpreting the Qurʾān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم تفسير القرآن<\/bdi>) is among the noblest sciences, and among the best pursuits upon which to expend the fruits of reflection and the inventive powers of understanding. Yet none should advance to this momentous undertaking except the consummate scholar whose feet are firmly planted in the outward sciences (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلوم الظاهرة<\/bdi>) and whose thought has ranged through the Qurʾān’s resplendent meanings. He must first have drunk deeply of outward learning: Arabic grammar, morphology, lexicography and rhetoric, jurisprudence, hadith and history—having received these from the mouths of men. He must then have plunged into the sciences of Sufism (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علوم التصوف<\/bdi>), in tasting, state and station (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذوقًا وحالًا ومقامًا<\/bdi>), through the companionship of perfected people of spiritual tasting (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الأذواق<\/bdi>). Otherwise, silence concerning this tremendous matter is safer for him, and occupation with whatever he can manage of the outward Sacred Law is more fitting.<\/p><p>Know that the mighty Qurʾān has an outward aspect (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظاهر<\/bdi>) for the people of the outward and an inward aspect (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">باطن<\/bdi>) for the people of the inward. The interpretation offered by the people of the inward is tasted only by the people of the inward: others neither understand it nor taste it. Nor is it sound to set it forth before establishing the outward meaning. Thereafter one may indicate inward knowledge through a delicate expression and a subtle allusion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إشارة<\/bdi>). Let anyone whose understanding does not reach the tasting of those mysteries yield assent, rather than hasten to deny them. For the knowledge of spiritual tastings (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم الأذواق<\/bdi>) lies beyond the compass of discursive intellects and is not attained through the successive transmission of reports.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-2\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[P02]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"We praise You, O You who disclose Yourself to Your servants in Your speech, in the perfection of Your splendour and beauty; who loose the tongues of consummate scholars to bring forth its pearls and precious gems, and cause springs of wisdom to gush from their hearts, sustained by its principles and foundations. As they dive into its current, they draw benefit from its unique treasures and reiterated teachings. With its dazzling signs and its manifest, overwhelming proofs, they refute the doubts of those who oppose and resist it. Yet all acknowledge their inadequacy, each drawing from the ocean of its mysteries and meanings according to the understanding and facility granted him. It is the surging ocean whose bottom cannot be reached, the delightful garden never without blossom or flower. And how could it be otherwise, when it is the speech of our Master, who knows hidden things, what has been, what now is, and what is to come? Blessings and peace be upon our master and patron Muḥammad, the manifestation of mercies, sent with wonders that breach the customary order ( خوارق العادات ) and the radiance of clear proofs; and upon his family and Companions, the possessors of generosity and open-handedness, mountains of certainty amid intensifying crises and ever more difficult perplexities. To proceed: the science of interpreting the Qurʾān ( علم تفسير القرآن ) is among the noblest sciences, and among the best pursuits upon which to expend the fruits of reflection and the inventive powers of understanding. Yet none should advance to this momentous undertaking except the consummate scholar whose feet are firmly planted in the outward sciences ( العلوم الظاهرة ) and whose thought has ranged through the Qurʾān’s resplendent meanings. He must first have drunk deeply of outward learning: Arabic grammar, morphology, lexicography and rhetoric, jurisprudence, hadith and history—having received these from the mouths of men. He must then have plunged into the sciences of Sufism ( علوم التصوف ), in tasting, state and station ( ذوقًا وحالًا ومقامًا ), through the companionship of perfected people of spiritual tasting ( أهل الأذواق ). Otherwise, silence concerning this tremendous matter is safer for him, and occupation with whatever he can manage of the outward Sacred Law is more fitting. Know that the mighty Qurʾān has an outward aspect ( ظاهر ) for the people of the outward and an inward aspect ( باطن ) for the people of the inward. The interpretation offered by the people of the inward is tasted only by the people of the inward: others neither understand it nor taste it. Nor is it sound to set it forth before establishing the outward meaning. Thereafter one may indicate inward knowledge through a delicate expression and a subtle allusion ( إشارة ). Let anyone whose understanding does not reach the tasting of those mysteries yield assent, rather than hasten to deny them. For the knowledge of spiritual tastings ( علم الأذواق ) lies beyond the compass of discursive intellects and is not attained through the successive transmission of reports. [P02]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The inward sense does not abolish the outward sense","html":"<p>He says in <i>Laṭāʾif al-minan<\/i>: “Know that this community’s interpretation”—meaning the Sufis—“of the speech of God and of His Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace, through unfamiliar meanings is not a diversion of the outward sense from its outward meaning. Rather, the outward aspect of the verse conveys what the verse was revealed for and what it indicates in the language’s expression.<\/p><p>“There are also inward understandings apprehended on hearing a verse or a hadith by those whose hearts God has opened. It has been related that he, blessings and peace be upon him, said: ‘Every verse has an outward aspect, an inward aspect, a limit, and a point of ascent (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظاهر وباطن وحدّ ومطّلع<\/bdi>).’ Do not let a contentious opponent turn you away from receiving unfamiliar meanings from them by saying, ‘This diverts the speech of God, mighty and majestic, and the speech of His Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace.’ It is no such diversion. It would be a diversion of God’s speech only were they to say, ‘The verse has no meaning except this.’ They do not say that. Rather, they confirm the outward senses in their outward meanings, intentions and proper applications, and understand from God what He has enabled them to understand.” End of quotation.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-3\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[P03]<\/a><\/p><p>Saʿd al-Dīn says in his commentary upon al-Nasafī’s <i>Creed<\/i>, after refuting impious distortion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإلحاد<\/bdi>): “As for the view of certain realized authorities that scriptural texts retain their outward meanings, while also containing hidden allusions to realities unveiled to the people of wayfaring, which can be reconciled with the intended outward senses, this is of the perfection of faith and pure gnosis.”<a class=\"note-ref\" href=\"#/note/XVI-N001\">[XVI-N001]<\/a> His words “can be reconciled,” and what follows, mean that these realities may be indicated within the inward dimension of the discourse in such a way that the discourse’s secret sense does not reject them, and the wording is not wholly remote from them, which would amount to distortion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تحريف<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Shaykh Zarrūq, may God be pleased with him, said: “The Sufi’s consideration is more particular than that of the exegete and the interpreter of hadith: each of those considers the ruling and the meaning, and no more, whereas the Sufi adds the search for the allusion after affirming what they have affirmed. Otherwise he is an esotericist (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">باطني<\/bdi>), outside the Sacred Law, let alone the Sufis. And God knows best.” End of quotation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He says in Laṭāʾif al-minan : “Know that this community’s interpretation”—meaning the Sufis—“of the speech of God and of His Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace, through unfamiliar meanings is not a diversion of the outward sense from its outward meaning. Rather, the outward aspect of the verse conveys what the verse was revealed for and what it indicates in the language’s expression. “There are also inward understandings apprehended on hearing a verse or a hadith by those whose hearts God has opened. It has been related that he, blessings and peace be upon him, said: ‘Every verse has an outward aspect, an inward aspect, a limit, and a point of ascent ( ظاهر وباطن وحدّ ومطّلع ).’ Do not let a contentious opponent turn you away from receiving unfamiliar meanings from them by saying, ‘This diverts the speech of God, mighty and majestic, and the speech of His Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace.’ It is no such diversion. It would be a diversion of God’s speech only were they to say, ‘The verse has no meaning except this.’ They do not say that. Rather, they confirm the outward senses in their outward meanings, intentions and proper applications, and understand from God what He has enabled them to understand.” End of quotation. [P03] Saʿd al-Dīn says in his commentary upon al-Nasafī’s Creed , after refuting impious distortion ( الإلحاد ): “As for the view of certain realized authorities that scriptural texts retain their outward meanings, while also containing hidden allusions to realities unveiled to the people of wayfaring, which can be reconciled with the intended outward senses, this is of the perfection of faith and pure gnosis.” [XVI-N001] His words “can be reconciled,” and what follows, mean that these realities may be indicated within the inward dimension of the discourse in such a way that the discourse’s secret sense does not reject them, and the wording is not wholly remote from them, which would amount to distortion ( تحريف ). Shaykh Zarrūq, may God be pleased with him, said: “The Sufi’s consideration is more particular than that of the exegete and the interpreter of hadith: each of those considers the ruling and the meaning, and no more, whereas the Sufi adds the search for the allusion after affirming what they have affirmed. Otherwise he is an esotericist ( باطني ), outside the Sacred Law, let alone the Sufis. And God knows best.” End of quotation."},{"type":"language","title":"Outward aspect, inward aspect, limit and point of ascent","html":"<p>Concerning his saying, blessings and peace be upon him, “Every verse has an outward aspect, an inward aspect, a limit, and a point of ascent”: the outward aspect belongs to those concerned with the wording’s outward form, such as the grammarians, lexicographers and morphologists. The inward aspect belongs to those concerned with the wording’s meaning and what the discourse indicates of command, prohibition, narratives, reports, the affirmation of divine unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد<\/bdi>), and the other sciences of the Qurʾān. This is the exegetes’ consideration. The limit (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحدّ<\/bdi>) belongs to those concerned to derive rulings from it—the jurists—who stop at what the wording indicates and the purpose for which it is expressed, without adding to it. The point of ascent (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المطّلع<\/bdi>) belongs to the people of realities (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الحقائق<\/bdi>), the great Sufis: they look through the verse’s outward aspect into its inward aspect, and mysteries, sciences and subtle matters are unveiled to them, disclosed when they exercise contemplative reflection (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفكرة<\/bdi>) upon it.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-4\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[P04]<\/a><\/p><p>He says in <i>al-Ṣiḥāḥ<\/i>: “In the report, ‘from the terror of the place of arrival (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المطّلع<\/bdi>),’ the affairs of the Hereafter upon which one is about to look are likened to a <i>muṭṭalaʿ<\/i>, that is, a place of approach. One says, ‘Where is the muṭṭalaʿ of this affair?’ meaning, ‘Where is its point of approach?’ It is the place from which one looks down from a height.” End of quotation. For the people of realities look from the verse’s outward aspect into the mysteries of its inward aspect, and dive into the surging depths of its ocean. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Concerning his saying, blessings and peace be upon him, “Every verse has an outward aspect, an inward aspect, a limit, and a point of ascent”: the outward aspect belongs to those concerned with the wording’s outward form, such as the grammarians, lexicographers and morphologists. The inward aspect belongs to those concerned with the wording’s meaning and what the discourse indicates of command, prohibition, narratives, reports, the affirmation of divine unity ( التوحيد ), and the other sciences of the Qurʾān. This is the exegetes’ consideration. The limit ( الحدّ ) belongs to those concerned to derive rulings from it—the jurists—who stop at what the wording indicates and the purpose for which it is expressed, without adding to it. The point of ascent ( المطّلع ) belongs to the people of realities ( أهل الحقائق ), the great Sufis: they look through the verse’s outward aspect into its inward aspect, and mysteries, sciences and subtle matters are unveiled to them, disclosed when they exercise contemplative reflection ( الفكرة ) upon it. [P04] He says in al-Ṣiḥāḥ : “In the report, ‘from the terror of the place of arrival ( المطّلع ),’ the affairs of the Hereafter upon which one is about to look are likened to a muṭṭalaʿ , that is, a place of approach. One says, ‘Where is the muṭṭalaʿ of this affair?’ meaning, ‘Where is its point of approach?’ It is the place from which one looks down from a height.” End of quotation. For the people of realities look from the verse’s outward aspect into the mysteries of its inward aspect, and dive into the surging depths of its ocean. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The commission and arrangement of this commentary","html":"<p>Now my shaykh, the God-knowing, divinely nurtured master Sīdī Muḥammad al-Būzīdī al-Ḥasanī, and likewise his shaykh, the all-embracing pole, master of masters, Mawlāy al-ʿArabī al-Darqāwī al-Ḥasanī, charged me to compose a commentary uniting the interpretation of the people of the outward with the allusion of the people of the inward. I answered their request and fulfilled their wish, hoping that its benefit would extend widely, and that it would delight hearts and ears. For each verse I place first the important matters of Arabic expression and language, then the outward meanings of its words, and then the inward allusions, steering a middle course between prolixity<\/p><p>and excessive brevity. In all this I await whatever is opened to me from the treasuries of the Generous, the All-Forgiving. I have called it <i>al-Baḥr al-madīd fī tafsīr al-Qurʾān al-majīd<\/i>—<i>The Vast Ocean: An Interpretation of the Glorious Qurʾān<\/i>. We ask God to clothe it in the robe of acceptance and bring to fulfilment the purpose for which it is hoped. He has power over whatever He wills and is worthy to answer. There is no power and no strength except through God, the Most High, the Tremendous. God bless our master Muḥammad, his family and his Companions, and grant them peace.<\/p><p>In my larger commentary upon al-Fātiḥa I have set out ten introductions concerning the principles of the sciences and their branches, and the sciences of the Qurʾān and the sources from which they spring. Let anyone who desires them consult them there. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Now my shaykh, the God-knowing, divinely nurtured master Sīdī Muḥammad al-Būzīdī al-Ḥasanī, and likewise his shaykh, the all-embracing pole, master of masters, Mawlāy al-ʿArabī al-Darqāwī al-Ḥasanī, charged me to compose a commentary uniting the interpretation of the people of the outward with the allusion of the people of the inward. I answered their request and fulfilled their wish, hoping that its benefit would extend widely, and that it would delight hearts and ears. For each verse I place first the important matters of Arabic expression and language, then the outward meanings of its words, and then the inward allusions, steering a middle course between prolixity and excessive brevity. In all this I await whatever is opened to me from the treasuries of the Generous, the All-Forgiving. I have called it al-Baḥr al-madīd fī tafsīr al-Qurʾān al-majīd — The Vast Ocean: An Interpretation of the Glorious Qurʾān . We ask God to clothe it in the robe of acceptance and bring to fulfilment the purpose for which it is hoped. He has power over whatever He wills and is worthy to answer. There is no power and no strength except through God, the Most High, the Tremendous. God bless our master Muḥammad, his family and his Companions, and grant them peace. In my larger commentary upon al-Fātiḥa I have set out ten introductions concerning the principles of the sciences and their branches, and the sciences of the Qurʾān and the sources from which they spring. Let anyone who desires them consult them there. And success is through God."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":9232,"arabic_characters":4744},{"id":"fatiha","q":"Introductory discussion","s":1,"surah":"Al-Fātiḥa","title":"Sūrat al-Fātiḥa — introduction, names and background","source":"Cairo I:52–70","cairo":"I:52","source_page_first":52,"source_page_last":52,"ar":"سورة الفاتحة\nمكية. ولها عشرة أسماء: الفاتحة والوافية والكافية والشافية، والسبع المثاني لأنها سبع آيات عند الشافعي منها البسملة، وأسقطها مالك وجعل السابعة: غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ الآية، أو تثنى فى كل صلاة، أو لاشتمالها على الثناء على الله. وأمّ القرآن لأنها مفتتحه ومبدؤه، أو لأنها اشتملت على ما فيه إجمالا على ما يأتى، وسورة الحمد والشكر، وسورة تعليم المسألة، وسورة الصلاة لتكريرها فيها، وأساس القرآن لأنها أصله ومبدؤه ويبنى سائره عليها.\n\nواتفقت المصاحف على افتتاحها ب","quran_ar":"","quran_en":"","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Introduction: names and verse-counting","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:52<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-002\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E52.0\">It is Meccan. It has ten names: the Opening (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفاتحة<\/span>); the Complete (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوافية<\/span>); the Sufficient (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكافية<\/span>); the Healing (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشافية<\/span>); and the Seven Oft-Repeated (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السبع المثاني<\/span>). It is called the Seven Oft-Repeated because, according to al-Shāfiʿī, it consists of seven verses with the basmala counted among them, whereas Mālik does not count the basmala and makes “not those upon whom wrath has fallen, nor those who are astray” the seventh verse; or because it is repeated in every prayer; or because it contains praise of God (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الثناء<\/span>), with an evident play upon the name al-mathānī. It is also called the Mother of the Qurʾān (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أم القرآن<\/span>), because it is the Qurʾān's opening and beginning, or because it contains in summary what the Qurʾān contains, as will be explained; the Sūrah of Praise and Thanksgiving; the Sūrah of Teaching How to Ask; the Sūrah of Prayer, because it is repeatedly recited therein; and the Foundation of the Qurʾān, because it is its root and beginning, upon which the rest is built.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:52 It is Meccan. It has ten names: the Opening ( الفاتحة ); the Complete ( الوافية ); the Sufficient ( الكافية ); the Healing ( الشافية ); and the Seven Oft-Repeated ( السبع المثاني ). It is called the Seven Oft-Repeated because, according to al-Shāfiʿī, it consists of seven verses with the basmala counted among them, whereas Mālik does not count the basmala and makes “not those upon whom wrath has fallen, nor those who are astray” the seventh verse; or because it is repeated in every prayer; or because it contains praise of God ( الثناء ), with an evident play upon the name al-mathānī. It is also called the Mother of the Qurʾān ( أم القرآن ), because it is the Qurʾān's opening and beginning, or because it contains in summary what the Qurʾān contains, as will be explained; the Sūrah of Praise and Thanksgiving; the Sūrah of Teaching How to Ask; the Sūrah of Prayer, because it is repeatedly recited therein; and the Foundation of the Qurʾān, because it is its root and beginning, upon which the rest is built."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1022,"arabic_characters":495},{"id":"fatiha_1","q":"1:1","s":1,"surah":"Al-Fātiḥa","title":"Al-Fātiḥa 1:1 — commentary","source":"Cairo I:52–70","cairo":"I:52–53","source_page_first":52,"source_page_last":53,"ar":"بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ واختلف الأئمة فيها، فقال مالك: ليست آية لا من الفاتحة ولا من غيرها إلا من النمل خاصة، وقال الشافعي: هى آية من الفاتحة فقط، وقال ابن عباس: هي آية من كل سورة.\nفحجة مالك: ما فى الصحيح عنه صلى الله عليه وسلّم قال: «أنزلت علىّ سورة ليس في التوراة ولا في الإنجيل ولا في الفرقان مثلها، ثم قال: الحمد لله رب العالمين) ولم يذكر البسملة. وكذلك ما ورد فى الصحيح أيضا أن الله يقول: «قسمت الصّلاة بينى وبين عبدى نصفين. يقول العبد: الحمد لله رب العالمين» فبدأ بها دون البسملة.\nوحجة الشافعي: ما ورد فى الصحيح «إن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلّم كان يقرأ بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم. الحمد لله رب العالمين» . وحجة ابن عباس: ثبوت البسملة مع كل سورة فى المصحف، مع تحرّى الصحابة ألا يدخلوا فى المصحف غير كلام الله، وقالوا: ما بين الدفّتين كلام الله.\nوإذا ابتدأت أوّل سورة بسملت إلا براءة، وسيأتى الكلام عليها. وإذا ابتدأت جزء سورة فأنت مخير عند الجمهور.\nوإذا أتممت سورة وابتدأت أخرى فاختلف القرّاء فى البسملة وتركها.\nوأما حكمها فى الصلاة، فقال مالك: مكروهة فى الفرض دون النفل، وقال الشافعي: فرض تبطل الصلاة بتركها، فيبسمل- عنده- جهرا فى الجهر وسرا فى السر، وعند أبى حنيفة كذلك إلا أنه يسرّها مطلقا، وحجة مالك أنها ليست بآية: ما فى الحديث الصحيح عن أنس أنه قال: (صلّيت خلف النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم وأبى بكر وعمر وعثمان، فكانوا يستفتحون بالحمد لله رب العالمين) لا يذكرون البسملة أصلا. وحجة الشافعي أنها عنده آية: ما ورد فى الحديث من قراءتها كما تقدم.\nولم تكن البسملة قبل الإسلام، فكانوا يكتبون: باسمك اللهم، حتى نزلت بِسْمِ اللَّهِ مَجْراها فكتبوا بِسْمِ اللَّهِ حتى نزل: ... أَوِ ادْعُوا الرَّحْمنَ فكتبوا: بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمنِ حتى نزل: ... وَإِنَّهُ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ، فكتبوها.\nوحذفت الألف لكثرة الاستعمال، والباء متعلقة بمحذوف، اسم عند البصريين، أي: ابتدائى كائن بسم الله، فموضعها رفع. وفعل عند الكوفيين، أي: أبدأ أو أتلو. فيقدّر كل واحد ما جعلت البسملة مبدأ له، فموضعها نصب، ويقدر مؤخرا لإفادة الحصر والاختصاص. وهو مشتق من السّموّ عند البصريين، فلامه محذوفة، وعند الكوفيين من السّمة، أي: العلامة، ففاؤه محذوفة، ودليل البصريين: التصغير والتكسير، فقالوا: أسماء، ولم يقولوا أوسام، وقالوا:\nسمى، ولم يقولوا: وسيم.\nو (الله) علم على الذات الواجبة الوجود، المستحق لجميع المحامد، وهل هو مشتق أو مرتجل؟ قولان يأتى الكلام عليهما فى (الحمد لله) ، وكذلك (الرحمن الرحيم) .\nقال الحق جل جلاله:","quran_ar":"بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ (1)","quran_en":"“In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful.”","segments":[{"type":"legal","title":"The basmala: Qurʾānic status and evidence","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-004\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E52.1\">The written copies of the Qurʾān agree in opening it with “In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful.” The imams disagree concerning this formula. Mālik said that it is not a verse of al-Fātiḥa or of any other sūrah, except in al-Naml alone. Al-Shāfiʿī said that it is a verse of al-Fātiḥa alone. Ibn ʿAbbās said that it is a verse of every sūrah.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-005\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E52.2\">Mālik's evidence is the authentic report in which the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, said: “A sūrah has been sent down to me whose like is not in the Torah, the Gospel, or the Criterion.” He then said, “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,” without mentioning the basmala. There is likewise the authentic report that God says: “I have divided the prayer between Myself and My servant into two halves. The servant says, ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.’” He thus begins with this, without the basmala.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-006\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E52.3\">Al-Shāfiʿī's evidence is the authentic report that the Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, used to recite: “In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful. Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.” Ibn ʿAbbās's evidence is that the basmala is written with every sūrah in the written copy of the Qurʾān, despite the Companions' care not to introduce anything into it other than God's speech. They said: “What is between the two covers is God's speech.”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"The written copies of the Qurʾān agree in opening it with “In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful.” The imams disagree concerning this formula. Mālik said that it is not a verse of al-Fātiḥa or of any other sūrah, except in al-Naml alone. Al-Shāfiʿī said that it is a verse of al-Fātiḥa alone. Ibn ʿAbbās said that it is a verse of every sūrah. Mālik's evidence is the authentic report in which the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, said: “A sūrah has been sent down to me whose like is not in the Torah, the Gospel, or the Criterion.” He then said, “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,” without mentioning the basmala. There is likewise the authentic report that God says: “I have divided the prayer between Myself and My servant into two halves. The servant says, ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.’” He thus begins with this, without the basmala. Al-Shāfiʿī's evidence is the authentic report that the Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, used to recite: “In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful. Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.” Ibn ʿAbbās's evidence is that the basmala is written with every sūrah in the written copy of the Qurʾān, despite the Companions' care not to introduce anything into it other than God's speech. They said: “What is between the two covers is God's speech.”"},{"type":"legal","title":"Recitation and prayer rulings","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-007\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E52.4\">When you begin at the opening of a sūrah, recite the basmala, except in Barāʾa [al-Tawba], which will be discussed in its place. When you begin within a sūrah, the majority give you a choice. When you complete one sūrah and begin another, the Qurʾānic readers differ over whether to recite or omit the basmala.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-008\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E52.5\">As for its ruling in prayer, Mālik said that it is disliked in obligatory prayer, but not in voluntary prayer. Al-Shāfiʿī said that it is obligatory and that omitting it invalidates the prayer. According to him, therefore, one recites it aloud in audible prayer and silently in silent prayer. With Abū Ḥanīfa it is likewise, except that he recites it silently in every case. Mālik's evidence that it is not a verse is the authentic report from Anas, who said: “I prayed behind the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān, and they used to begin with ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,’” not mentioning the basmala at all. Al-Shāfiʿī's evidence that it is a verse, in his view, is the report of its recitation already mentioned.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"When you begin at the opening of a sūrah, recite the basmala, except in Barāʾa [al-Tawba], which will be discussed in its place. When you begin within a sūrah, the majority give you a choice. When you complete one sūrah and begin another, the Qurʾānic readers differ over whether to recite or omit the basmala. As for its ruling in prayer, Mālik said that it is disliked in obligatory prayer, but not in voluntary prayer. Al-Shāfiʿī said that it is obligatory and that omitting it invalidates the prayer. According to him, therefore, one recites it aloud in audible prayer and silently in silent prayer. With Abū Ḥanīfa it is likewise, except that he recites it silently in every case. Mālik's evidence that it is not a verse is the authentic report from Anas, who said: “I prayed behind the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān, and they used to begin with ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,’” not mentioning the basmala at all. Al-Shāfiʿī's evidence that it is a verse, in his view, is the report of its recitation already mentioned."},{"type":"exposition","title":"History of the written formula","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:53<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-009\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.0\">The basmala was not in use before Islam. People used to write, “In Your Name, O God,” until “In the Name of God is its sailing” [11:41] was revealed, whereupon they wrote, “In the Name of God.” Then “or call upon the All-Merciful” [17:110] was revealed, and they wrote, “In the Name of God, the All-Merciful.” Finally, “and it is: In the Name of God, the All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful” [27:30] was revealed, and they wrote it in this form.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:53 The basmala was not in use before Islam. People used to write, “In Your Name, O God,” until “In the Name of God is its sailing” [11:41] was revealed, whereupon they wrote, “In the Name of God.” Then “or call upon the All-Merciful” [17:110] was revealed, and they wrote, “In the Name of God, the All-Merciful.” Finally, “and it is: In the Name of God, the All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful” [27:30] was revealed, and they wrote it in this form."},{"type":"language","title":"Grammar and derivation of the basmala","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-010\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.1\">The alif of ism in bism is omitted because of frequent usage. The preposition bāʾ is connected to an elided governing element (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">متعلِّقة بمحذوف<\/span>). According to the Basrans, the ellipsis is nominal: “My beginning is [made] in the Name of God” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ابتدائي كائن بسم الله<\/span>), so its syntactic locus is nominative (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">موضعها الرفع<\/span>). According to the Kufans, the ellipsis is verbal: “I begin” or “I recite,” so each speaker supplies the action for which the basmala is made the beginning, and its syntactic locus is accusative (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">موضعها النصب<\/span>). The omitted element is understood after the basmala in order to convey restriction (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحصر<\/span>) and exclusive specification (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاختصاص<\/span>).<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-011\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.2\">According to the Basrans, ism (“name”) is derived (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مشتق<\/span>) from sumuww (“elevation”), with the final radical - the lām of the root (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لام الكلمة<\/span>) - omitted. According to the Kufans it is derived from sima (“mark, sign”; <span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السِّمة، أي العلامة<\/span>), with the first radical - the fāʾ of the root (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فاء الكلمة<\/span>) - omitted. The Basrans appeal to the diminutive (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التصغير<\/span>) and the broken plural (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التكسير<\/span>): one says asmāʾ, not awsām, and sumayy, not wusaym.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-012\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.3\">“God” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الله<\/span>) is a proper name (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عَلَم<\/span>) for the Essence that is Necessary in Existence (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الذات الواجبة الوجود<\/span>) and deserving of every praise. Whether the name is derived (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مشتق<\/span>) or independently coined (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مرتجل<\/span>) is disputed. The two positions will be discussed under “Praise belongs to God,” as will the names al-Raḥmān and al-Raḥīm.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"transition\" id=\"pair-013\"><p class=\"enp\">The Real, glorious is His majesty, says:<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"The alif of ism in bism is omitted because of frequent usage. The preposition bāʾ is connected to an elided governing element ( متعلِّقة بمحذوف ). According to the Basrans, the ellipsis is nominal: “My beginning is [made] in the Name of God” ( ابتدائي كائن بسم الله ), so its syntactic locus is nominative ( موضعها الرفع ). According to the Kufans, the ellipsis is verbal: “I begin” or “I recite,” so each speaker supplies the action for which the basmala is made the beginning, and its syntactic locus is accusative ( موضعها النصب ). The omitted element is understood after the basmala in order to convey restriction ( الحصر ) and exclusive specification ( الاختصاص ). According to the Basrans, ism (“name”) is derived ( مشتق ) from sumuww (“elevation”), with the final radical - the lām of the root ( لام الكلمة ) - omitted. According to the Kufans it is derived from sima (“mark, sign”; السِّمة، أي العلامة ), with the first radical - the fāʾ of the root ( فاء الكلمة ) - omitted. The Basrans appeal to the diminutive ( التصغير ) and the broken plural ( التكسير ): one says asmāʾ, not awsām, and sumayy, not wusaym. “God” ( الله ) is a proper name ( عَلَم ) for the Essence that is Necessary in Existence ( الذات الواجبة الوجود ) and deserving of every praise. Whether the name is derived ( مشتق ) or independently coined ( مرتجل ) is disputed. The two positions will be discussed under “Praise belongs to God,” as will the names al-Raḥmān and al-Raḥīm. The Real, glorious is His majesty, says:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4387,"arabic_characters":2215},{"id":"fatiha_2","q":"1:2–4","s":1,"surah":"Al-Fātiḥa","title":"Al-Fātiḥa 1:2–4 — commentary","source":"Cairo I:52–70","cairo":"I:53–59","source_page_first":53,"source_page_last":59,"ar":"الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ (2) الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ (3) مالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ (4)\nقلت: (الحمد) مبتدأ، و (لله) خبر، وأصله النصب، وقرئ به، والأصل: أحمد الله حمداً، وإنما عدل عنه إلى الرفع ليدل على عموم الحمد وثباته، دون تجدده وحدوثه، وفيه تعليم اللفظ مع تعريض الاستغناء. أي: الحمد لله وإن لم تحمدوه. ولو قال (أحمد الله) لما أفاد هذا المعنى، وهو من المصادر التي تُنْصَب بأفعال مضمرة لا تكاد تذكر معها. والتعريف للجنس أي: للحقيقة من حيث هي، من غير قيد شيوعها، ومعناه: الإشارة إلى ما يَعْرِفه كل أحد أن الحمد ما هو. أو للاستغراق إذ الحمد في الحقيقة كُلُّه لله إذ ما من خير إلا وهو مُولِيهِ بواسطة وبغير واسطة. كما قال: وَما بِكُمْ مِنْ نِعْمَةٍ فَمِنَ اللَّهِ وقيل: للعهد، والمعهودُ حمدُه تعالى نفسه في أزله.\nوقُرِئ (الحمد لله) بإتباع الدال للام(1)، وبالعكس(2)، تنزيلاً لهما من حيث إنهما يستعملان معاً منزلة كلمة واحدة.\nومعناه في اللغة: الثناءُ بالجميل على قصد التعظيم والتبجيل، وفي العُرف: فعل يُنبئ عن تعظيم المُنعم بسبب كونه منعماً. والشكر في اللغة: فعل يُشعر بتعظيم المنعم، فهو مرادف للحمد العرفي، وفي العرف: صرف\nالعبد جميعَ ما أنعم الله عليه من السمع والبصر إلى ما خُلِقَ لأجله وأعطاه إياه. وانظر شرحنا الكبير للفاتحة في النَّسَبِ التي بيناها نظماً ونثراً.\nو (الله) اسم مُرْتَجَلٌ جامد، والألف واللام فيه لازمة لا للتعريف، قال الواحدي: اسم تفرِّد به الباري- سبحانه- يجري في وصفه مجرى الأسماء الأعلام، لا يُعرف له اشتقاق، وقال الأقْلِيشي: إن هذا الاسم مهما لم يكن مشتقّاً كان دليلاً على عين الذات، دون أن يُنظر فيها إلى صفة من الصفات، وليس باسمٍ مشتق من صفة، كالعالِم والحق والخالق والرازق، فالألف واللام على هذا في (الله) من نفس الكلمة، كالزاي من زيد، وذهب إلى هذا جماعة، واختاره الغزالي وقال: كل ما قيل في اشتقاقه فهو تعسُّف.\nوقيل: مشتق من التَّأَلُّهِ وهو التعبد، وقيل: من الوَلَهَان، وهو الحيرة لتحيُّر العقول في شأنه. وقيل: أصله:\nالإلهُ، ثم حذفت الهمزة ونقلت حركتها إلى اللام، ثم وقع الإدغام وفُخمت للتعظيم، إلا إذا كان قبلها كسر.\nو (رب) نعت (لله) ، وهو في الأصل: مصدر بمعنى التربية، وهو تبليغ الشيء إلى كماله شيئاً فشيئاً، ثم وُصف به للمبالغة كالصوم والعدل.\nوقيل: هو وصفٌ من رَبِّه يَرُبُّهُ، وأصله: رَبَبَ ثم أُدغم، سُمي به المالكُ لأنه يحفظ ما يملكه ويربيه، ولا يطلق على غيره تعالى إلا بقيد كقوله تعالى: ارْجِعْ إِلى رَبِّكَ. قال ابن جُزَيّ: ومعانيه أربعة: الإله والسيد والمالك والمصلح، وكلها تصلح في رب العالمين، إلا أن الأرجح في معناه: الإله لاختصاصه بالله تعالى.\nو (العالمين) جمع عالَم، والعالَمُ: اسم لما يُعْلَمُ به، كالخاتم لما يُختم به، والطابع لما يطبع به. غلب فيما يُعلم به الصانع. وهو كل ما سواه من الجواهر والأعراض، فإنها لإمكانها وافتقارها إلى مُؤثِرٍ واجبٍ لذاته، تدل على وجوده، وإنما جُمع ليشمل ما تحته من الأجناس المختلفة، وغلب العقلاء منهم فجُمِعَ بالياء والنون كسائر أوصافهم، فهو جمع، لا اسم جمع، خلافاً لابن مالك.\nوقيل: اسم وضع لذوي العلم من الملائكة والثقلين، وتناولُه لغيرهم على سبيل الاستتباع، وقيل: عنى به هذا الناس، فإن كل واحد منهم عالَمٌ، حيث إنه يشتمل على نظائر ما في العالم الكبير، ولذا سوّى بين النظر فيهما فقال:\nوَفِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ أَفَلا تُبْصِرُونَ.\nقلت: وإليه يشير قول الشاعر:\nيا تَائهاً في مَهْمَهٍ عَنْ سِرِّه ... انْظُرْ تجِدْ فِيكَ الوُجُودُ بأَسْره\nأنْتَ الكمَالُ طَرِيقَةً وحَقِيقَةً ... يا جَامِعاً سِرَّ الإلّهِ بأسره\nوالرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ اسمان بُنيا للمبالغة، من رَحِمَ، كالغضبان من غضب، والعليم من علم، والرحمة في اللغة: رَقَّةُ القلب، وانعطافٌ يقتضي التفضل والإحسان، ومنه الرَّحِم لانعطافها على ما فيها. وأسماء الله تعالى إنما تُؤخذ باعتبار الغايات، التي هي أفعال، دون المبادئ التي هي انفعالات. و (الرحمن) أبلغ من (الرحيم) لأن زيادةَ المبنى تدل على زيادة المعنى، كقَطَّعَ وقَطَعَ، وذلك إنما يُؤخذ تارة باعتبار الكمية، وأخرى باعتباره الكيفية.\nفعلى الأول: قيل: يا رحمنَ الدنيا لأنه يَعُمُّ المؤمنَ والكافر، ورحيمَ الآخرة لأنه يختص بالمؤمن، وعلى الثاني قيل: يا رحمن الدنيا والآخرة ورحيم الدنيا لأن النعم الأخروية كلها جِسَام، وأما النعم الدنيوية فجليلة وحقيرة.\nوإنما قدّم (الرحمن) - والقياس الترقي من الأدنى إلى الأعلى- لتقدُّم رحمة الدنيا، ولأنه صار كالعَلَم من حيث إنه لا يُوصف به غيره لأن معناه المنعم الحقيقي البالغُ في الرحمة غايتَها، وذلك لا يصدُق على غيره تعالى.\nانظر البيضاوي. وسيأتي الكلام عليهما في المعنى.\nو (مَلِكَ) نِعت لما قبله، قراءةُ الجماعة بغير ألف من (المُلك) بالضم، وقرأ عاصم والكسائي بالألف، من (المِلك) بالكسر، والتقدير على هذا: مالك مجئ يوم الدين، أو مالك الأمر يوم الدين. وقراءةُ الجماعة أرجح، لثلاثة أوجه:\nالأول: أن الملك أعظم من مالك، إذ قد يوصف كل أحد بالمالك لماله، وأما المَلِكُ فهو سيد الناس، والثاني: قوله:\nوَلَهُ الْمُلْكُ يَوْمَ يُنْفَخُ فِي الصُّورِ، والثالث: أنها لا تقتضي حذفا، والحذف خلاف الأصل(1).\nو (يوم الدين) ظرف مضاف إلى ما قبله على طريق الاتساع، وأُجري الظرف مجرى المفعول به، والمعنى على الظرفية، أي: الملك في يوم الدين، أو ملك الأمر يوم الدين، فيكون فيه حذف. وقد رُويت القراءتان- أي:\nالقصر والمد- عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم.\nوقد قرئ (ملك) بوجوه كثيرة تركنا ذكرها لشذوذها. فإن قيل: ملك ومالك نكرة لأن إضافة اسم الفاعل لا تُخصص، وكيف ينعت به (الرحمن الرحيم) وهما معرفتان؟ قلت: إنما تكون إضافةُ اسم الفاعل لا تخصص إذا كان بمعنى الحال أو الاستقبال لأنها حينئذٍ غيرُ مَحْضَةٍ، وأما هذا فهو مستمر دائماً، فإضافته محضة. قاله ابن جزي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله معلما لعباده كيف يُثْنُونَ عليه ويعظمونه ثم يسألونه: يا عبادي قولوا: الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ أي: الثناء الجميل إنما يستحقه العظيم الجليل، فلا يستحق الحمدَ سواه، إذ لا منعم على الحقيقة إلا الله، وَما بِكُمْ مِنْ نِعْمَةٍ فَمِنَ اللَّهِ. أو جميعُ المحامدِ كلُّها لله، أو الحمدُ المعهودُ في الأذهان هو حمدُ الله تعالى نفسَه في أزله، قبل أن يُوجِدَ خلقّه، فلما أوجد خلقه قال لهم: الحمد لله، أي: احمدونى بذلك الحمد المعهود في الأزل.\nوإنما استحق الحمد وحده لأنه رَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ، وكأن سائلاً سأله: لم اختصصت بالحمد؟ فقال: لأني ربُّ العالمين، أنا أوجدتُهم برحمتي، وأمددتهم بنعمتي، فلا منعم غيري، فاستحققت الحمد وحدي، مِنِّي كان الإيجاد وعليَّ توالي الإِمْدَاد، فأنا ربُّ العباد، فالعوالم كلها- على تعدد أجناسها واختلاف أنواعها- في قبضتي وتحت تربيتي ورعايتي.\nقال بعضهم: خلق الله ثمانيةَ عَشرَ ألف عالَم، نصفها في البر ونصفها في البحر. وقال الفخرُ الرازي: رُوِيَ أن بني آدم عُشْرُ الجن، وبنو آدم والجنُ عُشْرُ حيوانات البر، وهؤلاء كلُّهم عشر الطيور، وهؤلاء كلهم عشر حيوانات البحار، وهؤلاء كلهم عشر ملائكة الأرض الموكلين ببني آدم، وهؤلاء كلهم عشر ملائكة سماء الدنيا، وهؤلاء كلهم عشر ملائكة السماء الثانية، ثم على هذا الترتيب إلى ملائكة السماء السابعة، ثم الكلُّ في مقابلة الكرسي نَزْرٌ قليل، ثم هؤلاء عشر ملائكة السُّرَادِق الواحد من سُرادقات العرش، التي عددُها: مائةُ ألف، طول كل سرادق وعرضُه- إذا قُوبلتْ به السموات والأرض وما فيهما وما بينهما- يكون شيئاً يسيراً ونَزْراً قليلاً. وما من موضع شِبْرٍ إلا وفيه مَلَكٌ ساجد أو راكع أو قائم، وله زَجَل بالتسبيح والتهليل. ثم هؤلاء كلهم في مقابلة الذين يَجُولُون حول العرش كالقطرة في البحر، ولا يَعلم عددهم إلا الله تعالى: وَما يَعْلَمُ جُنُودَ رَبِّكَ إِلَّا هُوَ. هـ.\nوقال وَهْبُ بن منبه: (قوائم العرش ثلاثُمائةٍ وست وستون قائمة، وبين كل قائمة وقائمة ستون ألف صحراء، وفي كل صحراء ستون ألف عالم، وكل عالم قَدْرُ الثقلين) .\nفهذه العوالم كلها في قبضة الحق وتحت تربيته وحفظه، يوصل المدد إلى كل واحد وهو في مستقرِّه ومستودعه، إما إلى روحانيته من قوة العلوم والمعارف، وإما إلى بشريته من قوة الأشباح، من العرش إلى الفرش،\nكلها مقدَّرة أرزاقها محصورة آجالها، محفوظة أشباحها، معلومة أماكنها، لا يخفى عليه شيء في الأرض ولا في السماء وهو السميع العليم.\nثم هذه التربية التي ربى سبحانه بها خلقه إنما هي رحمة منه وإحسان، لا لزوم عليه وإيجاب، ولذلك وصلَه بقوله الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ، أي: الرحمن بنعمة الإيجاد، الرحيم بنعمة الإمداد. «نعمتان ما خلا موجود عنهما، ولا بد لكل مُكَوَّنَ منهما: نعمة الإيجاد ونعمة الإمداد، أنعم أولاً بالإيجاد، وثنى بتوالي الإمداد» . كما في (الحِكَم)(1). فاسمُه (الرحمن) يقتضي إيجادَ الأشياء وإبرازها، واسمه (الرحيم) يقتضي تربيتَها وإمدادها. ولذلك لا يجوز إطلاق اسم (الرحمن) على أحد، ولم يَتَسَمَّ أحد به إذ الإيجاد لا يصح من غيره تعالى، بخلاف اسمه (الرحيم) فيجوز إطلاقه على غيره تعالى لمشاركة صدور الإمداد في الظاهر من بعض المخلوقات مجازاً وعاريةً.\nأو: الرحمن في الدنيا والآخرة، والرحيم في الآخرة لأن رحمة الآخرة خاصة بالمؤمنين. أو الرحمن بجلائل النِعم والرحيم بدقائقها، فجلائل النعم مثل: نعمة الإسلام والإيمان والإحسان، والمعرفة والهداية، وكشف الحجاب وفتح الباب والدخول مع الأحباب، ودقائقُ النعم مثل: الصحة والعافية والمال الحلال، وغير ذلك مما يأتي ذكره في المُنْعَم عليهم.\nثم من تحقق منه الإيجادُ والإمداد استحق أن يكون ملكاً لجميع العباد، ولذلك ذكرَهِ بِأَثَره فقال: مَلِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ أي: المتصرف في عباده كيف شاء، لا راد لما قضى ولا مانع لما أعطى، فهو ملكُ الملوك رب الأرباب في هذه الدار وفي تلك الدار. وإنما خصّ يوم الدين- وهو يوم الجزاء- بالملكية لأن ذلك اليوم يظهرُ فيه المُلْكُ لله عيَاناً لجميع الخلق، فإن الله تعالى يتجلّى لفصل عباده، حتى يراه المؤمنون عياناً، بخلاف الدنيا فإن تصرفه تعالى لا يفهمه إلا الكَمَلَةُ من المؤمنين، ولذلك ادَّعى كثير من الجهلة الملكَ ونسبوه لأنفسهم. ويوم القيامة ينفرد الملك لله عند الخاص والعام، قال تعالى: لِمَنِ الْمُلْكُ الْيَوْمَ لِلَّهِ الْواحِدِ الْقَهَّارِ.\nالإشارة: لمّا تجلّى الحقّ سبحانه من عالَم الجبروت إلى عالم الملكوت، أو تقول: من عالم الغيب إلى عالم الشهادة، حمد نفسه بنفسه، ومجَّد نفسه بنفسه، ووحَّد نفسه بنفسه، ولله دَرُّ الهَرَوِيّ، حيث قال:\nمَا وَحَّدَ الواحِدَ مِنْ واحِدِ ... إِذْ كُلُّ مَنْ وحّده جاحد\nتوحيدُ مَنْ ينطقُ عَنْ نَعْتِهِ ... عاريةُ أَبْطَلَهَا الواحِدُ\nتوحيدُه إِيّاه توحيدُه ... ونعتُ من يَنْعَتُه لاَحِدُ(1)\nفقال في توحيد نفسه بنفسه مترجماً عن نفسه بنفسه: (الحمد لله رب العالمين) ، فكأنه يقول في عنوان كتابه وسر خطابه: أنا الحامد والمحمود، وأنا القائم بكل موجود، أنا رب الأرباب، وأنا مسبب الأسباب لمن فهم الخطاب، أنا رب العالمين، أنا قيوم السموات والأرَضين، بل أنا المتوحِّدُ في وجودي، والمتجلِّي لعبادي بكرمي وجودي، فالعوالم كلها ثابتة بإثباتي، مَمْحُوَّةٌ بأحدية ذاتي.\nقال رجل بين يدي الجنيد: (الحمد لله) ولم يقل: (رب العالمين) ، فقال له الجنيد: كَمِّلْهَا يا أخي، فقال الرجل: وأيّ قَدْر للعالمين حتى تُذكر معه؟! فقال الجنيد: قُلها يا أخي فإن الحادث إذا قُرن بالقديم تلاشى الحادث وبقي القديم.\nيقول سبحانه: يا مَن هو مني قريب، تَدبر سِرِّي فإنه غريب، أنا المحبُ، وأنا الحبيب، وأنا القريب، وأنا المجيب، أنا الرحيم الرحمن، وأنا الملك الديّان، أنا الرحمن بنعمة الإيجاد، والرحيمُ بتوالي الإمداد. منِّي كان الإيجاد، وعليَّ دوام الإمداد، وأنا رب العباد، أنا الملك الديَّان، وأنا المجازي بالإحسان على الإحسان، أنا الملك على الإطلاق، لولا جهالة أهل العناد والشقاق، الأمر لنا على الدوام، لمن فهم عنا من الأنام.\nقال في الرسائل الكبرى(2): لا عبرة بظواهر الأشياء، وإنما العبرة بالسر المكنون، وليس ذلك إلا بظهور أمر الحق وارتفاع غَطَائه وزوال أستاره وخفائه، فإذا تحقق ذلك التجلّي والظهور، واستولى على الأشياء الفناءُ والدُّثُور، وانقشعت الظلمات بإشراق النور، فهناك يبدو عين اليقين ويَحِقُّ الحق المبين، وعند ذلك تبطل دعوى المدعين، كما يفهم العامة بطلان ذلك في يوم الدين، حين يكون الملك لله رب العالمين، وليت شعري أيُّ وقت كان الملكُ لسواه حتى يقع التقييد بقوله: الْمُلْكُ يَوْمَئِذٍ لِلَّهِ وقوله: وَالْأَمْرُ يَوْمَئِذٍ لِلَّهِ؟! لولا الدعاوَى العريضة من القلوب المريضة. هـ.\nثم تنزل لبيان العبودية، فقال:","quran_ar":"الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ (2) الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ (3) مالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ (4)","quran_en":"“Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds, the Compassionate, the Merciful, Master of the Day of Recompense.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Al-ḥamd: syntax, definiteness and readings","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-015\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.4\">I say: “Praise” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحمد<\/span>) is the subject (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ<\/span>), and “belongs to God” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لله<\/span>) is the predicate (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/span>). Its underlying construction is accusative (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النصب<\/span>), and it has in fact been recited that way; the underlying expression is “I praise God with praise” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أحمد الله حمداً<\/span>). It was shifted from the accusative to the nominative (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرفع<\/span>) in order to signify the universality and abiding stability of praise, rather than its renewed occurrence and origination. The form also teaches the very words to be uttered while intimating God's independence of their praise: praise belongs to God even if you do not praise Him. Had He said, “I praise God,” this meaning would not have been conveyed. Ḥamd is one of those verbal nouns (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المصادر<\/span>) that are put in the accusative by implicit verbs (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أفعال مضمرة<\/span>) which are scarcely ever expressed alongside them.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-016\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.5\">The definite article (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التعريف<\/span>) may be generic (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للجنس<\/span>): it then denotes the reality as such, without making its universal extension a condition, and points to what everyone already recognises praise to be. Or it may be exhaustive (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للاستغراق<\/span>): in reality every praise belongs to God, since there is no good that He does not bestow, either through an intermediary or without one, as He says, “Whatever blessing you have is from God” [16:53]. Or it may be referential (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للعهد<\/span>), the referent being God's praise of Himself in His beginningless eternity.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-017\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.6\">“Praise belongs to God” has also been recited with the vowel of the dāl made to follow that of the lām, and conversely (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بإتباع الدال للام وبالعكس<\/span>), treating the two, because of their habitual conjunction, as though they were a single word.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Praise” ( الحمد ) is the subject ( مبتدأ ), and “belongs to God” ( لله ) is the predicate ( خبر ). Its underlying construction is accusative ( النصب ), and it has in fact been recited that way; the underlying expression is “I praise God with praise” ( أحمد الله حمداً ). It was shifted from the accusative to the nominative ( الرفع ) in order to signify the universality and abiding stability of praise, rather than its renewed occurrence and origination. The form also teaches the very words to be uttered while intimating God's independence of their praise: praise belongs to God even if you do not praise Him. Had He said, “I praise God,” this meaning would not have been conveyed. Ḥamd is one of those verbal nouns ( المصادر ) that are put in the accusative by implicit verbs ( أفعال مضمرة ) which are scarcely ever expressed alongside them. The definite article ( التعريف ) may be generic ( للجنس ): it then denotes the reality as such, without making its universal extension a condition, and points to what everyone already recognises praise to be. Or it may be exhaustive ( للاستغراق ): in reality every praise belongs to God, since there is no good that He does not bestow, either through an intermediary or without one, as He says, “Whatever blessing you have is from God” [16:53]. Or it may be referential ( للعهد ), the referent being God's praise of Himself in His beginningless eternity. “Praise belongs to God” has also been recited with the vowel of the dāl made to follow that of the lām, and conversely ( بإتباع الدال للام وبالعكس ), treating the two, because of their habitual conjunction, as though they were a single word."},{"type":"language","title":"Praise and thanksgiving: technical meanings","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:53–54<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-018\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E53.7\">In the language (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في اللغة<\/span>), praise (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحمد<\/span>) is commendation of what is beautiful, intended as magnification and reverence. In technical usage (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في العرف<\/span>), it is an act that discloses magnification of a benefactor precisely insofar as he is a benefactor. Thanksgiving (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشكر<\/span>), in the language, is an act that signifies magnification of the benefactor and is therefore synonymous with ḥamd in that technical sense. In technical usage, however, shukr is the servant's directing everything God has bestowed upon him - hearing, sight, and the rest - toward the purpose for which it was created and bestowed. See our larger commentary on al-Fātiḥa for the relations (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النِّسَب<\/span>) among these meanings, which we set out there in verse and prose.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:53–54 In the language ( في اللغة ), praise ( الحمد ) is commendation of what is beautiful, intended as magnification and reverence. In technical usage ( في العرف ), it is an act that discloses magnification of a benefactor precisely insofar as he is a benefactor. Thanksgiving ( الشكر ), in the language, is an act that signifies magnification of the benefactor and is therefore synonymous with ḥamd in that technical sense. In technical usage, however, shukr is the servant's directing everything God has bestowed upon him - hearing, sight, and the rest - toward the purpose for which it was created and bestowed. See our larger commentary on al-Fātiḥa for the relations ( النِّسَب ) among these meanings, which we set out there in verse and prose."},{"type":"language","title":"Allāh: the name and its derivation","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:54<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-019\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E54.0\">“God” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الله<\/span>) is, on this view, an independently coined (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مرتجل<\/span>), underived (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جامد<\/span>) name; the alif and lām are integral to the word and are not the definite article. Al-Wāḥidī said: “It is a name with which the Creator - glory be to Him - is uniquely named; in describing Him it functions as a proper name, and no derivation is known for it.” Al-Uqlīshī said: “If this name is not derived, it signifies the very Essence without considering in it any particular attribute. It is not a name derived from an attribute, like the Knowing, the Real, the Creator, or the Provider.” On this account the alif and lām in Allāh belong to the word itself, just as the zāʾ belongs to Zayd. A number of scholars held this view; al-Ghazālī preferred it and said that everything proposed by way of derivation is strained.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-020\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E54.1\">It has also been said that Allāh is derived from taʾalluh (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التألّه<\/span>), worship or devotion; or from walahān (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولهان<\/span>), bewilderment, because intellects are bewildered concerning Him. Another view is that its original form was al-ilāh: the hamza was omitted, its vowel transferred to the lām, assimilation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإدغام<\/span>) then occurred, and the lām was pronounced emphatically in magnification, except when preceded by kasra.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:54 “God” ( الله ) is, on this view, an independently coined ( مرتجل ), underived ( جامد ) name; the alif and lām are integral to the word and are not the definite article. Al-Wāḥidī said: “It is a name with which the Creator - glory be to Him - is uniquely named; in describing Him it functions as a proper name, and no derivation is known for it.” Al-Uqlīshī said: “If this name is not derived, it signifies the very Essence without considering in it any particular attribute. It is not a name derived from an attribute, like the Knowing, the Real, the Creator, or the Provider.” On this account the alif and lām in Allāh belong to the word itself, just as the zāʾ belongs to Zayd. A number of scholars held this view; al-Ghazālī preferred it and said that everything proposed by way of derivation is strained. It has also been said that Allāh is derived from taʾalluh ( التألّه ), worship or devotion; or from walahān ( الولهان ), bewilderment, because intellects are bewildered concerning Him. Another view is that its original form was al-ilāh: the hamza was omitted, its vowel transferred to the lām, assimilation ( الإدغام ) then occurred, and the lām was pronounced emphatically in magnification, except when preceded by kasra."},{"type":"language","title":"Rabb: nurture, lordship and meaning","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-021\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E54.2\">“Lord” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ربّ<\/span>, rabb) is an adjective qualifying (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعت<\/span>) “God.” In origin it is a verbal noun (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر<\/span>) meaning nurture or bringing-up (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التربية<\/span>): bringing a thing to its perfection little by little. It is then used descriptively for intensification, as are ṣawm (“fasting”) and ʿadl (“justice”). It is also said to be a descriptive form from rabbahu yarubbuhu, “he tended it / nurtured it”; its original form is rabab, after which assimilation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإدغام<\/span>) occurs. An owner is called rabb because he preserves what he owns and nurtures it. The term is not applied to anyone other than God without qualification, as in “Return to your lord” [12:50]. Ibn Juzayy said: “It has four meanings: deity, master, owner, and one who sets things right. All four are suitable in ‘Lord of the worlds,’ though the strongest meaning is ‘deity,’ because that is peculiar to God Most High.”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"“Lord” ( ربّ , rabb) is an adjective qualifying ( نعت ) “God.” In origin it is a verbal noun ( مصدر ) meaning nurture or bringing-up ( التربية ): bringing a thing to its perfection little by little. It is then used descriptively for intensification, as are ṣawm (“fasting”) and ʿadl (“justice”). It is also said to be a descriptive form from rabbahu yarubbuhu, “he tended it / nurtured it”; its original form is rabab, after which assimilation ( الإدغام ) occurs. An owner is called rabb because he preserves what he owns and nurtures it. The term is not applied to anyone other than God without qualification, as in “Return to your lord” [12:50]. Ibn Juzayy said: “It has four meanings: deity, master, owner, and one who sets things right. All four are suitable in ‘Lord of the worlds,’ though the strongest meaning is ‘deity,’ because that is peculiar to God Most High.”"},{"type":"language","title":"ʿĀlam: the cosmos as a sign of its Maker","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-022\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E54.3\">“The worlds” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العالمين<\/span>) is the plural of ʿālam (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالَم<\/span>). Ibn ʿAjība explains the word morphologically: al-ʿālam is “a noun for that by which something is known” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسم لما يُعلَم به<\/span>), just as khātam is that by which something is sealed and ṭābiʿ that by which something is stamped. The term came to be used predominantly for that by which the Maker is known (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ما يُعلَم به الصانع<\/span>): everything other than Him, comprising substances (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجواهر<\/span>) and accidents (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأعراض<\/span>). Because they are contingent or possible (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لإمكانها<\/span>) and stand in need of an efficient cause (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مؤثِّر<\/span>) that is Necessary in Itself (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">واجب لذاته<\/span>), they signify His existence. Thus the cosmos functions as a sign (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علامة<\/span>) of its Maker, although Ibn ʿAjība's wording here is literally “that by which [He] is known.” The plural is used to include the differing genera contained beneath the term. Rational beings are given grammatical precedence, so it is pluralised with yāʾ and nūn like their other descriptions. It is therefore a true plural (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جمع<\/span>), not a collective noun (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسم جمع<\/span>), contrary to Ibn Mālik.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-023\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E54.4\">It is also said that the term was coined for beings possessed of knowledge - the angels and the two weighty species (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الثقلان<\/span>), humankind and jinn - and that it includes other beings only by concomitant extension (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">على سبيل الاستتباع<\/span>). Another view is that humankind is intended here: every human being is a world (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالَم<\/span>), since he contains counterparts of what exists in the greater world (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العالم الكبير<\/span>). Hence revelation places contemplation of the two on the same footing: “And in your own selves - do you not see?” [51:21].<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:55<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-024\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E55.0\">I say: to this the poet alludes:<br/>O you who wander in a wilderness, estranged from your own secret,<br/>look: you will find all existence within yourself.<br/>You are perfection, in the Way and in the Reality,<br/>you who gather within yourself the divine secret in its entirety.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"“The worlds” ( العالمين ) is the plural of ʿālam ( عالَم ). Ibn ʿAjība explains the word morphologically: al-ʿālam is “a noun for that by which something is known” ( اسم لما يُعلَم به ), just as khātam is that by which something is sealed and ṭābiʿ that by which something is stamped. The term came to be used predominantly for that by which the Maker is known ( ما يُعلَم به الصانع ): everything other than Him, comprising substances ( الجواهر ) and accidents ( الأعراض ). Because they are contingent or possible ( لإمكانها ) and stand in need of an efficient cause ( مؤثِّر ) that is Necessary in Itself ( واجب لذاته ), they signify His existence. Thus the cosmos functions as a sign ( علامة ) of its Maker, although Ibn ʿAjība's wording here is literally “that by which [He] is known.” The plural is used to include the differing genera contained beneath the term. Rational beings are given grammatical precedence, so it is pluralised with yāʾ and nūn like their other descriptions. It is therefore a true plural ( جمع ), not a collective noun ( اسم جمع ), contrary to Ibn Mālik. It is also said that the term was coined for beings possessed of knowledge - the angels and the two weighty species ( الثقلان ), humankind and jinn - and that it includes other beings only by concomitant extension ( على سبيل الاستتباع ). Another view is that humankind is intended here: every human being is a world ( عالَم ), since he contains counterparts of what exists in the greater world ( العالم الكبير ). Hence revelation places contemplation of the two on the same footing: “And in your own selves - do you not see?” [51:21]. I:55 I say: to this the poet alludes: O you who wander in a wilderness, estranged from your own secret, look: you will find all existence within yourself. You are perfection, in the Way and in the Reality, you who gather within yourself the divine secret in its entirety."},{"type":"language","title":"Al-Raḥmān and al-Raḥīm: form and meaning","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-025\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E55.1\">Al-Raḥmān (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرَّحمن<\/span>, “the Compassionate”) and al-Raḥīm (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرَّحيم<\/span>, “the Merciful”) are two intensive forms (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسمان بُنيا للمبالغة<\/span>) derived from raḥima, “to have mercy,” as ghaḍbān is formed from ghaḍiba and ʿalīm from ʿalima. In ordinary language, mercy (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرحمة<\/span>) is tenderness of heart and an inclination that entails graciousness and beneficence; from the same root comes raḥim, “womb,” because of its tender enclosing of what is within it. The divine Names, however, are taken with regard to their ends (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغايات<\/span>), which are acts, not their beginnings (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المبادئ<\/span>), which in creatures would be passive affections (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">انفعالات<\/span>).<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-026\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E55.2\">Al-Raḥmān (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرَّحمن<\/span>) is more intensive than al-Raḥīm (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرَّحيم<\/span>), because increase in morphological form indicates increase in meaning (<span class=\"arinline\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">زيادة المبنى تدل على زيادة المعنى<\/span>), as with qaṭṭaʿa and qaṭaʿa. This increase may be understood quantitatively or qualitatively. Quantitatively one says: “Compassionate in this world,” because that mercy encompasses believer and unbeliever, “and Merciful in the hereafter,” because that mercy is particular to believers. Qualitatively one says: “Compassionate in this world and the hereafter, and Merciful in this world,” because all blessings of the hereafter are immense, while worldly blessings include both great and small.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-027\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E55.3\">Al-Raḥmān is placed first, although the normal progression (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الترقي<\/span>) would be from the lesser to the greater, because the mercy of this world comes first and because al-Raḥmān has become like a proper name (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كالعَلَم<\/span>), inasmuch as none other is described by it. Its meaning is the true Benefactor whose mercy reaches its utmost limit, and this is true of none but God Most High. See al-Bayḍāwī. The two Names will be discussed again in the running interpretation.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Raḥmān ( الرَّحمن , “the Compassionate”) and al-Raḥīm ( الرَّحيم , “the Merciful”) are two intensive forms ( اسمان بُنيا للمبالغة ) derived from raḥima, “to have mercy,” as ghaḍbān is formed from ghaḍiba and ʿalīm from ʿalima. In ordinary language, mercy ( الرحمة ) is tenderness of heart and an inclination that entails graciousness and beneficence; from the same root comes raḥim, “womb,” because of its tender enclosing of what is within it. The divine Names, however, are taken with regard to their ends ( الغايات ), which are acts, not their beginnings ( المبادئ ), which in creatures would be passive affections ( انفعالات ). Al-Raḥmān ( الرَّحمن ) is more intensive than al-Raḥīm ( الرَّحيم ), because increase in morphological form indicates increase in meaning ( زيادة المبنى تدل على زيادة المعنى ), as with qaṭṭaʿa and qaṭaʿa. This increase may be understood quantitatively or qualitatively. Quantitatively one says: “Compassionate in this world,” because that mercy encompasses believer and unbeliever, “and Merciful in the hereafter,” because that mercy is particular to believers. Qualitatively one says: “Compassionate in this world and the hereafter, and Merciful in this world,” because all blessings of the hereafter are immense, while worldly blessings include both great and small. Al-Raḥmān is placed first, although the normal progression ( الترقي ) would be from the lesser to the greater, because the mercy of this world comes first and because al-Raḥmān has become like a proper name ( كالعَلَم ), inasmuch as none other is described by it. Its meaning is the true Benefactor whose mercy reaches its utmost limit, and this is true of none but God Most High. See al-Bayḍāwī. The two Names will be discussed again in the running interpretation."},{"type":"language","title":"Malik and mālik: readings and annexation","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-028\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E55.4\">“King” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَلِك<\/span>, malik) is an adjective (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعت<\/span>) qualifying what precedes. The majority recite it without alif, from mulk (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المُلك<\/span>, sovereignty), with ḍamma; ʿĀṣim and al-Kisāʾī recite mālik (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مالِك<\/span>, master/owner) with alif, from milk (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المِلك<\/span>, possession), with kasra. On the latter reading the ellipsis is understood as “Master of the coming of the Day of Recompense,” or “Master of the affair on the Day of Recompense.” Ibn ʿAjība prefers the majority reading for three reasons: first, a king is greater than an owner, for anyone may be called owner of his property whereas the king is master of the people; second, “His is the sovereignty on the day the Trumpet is blown” [6:73]; and third, the reading malik requires no ellipsis (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حذف<\/span>), whereas ellipsis is contrary to the default (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خلاف الأصل<\/span>).<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-029\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E55.5\">“The Day of Recompense” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يوم الدين<\/span>) is an adverbial expression (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظرف<\/span>) annexed (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مضاف<\/span>) to what precedes by an extended construction (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">على طريق الاتساع<\/span>). The adverbial is treated syntactically like a direct object (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول به<\/span>), while the meaning remains adverbial: “the King on the Day of Recompense.” Or, on the mālik reading, the sense is “Master of the affair on the Day of Recompense,” in which case an ellipsis (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حذف<\/span>) is posited. Both readings - the short form malik and the long form mālik - are transmitted from the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:56<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-030\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E56.0\">Malik has been recited in several other ways which Ibn ʿAjība omits because they are anomalous (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شاذة<\/span>). If one objects: “Malik and mālik are indefinite (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نكرة<\/span>), because an active participle's annexation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إضافة اسم الفاعل<\/span>) does not make it specific; how then can either qualify al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm, which are definite (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة<\/span>)?” The answer is that an active participle's annexation fails to specify only when it denotes present or future time, for then the annexation is non-pure (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إضافة غير محضة<\/span>). Here the meaning is continuously and eternally true, so the annexation is pure (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إضافة محضة<\/span>). This is Ibn Juzayy's explanation.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"“King” ( مَلِك , malik) is an adjective ( نعت ) qualifying what precedes. The majority recite it without alif, from mulk ( المُلك , sovereignty), with ḍamma; ʿĀṣim and al-Kisāʾī recite mālik ( مالِك , master/owner) with alif, from milk ( المِلك , possession), with kasra. On the latter reading the ellipsis is understood as “Master of the coming of the Day of Recompense,” or “Master of the affair on the Day of Recompense.” Ibn ʿAjība prefers the majority reading for three reasons: first, a king is greater than an owner, for anyone may be called owner of his property whereas the king is master of the people; second, “His is the sovereignty on the day the Trumpet is blown” [6:73]; and third, the reading malik requires no ellipsis ( حذف ), whereas ellipsis is contrary to the default ( خلاف الأصل ). “The Day of Recompense” ( يوم الدين ) is an adverbial expression ( ظرف ) annexed ( مضاف ) to what precedes by an extended construction ( على طريق الاتساع ). The adverbial is treated syntactically like a direct object ( مفعول به ), while the meaning remains adverbial: “the King on the Day of Recompense.” Or, on the mālik reading, the sense is “Master of the affair on the Day of Recompense,” in which case an ellipsis ( حذف ) is posited. Both readings - the short form malik and the long form mālik - are transmitted from the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace. I:56 Malik has been recited in several other ways which Ibn ʿAjība omits because they are anomalous ( شاذة ). If one objects: “Malik and mālik are indefinite ( نكرة ), because an active participle's annexation ( إضافة اسم الفاعل ) does not make it specific; how then can either qualify al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm, which are definite ( معرفة )?” The answer is that an active participle's annexation fails to specify only when it denotes present or future time, for then the annexation is non-pure ( إضافة غير محضة ). Here the meaning is continuously and eternally true, so the annexation is pure ( إضافة محضة ). This is Ibn Juzayy's explanation."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Praise and lordship","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-031\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E56.1\">The Real, glorious is His majesty, teaches His servants how to praise and magnify Him and then to ask of Him: “My servants, say: ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.’” That is: beautiful praise is deserved only by the Magnificent, the Majestic. None other deserves praise, since in reality there is no benefactor but God: “Whatever blessing you have is from God” [16:53]. Or: all praises whatsoever belong to God. Or: the praise already known to the mind is God's praise of Himself in His beginningless eternity, before He brought His creation into existence. When He brought His creation into existence, He said to them, “Praise belongs to God”: that is, “Praise Me with that praise already known in beginningless eternity.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-032\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E56.2\">He alone deserves praise because He is “Lord of the worlds.” It is as though someone asked Him, “Why does praise belong exclusively to You?” and He answered: “Because I am Lord of the worlds. I brought them into existence through My mercy and sustain them through My bounty. There is no benefactor but Me, and so I alone deserve praise. From Me comes their being brought into existence, and upon Me rests their continually renewed sustenance. I am the Lord of the servants. All the worlds, with their many genera and differing species, are in My grasp and under My nurture and care.”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, teaches His servants how to praise and magnify Him and then to ask of Him: “My servants, say: ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.’” That is: beautiful praise is deserved only by the Magnificent, the Majestic. None other deserves praise, since in reality there is no benefactor but God: “Whatever blessing you have is from God” [16:53]. Or: all praises whatsoever belong to God. Or: the praise already known to the mind is God's praise of Himself in His beginningless eternity, before He brought His creation into existence. When He brought His creation into existence, He said to them, “Praise belongs to God”: that is, “Praise Me with that praise already known in beginningless eternity.” He alone deserves praise because He is “Lord of the worlds.” It is as though someone asked Him, “Why does praise belong exclusively to You?” and He answered: “Because I am Lord of the worlds. I brought them into existence through My mercy and sustain them through My bounty. There is no benefactor but Me, and so I alone deserve praise. From Me comes their being brought into existence, and upon Me rests their continually renewed sustenance. I am the Lord of the servants. All the worlds, with their many genera and differing species, are in My grasp and under My nurture and care.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Reports on the worlds and the angelic hosts","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-033\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E56.3\">Some have said that God created eighteen thousand worlds, half on land and half in the sea. Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī said: “It has been related that the children of Adam are one tenth of the jinn; the children of Adam and the jinn together are one tenth of the land animals; all these are one tenth of the birds; all these are one tenth of the sea animals; all these are one tenth of the angels of the earth charged with the children of Adam; all these are one tenth of the angels of the lowest heaven; and all these are one tenth of the angels of the second heaven. This progression continues to the angels of the seventh heaven.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-034\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E56.4\">“All of these, in comparison with the Footstool, are very few. These in turn are one tenth of the angels of a single pavilion among the hundred thousand pavilions of the Throne. Were the heavens and the earth, and all within and between them, compared with the length and breadth of one pavilion, they would be a very small and trifling thing. There is not a handspan of space without an angel prostrating, bowing, or standing, with a resounding voice glorifying God and declaring His unity. Then all these, in comparison with those who move around the Throne, are like a drop in the sea. None but God Most High knows their number: ‘None knows the hosts of your Lord but He’ [74:31].” Here ends the quotation.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-035\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E56.5\">Wahb ibn Munabbih said: “The Throne has three hundred and sixty-six supports. Between each pair of supports are sixty thousand deserts; in each desert are sixty thousand worlds; and each world is the size of the two weighty kinds.”<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:56–57<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-036\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E56.6\">All these worlds are in the grasp of the Real, beneath His nurture and preservation. He conveys sustaining influx (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المدد<\/span>) to each in its settled abode and place of deposit: to its spiritual dimension through the strength of sciences and gnoses (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلوم والمعارف<\/span>), and to its bodily dimension through the strength of corporeal forms (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأشباح<\/span>). From the Throne to the earth, their provisions are apportioned, their lifespans delimited, their bodily forms preserved, and their locations known. Nothing on earth or in heaven is hidden from Him; He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Some have said that God created eighteen thousand worlds, half on land and half in the sea. Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī said: “It has been related that the children of Adam are one tenth of the jinn; the children of Adam and the jinn together are one tenth of the land animals; all these are one tenth of the birds; all these are one tenth of the sea animals; all these are one tenth of the angels of the earth charged with the children of Adam; all these are one tenth of the angels of the lowest heaven; and all these are one tenth of the angels of the second heaven. This progression continues to the angels of the seventh heaven. “All of these, in comparison with the Footstool, are very few. These in turn are one tenth of the angels of a single pavilion among the hundred thousand pavilions of the Throne. Were the heavens and the earth, and all within and between them, compared with the length and breadth of one pavilion, they would be a very small and trifling thing. There is not a handspan of space without an angel prostrating, bowing, or standing, with a resounding voice glorifying God and declaring His unity. Then all these, in comparison with those who move around the Throne, are like a drop in the sea. None but God Most High knows their number: ‘None knows the hosts of your Lord but He’ [74:31].” Here ends the quotation. Wahb ibn Munabbih said: “The Throne has three hundred and sixty-six supports. Between each pair of supports are sixty thousand deserts; in each desert are sixty thousand worlds; and each world is the size of the two weighty kinds.” I:56–57 All these worlds are in the grasp of the Real, beneath His nurture and preservation. He conveys sustaining influx ( المدد ) to each in its settled abode and place of deposit: to its spiritual dimension through the strength of sciences and gnoses ( العلوم والمعارف ), and to its bodily dimension through the strength of corporeal forms ( الأشباح ). From the Throne to the earth, their provisions are apportioned, their lifespans delimited, their bodily forms preserved, and their locations known. Nothing on earth or in heaven is hidden from Him; He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Mercy, origination and sustaining influx","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:57<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-037\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E57.0\">This nurture (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التربية<\/span>) by which He nurtures His creation is mercy and beneficence from Him, not something binding or obligatory upon Him. Hence He joins to “Lord of the worlds” the words al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm: the Compassionate through the blessing of bringing-into-existence (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعمة الإيجاد<\/span>), the Merciful through the blessing of sustaining influx (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعمة الإمداد<\/span>). As the Ḥikam says: “Two blessings no existent is without, and every originated being must have them: the blessing of being brought into existence and the blessing of sustaining influx. He first favoured you with existence, then followed it with successive sustaining influx.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-038\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E57.1\">His Name al-Raḥmān entails the bringing of things into existence and their manifestation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإيجاد والإبراز<\/span>); His Name al-Raḥīm entails their nurture and sustaining influx (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التربية والإمداد<\/span>). Hence the Name al-Raḥmān may not be applied to anyone else, and no one has been named by it, because bringing into existence is not truly possible from other than Him. Al-Raḥīm differs: it may be applied to another, since the issuing of sustaining assistance from some creatures appears, metaphorically and by borrowed attribution, to be shared.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-039\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E57.2\">Or: He is al-Raḥmān in this world and the hereafter, and al-Raḥīm in the hereafter, because the mercy of the hereafter is particular to believers. Or: al-Raḥmān with respect to the great blessings (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جلائل النعم<\/span>) and al-Raḥīm with respect to their subtleties (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دقائقها<\/span>). The great blessings include Islam, faith (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإيمان<\/span>), spiritual excellence (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإحسان<\/span>), gnosis (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/span>), guidance, unveiling the veil, opening the door, and entrance among the beloved; subtler blessings include health, well-being, lawful wealth, and the other things to be mentioned under “those upon whom You have bestowed favour.”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:57 This nurture ( التربية ) by which He nurtures His creation is mercy and beneficence from Him, not something binding or obligatory upon Him. Hence He joins to “Lord of the worlds” the words al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm: the Compassionate through the blessing of bringing-into-existence ( نعمة الإيجاد ), the Merciful through the blessing of sustaining influx ( نعمة الإمداد ). As the Ḥikam says: “Two blessings no existent is without, and every originated being must have them: the blessing of being brought into existence and the blessing of sustaining influx. He first favoured you with existence, then followed it with successive sustaining influx.” His Name al-Raḥmān entails the bringing of things into existence and their manifestation ( الإيجاد والإبراز ); His Name al-Raḥīm entails their nurture and sustaining influx ( التربية والإمداد ). Hence the Name al-Raḥmān may not be applied to anyone else, and no one has been named by it, because bringing into existence is not truly possible from other than Him. Al-Raḥīm differs: it may be applied to another, since the issuing of sustaining assistance from some creatures appears, metaphorically and by borrowed attribution, to be shared. Or: He is al-Raḥmān in this world and the hereafter, and al-Raḥīm in the hereafter, because the mercy of the hereafter is particular to believers. Or: al-Raḥmān with respect to the great blessings ( جلائل النعم ) and al-Raḥīm with respect to their subtleties ( دقائقها ). The great blessings include Islam, faith ( الإيمان ), spiritual excellence ( الإحسان ), gnosis ( المعرفة ), guidance, unveiling the veil, opening the door, and entrance among the beloved; subtler blessings include health, well-being, lawful wealth, and the other things to be mentioned under “those upon whom You have bestowed favour.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Sovereignty on the Day of Recompense","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-040\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E57.3\">He from whom bringing into existence and sustaining truly proceed deserves to be King over all the servants. This is therefore mentioned immediately afterwards: “King of the Day of Recompense.” He disposes of His servants as He wills: none can repel what He decrees, and none can withhold what He gives. He is King of kings and Lord of lords in this abode and the next.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-041\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E57.4\">Sovereignty is associated particularly with the Day of Recompense, the day of requital, because on that day God's sovereignty becomes plainly manifest to all creation. God Most High discloses Himself to judge between His servants, so that believers see Him directly. In this world, by contrast, His disposal of affairs is understood only by the perfected among the believers. Many of the ignorant have therefore claimed sovereignty and attributed it to themselves. On the Day of Resurrection, God's sole sovereignty is evident to both the elect and the common people. God Most High says: “Whose is the sovereignty today? God's, the One, the All-Subduing” [40:16].<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"He from whom bringing into existence and sustaining truly proceed deserves to be King over all the servants. This is therefore mentioned immediately afterwards: “King of the Day of Recompense.” He disposes of His servants as He wills: none can repel what He decrees, and none can withhold what He gives. He is King of kings and Lord of lords in this abode and the next. Sovereignty is associated particularly with the Day of Recompense, the day of requital, because on that day God's sovereignty becomes plainly manifest to all creation. God Most High discloses Himself to judge between His servants, so that believers see Him directly. In this world, by contrast, His disposal of affairs is understood only by the perfected among the believers. Many of the ignorant have therefore claimed sovereignty and attributed it to themselves. On the Day of Resurrection, God's sole sovereignty is evident to both the elect and the common people. God Most High says: “Whose is the sovereignty today? God's, the One, the All-Subduing” [40:16]."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Divine self-disclosure and self-praise","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-043\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E57.5\">When the Real, glory be to Him, disclosed Himself (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تجلّى<\/span>) from the realm of jabarūt (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الجبروت<\/span>) into the realm of malakūt (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الملكوت<\/span>) - or, one may say, from the realm of the unseen into the realm of witnessing - He praised Himself by Himself, glorified Himself by Himself, and declared His own unity by Himself. How fine are al-Harawī's words:<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:57–58<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-044\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E57.6\">No one has declared the One to be One,<br/>for everyone who declares Him One is a denier.<br/>The tawḥīd of one who speaks from his own description<br/>is a borrowed thing annulled by the One.<br/>His declaring Himself One is His tawḥīd;<br/>the description of whoever describes Him deviates.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:58<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-045\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E58.0\">Thus, affirming His own unity by Himself, and expressing Himself by Himself, He said: “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.” It is as though He says in the heading of His Book and the secret of His address: “I am the Praiser and the Praised. I sustain every existent. I am Lord of lords and, for whoever understands the address, the Cause of causes. I am Lord of the worlds, Sustainer of the heavens and the earths. Indeed, I am alone in My existence, disclosing Myself to My servants through My generosity and bounty. All the worlds are established through My establishing them, and effaced through the absolute oneness of My Essence.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-046\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E58.1\">A man said in al-Junayd's presence, “Praise belongs to God,” without adding “Lord of the worlds.” Al-Junayd said to him, “Complete it, my brother.” The man replied, “What standing do the worlds have, that they should be mentioned with Him?” Al-Junayd said: “Say it, my brother. When that which has come to be is placed alongside the Beginningless, that which has come to be vanishes, and the Beginningless remains.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-047\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E58.2\">He says, glory be to Him [in this spiritual interpretation]: “O you who are near to Me, ponder My secret, for it is wondrous. I am the Lover and I am the Beloved; I am the Near and I am the Answerer. I am the Ever-Merciful, the All-Merciful; I am the King, the Requiter. I am All-Merciful through the blessing of bringing into existence, and Ever-Merciful through the succession of sustaining gifts. From Me comes the bringing into existence, and upon Me rests continual sustaining. I am Lord of the servants. I am the King, the Requiter, rewarding goodness with goodness. I am King without qualification — were it not for the ignorance of the people of obstinacy and dissension! For those of humankind who understand Us, command belongs to Us perpetually.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-048\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E58.3\">He says in the Greater Epistles: “What counts is not the outward appearances of things, but the hidden secret. This consists in the manifestation of the affair of the Real, the lifting of its covering, and the removal of its veils and concealment. When that self-disclosure and manifestation are realised, when effacement and passing away overcome things, and darkness is dispersed by the shining of the light, there the eye of certainty appears and the manifest Truth stands established. Then the claims of the claimants are nullified, just as ordinary people understand their nullity on the Day of Recompense, when sovereignty belongs to God, Lord of the worlds. Would that I knew at what time sovereignty belonged to another, so that it should be qualified by His saying, ‘Sovereignty on that day belongs to God’ [22:56], or ‘Command on that day belongs to God’ [82:19]! Were it not for the vast claims of diseased hearts!” Here ends the quotation.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:59<\/span><div class=\"transition\" id=\"pair-049\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E59.0\">He then descended to the exposition of servanthood (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العبودية<\/span>), saying:<\/p><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"When the Real, glory be to Him, disclosed Himself ( تجلّى ) from the realm of jabarūt ( عالم الجبروت ) into the realm of malakūt ( عالم الملكوت ) - or, one may say, from the realm of the unseen into the realm of witnessing - He praised Himself by Himself, glorified Himself by Himself, and declared His own unity by Himself. How fine are al-Harawī's words: I:57–58 No one has declared the One to be One, for everyone who declares Him One is a denier. The tawḥīd of one who speaks from his own description is a borrowed thing annulled by the One. His declaring Himself One is His tawḥīd; the description of whoever describes Him deviates. I:58 Thus, affirming His own unity by Himself, and expressing Himself by Himself, He said: “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds.” It is as though He says in the heading of His Book and the secret of His address: “I am the Praiser and the Praised. I sustain every existent. I am Lord of lords and, for whoever understands the address, the Cause of causes. I am Lord of the worlds, Sustainer of the heavens and the earths. Indeed, I am alone in My existence, disclosing Myself to My servants through My generosity and bounty. All the worlds are established through My establishing them, and effaced through the absolute oneness of My Essence.” A man said in al-Junayd's presence, “Praise belongs to God,” without adding “Lord of the worlds.” Al-Junayd said to him, “Complete it, my brother.” The man replied, “What standing do the worlds have, that they should be mentioned with Him?” Al-Junayd said: “Say it, my brother. When that which has come to be is placed alongside the Beginningless, that which has come to be vanishes, and the Beginningless remains.” He says, glory be to Him [in this spiritual interpretation]: “O you who are near to Me, ponder My secret, for it is wondrous. I am the Lover and I am the Beloved; I am the Near and I am the Answerer. I am the Ever-Merciful, the All-Merciful; I am the King, the Requiter. I am All-Merciful through the blessing of bringing into existence, and Ever-Merciful through the succession of sustaining gifts. From Me comes the bringing into existence, and upon Me rests continual sustaining. I am Lord of the servants. I am the King, the Requiter, rewarding goodness with goodness. I am King without qualification — were it not for the ignorance of the people of obstinacy and dissension! For those of humankind who understand Us, command belongs to Us perpetually.” He says in the Greater Epistles: “What counts is not the outward appearances of things, but the hidden secret. This consists in the manifestation of the affair of the Real, the lifting of its covering, and the removal of its veils and concealment. When that self-disclosure and manifestation are realised, when effacement and passing away overcome things, and darkness is dispersed by the shining of the light, there the eye of certainty appears and the manifest Truth stands established. Then the claims of the claimants are nullified, just as ordinary people understand their nullity on the Day of Recompense, when sovereignty belongs to God, Lord of the worlds. Would that I knew at what time sovereignty belonged to another, so that it should be qualified by His saying, ‘Sovereignty on that day belongs to God’ [22:56], or ‘Command on that day belongs to God’ [82:19]! Were it not for the vast claims of diseased hearts!” Here ends the quotation. I:59 He then descended to the exposition of servanthood ( العبودية ), saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C53.1","page":53,"ar":"في الكسر- وهي قراءة شاذة.","en":"With a kasra [the i vowel]; this is a noncanonical reading.","anchor":"بإتباع الدال للام"},{"id":"C53.2","page":53,"ar":"أي: إتباع اللام الدال في الضم، وهي قراءة شاذة أيضا.","en":"That is, making the lām follow the dāl in taking a ḍamma [the u vowel]; this too is a noncanonical reading.","anchor":"وبالعكس"},{"id":"C55.1","page":55,"ar":"ينبغي ألا يكون ترجيح في هذا المجال، مع ورود القراءتين عن الرسول صلى الله عليه وسلم والقراءتان- كما يقول الألوسي-: فرسا رهان، ومتى أردت الترجيح تعارضت الأدلة.","en":"There should be no preference for one reading in this matter, given that both readings are transmitted from the Messenger, God bless him and grant him peace. The two readings—as al-Ālūsī says—are two evenly matched racehorses; whenever you seek to prefer one, the proofs oppose one another.","anchor":"والثالث: أنها لا تقتضي حذفا، والحذف خلاف الأصل"},{"id":"C57.1","page":57,"ar":"لابن عطاء الله السكندري.","en":"By Ibn ʿAṭāʾ Allāh al-Iskandarī.","anchor":"كما في (الحكم)"},{"id":"C58.1","page":58,"ar":"مضمون الأبيات كما يقول الشيخ ابن عجيبة في إيقاظ الهمم: أن الحق تولى توحيد نفسه بنفسه. فكل من ادعى أنه وحده بنفسه فهو جاحد لوحدانيته، حيث أشرك معه نفسه، وكل من ينعته بنفسه فهو لاحد- أي: مائل عن الصواب. وهذا المعنى من المعاني التي ينبغي أن تفهم في ضوئها هذه الأبيات. وللأبيات محامل أخرى ذكرها العلامة ابن القيم. فلننظر في كتابه مدارج السالكين. وانظر أيضا: مدارج السلوك لأبي بكر بناني.","en":"The purport of the verses, as Shaykh Ibn ʿAjība says in Īqāẓ al-himam, is that the Real Himself undertook to affirm His own oneness. Thus anyone who claims that he has affirmed His oneness by himself denies His oneness, because he has associated himself with Him; and anyone who describes Him by himself is lāḥid—that is, one who deviates from what is right. This is one of the meanings in whose light these verses should be understood. The verses admit other interpretations mentioned by the learned Ibn al-Qayyim; let us consult his Madārij al-sālikīn. See also Madārij al-sulūk by Abū Bakr Bannānī.","anchor":"ونعت من ينعته لاحد"},{"id":"C58.2","page":58,"ar":"لابن عباد النفزي، شارح الحكم.","en":"By Ibn ʿAbbād al-Nafzī, the commentator on the Ḥikam.","anchor":"الرسائل الكبرى"}],"english_characters":19974,"arabic_characters":10590},{"id":"fatiha_3","q":"1:5","s":1,"surah":"Al-Fātiḥa","title":"Al-Fātiḥa 1:5 — commentary","source":"Cairo I:52–70","cairo":"I:59–61","source_page_first":59,"source_page_last":61,"ar":"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ (5)\nقلت: (إياك) مفعول (نعبد) ، وقُدِّم للتعظيم والاهتمام به، والدلالة على الحصر، ولذلك قال ابن عباس:\n(نعبُدك ولا نعبد معك غيرَك) ، ولتقديم ما هو مقدَّمٌ في الوجود وهو الملك المعبود، وللتنبيه على أن العابدَ ينبغي أن يكون نظرُه إلى المعبود أولاً وبالذات، ومنه إلى العبادة، لا من حيث إنها عبادةٌ صدَرتْ عنه، بل من حيث إنها نِسْبَةٌ شريفة إليه، وَوُصْلَةٌ بينه وبين الحق، فإن العارف إنما يحقّ وصوله إذا استغرق في ملاحظة جناب القدس، وغاب عما عداه، حتى إنه لا يلاحظ نفسَه ولا حالاً من أحوالها إلا من حيث إنها تَجَلٍّ من تجلياته ومظهرٌ لربوبيته، ولذلك فُضِّلَ ما حكى اللَّهُ عن حبيبه حين قال: لاَ تَحْزَنْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ مَعَنا، على ما حكاه عن كليمه حيث قال: إِنَّ مَعِي رَبِّي سَيَهْدِينِ أي: حيث صرّح بمطلوبه، و (إياك) مفعول (نستعين) وقدّم أيضاً للاختصاص والاهتمام، كما تقدم في إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ. وكرّر الضمير ولم يقل: إياك نعبد ونستعين لأن إظهارَه أبلغ في إظهار الاعتماد على الله، وأقطعُ في إحضار التعلق بالله والإقبال على الله وأمدحُ، ألا ترى أن قولك: بك أنتصر وبك أحتمي وبك أنال مطالبي- أبلغ وأمدح من قولك: بك أنتصر وأحتمي ... الخ؟.\nوَقَدَّمَ العبادة على الاستعانة ليتوافق رءوس الآي، وليُعلمَ منه أن تقديم الوسيلة على طلب الحاجة أَدْعَى إلى الإجابة، فإن مَنْ تَلبَّس بخدمة الملك وشرع فيها بحسب وُسْعَه، ثم طلب منه الإعانة عليها أجيب إلى مطلبه، بخلاف من كلّفه الملكُ بخدمته، فقال: أعطني ما يعينُني عليها، فهو سوء أدب، وأيضاً: من استحضر الأوصافَ العِظام ما أمكنه إلا المسارعةُ إلى الخضوع والعبادة، وأيضاً: لمّا نسبَ المتكلمُ العبادةَ إلى نفسه أوْهَمَ ذلك تبجحاً واعتداداً منه بما يصدُر عنه فعقَّبه بقوله: وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ، دفعاً لذلك التوهم.\nوالعبادة: أقصى غاية الخضوع والتذلل، ومنه طريق مُعَبَّدٌ، أي: مُذَلل، والاستعانة: طلب المعونة، والمراد طلب المعونة في المُهمات كُلِّها، أو في أداء العبادات.\nوالضمير المستتر فى الفعلين للقارىء ومن معه من الحفظة وحاضري صلاة الجماعة، أو له ولسائر الموجودين.\nأدْرَجَ عبادته في تضاعيف عبادتهم وخلط حاجته بحاجتهم لعلها تُقبل ببركتها ويُجاب إليها، ولهذا شرعت الجماعة. قاله البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله، تتميماً لتعليم عباده: فإذا أثنيتمُ عليَّ ومجدتموني وعظمتموني فأقِرُّوا لي بالربوبية، وأظهروا من أنفسكم العبودية، واطلبوا مني العون في كل وقت وقولوا: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ، وكأنه- جلّ جلاله- لَمّا ذكر أنه مستحق للمحامد كلها قديمها وحديثها لأنه رب العوالِم وقيومها، أصل الأصول وفروعها، أنعم عليها أولاً بالإيجاد، وثانياً بتوالي الإمداد، فهو مالكها على الإطلاق، ذكر أنه لا يستحق أن يُعبد سواه إذ لا مُنعمَ على الحقيقة إلا الله، فهو أحقُّ أن يُعبد، وأولى أن يفرد بالوجهة والقصد، لأنه مُسْتَبِدٌ وغير مُسْتَمدّ، والمادة من عَيْنِ الجود، فإذا انقطعت المادة انعدم الوجود.\nقال البيضاوي: ثم إنه لما ذكر الحقيق بالحمد، ووصف بصفات عظام تميَّز بها عن سائر الذوات، تعلَّق العلمُ بمعلوم معين، خوطب بذلك، أي: يا من هذا شأنه نخصُّك بالعبادة والاستعانة، ليكون أدلّ على الاختصاص، وللترقي من الغَيْبة إلى الشهود، وكأن المعلومَ صار عياناً، والمعقولَ مُشاهَداً، والغيبة حضوراً. بَنَى أول الكلام على ما هو مبادئ حالِ العارفِ من الذكر والفكر والتأمل في أسمائه، والنظر في آلائه، والاستدلال بصنائعه على عظيم شأنه وباهر سلطانه، ثم قفَّى بما هو منتهى أمرِه، وهو أن يخوض لُجَّةَ الوصول، ويصير من أهل المشاهدة، فيراه عياناً ويناجيه شِفاها. اللهم اجعلنا من الواصلين إلى العين دون التابعين للأثر. ومن عادة العرب التفنن في الكلام والعدول عن أسلوبٍ إلى آخر، تَطْريَةً وتنشيطاً للسامع، فَتَعْدِل من الخطاب إلى الغيبة، ومن الغيبة إلى التكلم، كقوله: حَتَّى إِذا كُنْتُمْ فِي الْفُلْكِ وَجَرَيْنَ بِهِمْ بِرِيحٍ ... ولم يقل (بكم) وقوله أَرْسَلَ الرِّياحَ فَتُثِيرُ سَحاباً فَسُقْناهُ إِلى بَلَدٍ ... أي: ولم يقل: فساقه.. انظر تمام كلامه.\nوالالتفات هنا في قوله: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ ولم يقل: إياه نعبد لأن الظاهر من قبل الغيبة، وحسنه أن الموصوف تعيَّن وصار حاضراً.\nقال الأقليشي: فهذه الآية هي التي قال فيها النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم: «فإذا قال العبد: إياك نعبد وإياك نستعين، يقول الله تعالى: هذه بيني وبين عَبْدِي ولعَبْدِيَ مَا سَأَلَ» . معناه: أيُّ عبد توجَّه إليَّ بالعبادة وسألني العون عليها فعبادته متقبلة، والعون مني له عليها حاصل حتى يُوقعها على وجهها، فالعبادة وصف العبد، والعون من الله تعالى للعبد، فلهذا قال: «فهذه بيني وبين عبدي» .\nقال ابن جُزَي: أي نطلب العون منك على العبادة وعلى جميع أمورنا، وفي هذا دليل على بطلان قول القدَرية والجبرية، وأنَّ الحق بين ذلك.\nالإشارة: لمّا تجلى الحق جل جلاله من عالم الجبروت إلى عالم الملكوت، وحَمِدَ نفسه بنفسه، تجلّى أيضاً وتنزَّل من عالم الملكوت إلى عالم المُلك بقدرته وحكمته لإظهار آثار أسمائه وصفاته، فأظهر العبودية وأخفى الربوبية، أظهر الحكمة وأبطن القدرة، فجعلَ عالَم الحكمة يخاطبُ عالمَ القدرة، ويخضع له، ويتعبّد ويستمد، منه الإعانة والهداية، ويتحرز من طريق الضلالة والغواية.\nفعالَمً الحكمة محلُّ التكليف، وعالم القدرة محل التصريف، عالم الحكمة عالم الأشباح، وعالم القدرة عالم الأرواح، فإياك نعبد لأهل عالم الحكمة، وإياك نستعين لأهل عالم القدرة. ولذلك قال الشيخ أبو العباس المرسي رضى الله عنه: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ شريعة، وإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ حقيقة، إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ إسلاما، وإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ إحساناً، إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ عبادة، وإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ عبودية، إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ فرق إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ جَمْعٌ. هـ.\nوإن شئت قلت: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ لأهل العمل لله وهم المخلصون، وإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ لأهل العمل بالله وهم الموحِّدون، العمل لله يوجب المثوبة، والعمل بالله يوجب القُرْبَة، العمل لله يوجب تحقيق العبادة، والعمل بالله يوجب تصحيح الإرادة، العمل لله نعتُ كُلِّ عابد، والعمل بالله نعت كل قاصد، العمل لله قيامٌ بأحكام الظواهر، والعمل بالله قيام بإصلاح الضمائر. قاله القشيري.\nثم إنَّ الناسَ في شهود القدرة والحكمة على ثلاثة أقسام: قسم حُجبوا بالحكمة عن شهود القدرة، وهم أهل الحجاب من أهل الغفلة، وقفوا مع قوله: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ، وقسم حُجبوا بشهود القدرة عن الحكمة، وهم أهل الفناء، وقفوا مع قوله: إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ، وقسم لم يحجبوا بالحكمة عن القدرة ولا بالقدرة عن الحكمة، أَعْطَوا كُلَّ ذي حق حقَّه وَوَفَّوْا كل ذي قسط قسطه، وهم أهل الكمال من أهل البقاء، جمعوا بين قوله: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ، وبالله التوفيق.\nثم بيّن المقصود الأعظم وما هو المطلوب الأهم، وهو طلب الهداية والتوفيق إلى عين التحقيق، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ (5)","quran_en":"“You alone we worship, and from You alone we seek help.” [1:5]","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The fronting of iyyāka and the worshipper’s orientation","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-051\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E59.2\">I say: “You alone” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إياك<\/span>, iyyāka) is the direct object (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول<\/span>) of “we worship” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعبد<\/span>). It is fronted (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قُدِّم<\/span>) for magnification and special attention, and to indicate restriction or exclusivity (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحصر<\/span>); hence Ibn ʿAbbās said: “We worship You and worship none other with You.” Its fronting also places first what is first in existence: the Sovereign who is worshipped (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الملك المعبود<\/span>). It alerts the worshipper that his regard should be directed first and essentially to the One worshipped (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعبود<\/span>), and only from Him to the worship - not considering it merely as an act that proceeds from himself, but as a noble relation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نسبة شريفة<\/span>) to Him and a bond (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وصلة<\/span>) between himself and the Real. The gnostic's arrival (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وصول العارف<\/span>) becomes true only when he is absorbed in attending to the Holy Presence and absent from everything besides It, so that he observes neither himself nor any state of himself except insofar as it is a self-disclosure (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تجلٍّ<\/span>) from His self-disclosures and a locus manifesting His Lordship (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مظهر لربوبيته<\/span>). For this reason what God relates of His Beloved - “Do not grieve; God is with us” [9:40] - is superior to what He relates of His interlocutor Moses - “Indeed, my Lord is with me; He will guide me” [26:62] - insofar as the latter explicitly states the object sought.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-052\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E59.3\">Likewise, “You alone” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إياك<\/span>) is the direct object (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول<\/span>) of “we seek help” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نستعين<\/span>), and it too is fronted for exclusive specification (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاختصاص<\/span>) and attention. The pronoun is repeated instead of saying, “You alone we worship and seek help,” because making it explicit a second time is stronger in manifesting reliance upon God, more decisive in bringing attachment and turning to God into presence, and more perfect in praise. Thus “Through You I prevail, through You I seek protection, and through You I attain my aims” is more forceful and laudatory than “Through You I prevail and seek protection,” and so forth.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “You alone” ( إياك , iyyāka) is the direct object ( مفعول ) of “we worship” ( نعبد ). It is fronted ( قُدِّم ) for magnification and special attention, and to indicate restriction or exclusivity ( الحصر ); hence Ibn ʿAbbās said: “We worship You and worship none other with You.” Its fronting also places first what is first in existence: the Sovereign who is worshipped ( الملك المعبود ). It alerts the worshipper that his regard should be directed first and essentially to the One worshipped ( المعبود ), and only from Him to the worship - not considering it merely as an act that proceeds from himself, but as a noble relation ( نسبة شريفة ) to Him and a bond ( وصلة ) between himself and the Real. The gnostic's arrival ( وصول العارف ) becomes true only when he is absorbed in attending to the Holy Presence and absent from everything besides It, so that he observes neither himself nor any state of himself except insofar as it is a self-disclosure ( تجلٍّ ) from His self-disclosures and a locus manifesting His Lordship ( مظهر لربوبيته ). For this reason what God relates of His Beloved - “Do not grieve; God is with us” [9:40] - is superior to what He relates of His interlocutor Moses - “Indeed, my Lord is with me; He will guide me” [26:62] - insofar as the latter explicitly states the object sought. Likewise, “You alone” ( إياك ) is the direct object ( مفعول ) of “we seek help” ( نستعين ), and it too is fronted for exclusive specification ( الاختصاص ) and attention. The pronoun is repeated instead of saying, “You alone we worship and seek help,” because making it explicit a second time is stronger in manifesting reliance upon God, more decisive in bringing attachment and turning to God into presence, and more perfect in praise. Thus “Through You I prevail, through You I seek protection, and through You I attain my aims” is more forceful and laudatory than “Through You I prevail and seek protection,” and so forth."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Why worship precedes asking for help","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-053\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E59.4\">Worship is placed before seeking help so that the endings of the verses correspond, and to make it known that putting forward the means of approach before asking for one’s need is more conducive to receiving an answer. One who has entered the king’s service and begun to perform it to the extent of his capacity, then asks the king’s assistance in it, receives what he asks. This differs from one whom the king charges with his service and who says, “Give me what will help me perform it”: that is discourtesy. Moreover, one who brings those mighty attributes to mind cannot but hasten to submission and worship. Again, when the speaker ascribes worship to himself, this might suggest boasting and self-congratulation over what proceeds from him. He therefore follows it with “and You alone we ask for help,” to dispel that suggestion.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Worship is placed before seeking help so that the endings of the verses correspond, and to make it known that putting forward the means of approach before asking for one’s need is more conducive to receiving an answer. One who has entered the king’s service and begun to perform it to the extent of his capacity, then asks the king’s assistance in it, receives what he asks. This differs from one whom the king charges with his service and who says, “Give me what will help me perform it”: that is discourtesy. Moreover, one who brings those mighty attributes to mind cannot but hasten to submission and worship. Again, when the speaker ascribes worship to himself, this might suggest boasting and self-congratulation over what proceeds from him. He therefore follows it with “and You alone we ask for help,” to dispel that suggestion."},{"type":"language","title":"Worship, assistance and the plural “we”","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-054\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E59.5\">Worship (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العبادة<\/span>, ʿibāda) is the utmost limit of submission and self-abasement; hence ṭarīq muʿabbad means “a road made low and submissive [by being trodden].” Seeking help (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستعانة<\/span>, istiʿāna) is requesting assistance. What is intended is assistance in all important affairs, or specifically assistance in performing acts of worship.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-055\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E59.6\">The pronoun implicit in the two verbs refers to the reciter together with the guardian angels accompanying him and those present at the congregational prayer; or to him together with all other existing beings. He incorporates his worship into the midst of theirs, and mingles his need with theirs, in the hope that his worship will be accepted through its blessing and his need answered. It is for this reason that congregational prayer was prescribed. So said al-Bayḍāwī. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Source comparison:<\/strong> Cairo I:59 reads <bdi dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لسائر الموجودين<\/bdi>, “all other existing beings.” The inspected electronic text of al-Bayḍāwī, <i>Anwār al-tanzīl<\/i>, I:29, on Q1:5, instead has <bdi dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لسائر الموحدين<\/bdi>, “all other affirmers of God’s unity.” The difference changes the proposed scope of the plural “we”: Cairo gives a wider company than this source witness. The translation follows Cairo; establishing whether the difference arose in Ibn ʿAjība’s adaptation or in transmission requires further textual evidence.<\/span><\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Worship ( العبادة , ʿibāda) is the utmost limit of submission and self-abasement; hence ṭarīq muʿabbad means “a road made low and submissive [by being trodden].” Seeking help ( الاستعانة , istiʿāna) is requesting assistance. What is intended is assistance in all important affairs, or specifically assistance in performing acts of worship. The pronoun implicit in the two verbs refers to the reciter together with the guardian angels accompanying him and those present at the congregational prayer; or to him together with all other existing beings. He incorporates his worship into the midst of theirs, and mingles his need with theirs, in the hope that his worship will be accepted through its blessing and his need answered. It is for this reason that congregational prayer was prescribed. So said al-Bayḍāwī. Source comparison: Cairo I:59 reads لسائر الموجودين , “all other existing beings.” The inspected electronic text of al-Bayḍāwī, Anwār al-tanzīl , I:29, on Q1:5, instead has لسائر الموحدين , “all other affirmers of God’s unity.” The difference changes the proposed scope of the plural “we”: Cairo gives a wider company than this source witness. The translation follows Cairo; establishing whether the difference arose in Ibn ʿAjība’s adaptation or in transmission requires further textual evidence."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Worship and dependence upon God","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:60<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-056\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E60.0\">The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, completing His instruction of His servants: “When you have praised Me, glorified Me, and magnified Me, acknowledge My lordship, manifest servanthood in yourselves, and ask My assistance at every moment. Say: ‘You alone we worship, and You alone we ask for help.’” It is as though, when He mentioned that He deserves all praise, from eternity and in time, because He is the Lord and Sustainer of the worlds, the origin of all roots and their branches, who first bestowed existence upon them and then a continuous supply of support, and who therefore possesses them absolutely, He went on to declare that none besides Him deserves worship. For there is in reality no benefactor but God. He therefore has the greatest claim to be worshipped, and is most worthy to be made the sole object of orientation and intent. He is wholly independent and draws support from no other. The sustaining supply comes from the very fountain of munificence; were that supply cut off, existence would cease.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:60 The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, completing His instruction of His servants: “When you have praised Me, glorified Me, and magnified Me, acknowledge My lordship, manifest servanthood in yourselves, and ask My assistance at every moment. Say: ‘You alone we worship, and You alone we ask for help.’” It is as though, when He mentioned that He deserves all praise, from eternity and in time, because He is the Lord and Sustainer of the worlds, the origin of all roots and their branches, who first bestowed existence upon them and then a continuous supply of support, and who therefore possesses them absolutely, He went on to declare that none besides Him deserves worship. For there is in reality no benefactor but God. He therefore has the greatest claim to be worshipped, and is most worthy to be made the sole object of orientation and intent. He is wholly independent and draws support from no other. The sustaining supply comes from the very fountain of munificence; were that supply cut off, existence would cease."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The shift to direct address and spiritual presence","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-057\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E60.1\">Al-Bayḍāwī said: “When the One truly deserving praise had been mentioned and described with mighty attributes by which He is distinguished from all other essences, knowledge became attached to a determinate object of knowledge. He was therefore addressed directly: ‘O You whose affair is thus, we single You out for worship and for seeking help.’ This more strongly indicates exclusive specification (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاختصاص<\/span>), and it marks an ascent from absence/third-person reference (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغيبة<\/span>) to witnessing (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشهود<\/span>): as though what had been known became directly seen, what had been intellectually apprehended became witnessed, and absence became presence. The opening of the discourse is built upon the beginnings of the gnostic's state: remembrance (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الذكر<\/span>), reflection (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفكر<\/span>), contemplation of His Names, consideration of His favours, and inference from His works to the greatness of His affair and the splendour of His sovereignty. It is then followed by the culmination of his affair: that he plunge into the sea of arrival (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لجة الوصول<\/span>), become one of the people of witnessing (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل المشاهدة<\/span>), see Him directly, and converse intimately with Him face to face. O God, make us among those who arrive at the Reality itself (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العين<\/span>), not those who merely follow the trace (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأثر<\/span>).” <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Source comparison:<\/strong> Cairo I:60 has <bdi dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دون التابعين للأثر<\/bdi>, “rather than those who follow the trace.” The inspected electronic text of al-Bayḍāwī, <i>Anwār al-tanzīl<\/i>, I:29, on Q1:5, reads <bdi dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دون السامعين للأثر<\/bdi>, “rather than those who hear of the trace.” Both contrast arrival with an indirect relation to its evidence, but the source witness specifies hearing where Cairo specifies following. The translation retains Cairo’s wording and records the difference without choosing an unverified authorial reading.<\/span><\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-058\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E60.2\">“It is the Arabs’ custom to vary their speech and pass from one mode of expression to another, refreshing and enlivening the hearer. Thus they move from direct address to the third person, and from the third person to the first, as in His saying, ‘Until, when you are in ships and they sail with them upon a wind …’ [10:22]. He did not say ‘with you.’ And in His saying, ‘He sent the winds, which stir up clouds, and We drove them to a land …’ [35:9]”: that is, He did not say, “and He drove them.” Consult his discussion in full.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-059\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E60.3\">The rhetorical shift (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الالتفات<\/span>, iltifāt) here occurs in “You alone we worship.” He did not say “Him alone we worship,” although the apparent continuation of the preceding discourse would have been in the third person (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغيبة<\/span>). The beauty of the shift lies in the fact that the One described has now become determinate and present.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Bayḍāwī said: “When the One truly deserving praise had been mentioned and described with mighty attributes by which He is distinguished from all other essences, knowledge became attached to a determinate object of knowledge. He was therefore addressed directly: ‘O You whose affair is thus, we single You out for worship and for seeking help.’ This more strongly indicates exclusive specification ( الاختصاص ), and it marks an ascent from absence/third-person reference ( الغيبة ) to witnessing ( الشهود ): as though what had been known became directly seen, what had been intellectually apprehended became witnessed, and absence became presence. The opening of the discourse is built upon the beginnings of the gnostic's state: remembrance ( الذكر ), reflection ( الفكر ), contemplation of His Names, consideration of His favours, and inference from His works to the greatness of His affair and the splendour of His sovereignty. It is then followed by the culmination of his affair: that he plunge into the sea of arrival ( لجة الوصول ), become one of the people of witnessing ( أهل المشاهدة ), see Him directly, and converse intimately with Him face to face. O God, make us among those who arrive at the Reality itself ( العين ), not those who merely follow the trace ( الأثر ).” Source comparison: Cairo I:60 has دون التابعين للأثر , “rather than those who follow the trace.” The inspected electronic text of al-Bayḍāwī, Anwār al-tanzīl , I:29, on Q1:5, reads دون السامعين للأثر , “rather than those who hear of the trace.” Both contrast arrival with an indirect relation to its evidence, but the source witness specifies hearing where Cairo specifies following. The translation retains Cairo’s wording and records the difference without choosing an unverified authorial reading. “It is the Arabs’ custom to vary their speech and pass from one mode of expression to another, refreshing and enlivening the hearer. Thus they move from direct address to the third person, and from the third person to the first, as in His saying, ‘Until, when you are in ships and they sail with them upon a wind …’ [10:22]. He did not say ‘with you.’ And in His saying, ‘He sent the winds, which stir up clouds, and We drove them to a land …’ [35:9]”: that is, He did not say, “and He drove them.” Consult his discussion in full. The rhetorical shift ( الالتفات , iltifāt) here occurs in “You alone we worship.” He did not say “Him alone we worship,” although the apparent continuation of the preceding discourse would have been in the third person ( الغيبة ). The beauty of the shift lies in the fact that the One described has now become determinate and present."},{"type":"theology","title":"Divine aid and human action","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-060\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E60.4\">Al-Uqlīshī said: “This is the verse concerning which the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, said: ‘When the servant says, “You alone we worship, and You alone we ask for help,” God Most High says, “This is between Me and My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks.”’ Its meaning is: ‘Whichever servant turns to Me in worship and asks My assistance in it, his worship is accepted, and My assistance in it is assured to him, so that he may perform it as it should be performed.’ Worship is the servant’s attribute, and assistance is from God Most High to the servant. For this reason He said, ‘This is between Me and My servant.’”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-061\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E60.5\">Ibn Juzayy said: “That is, we ask Your assistance in worship and in all our affairs. This is evidence of the falsity of the positions of the Qadariyya and the Jabriyya, and that the truth lies between the two.”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Uqlīshī said: “This is the verse concerning which the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, said: ‘When the servant says, “You alone we worship, and You alone we ask for help,” God Most High says, “This is between Me and My servant, and My servant shall have what he asks.”’ Its meaning is: ‘Whichever servant turns to Me in worship and asks My assistance in it, his worship is accepted, and My assistance in it is assured to him, so that he may perform it as it should be performed.’ Worship is the servant’s attribute, and assistance is from God Most High to the servant. For this reason He said, ‘This is between Me and My servant.’” Ibn Juzayy said: “That is, we ask Your assistance in worship and in all our affairs. This is evidence of the falsity of the positions of the Qadariyya and the Jabriyya, and that the truth lies between the two.”"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Wisdom and power; effacement and subsistence","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:61<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-063\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E61.0\">When the Real, glorious is His majesty, disclosed Himself from the realm of jabarūt (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الجبروت<\/span>) to the realm of malakūt (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الملكوت<\/span>), and praised Himself by Himself, He also disclosed Himself and descended from the realm of malakūt to the realm of mulk (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الملك<\/span>) through His power (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القدرة<\/span>) and wisdom (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/span>), in order to manifest the traces of His Names and Attributes. He manifested servanthood (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العبودية<\/span>) and concealed Lordship (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الربوبية<\/span>); He manifested wisdom and concealed power. He thus made the realm of wisdom (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الحكمة<\/span>) address the realm of power (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم القدرة<\/span>), submit to it, worship, draw from it assistance and guidance, and seek protection from the path of error and misguidance.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-064\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E61.1\">The realm of wisdom (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الحكمة<\/span>) is the locus of legal-moral responsibility (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">محل التكليف<\/span>), while the realm of power (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم القدرة<\/span>) is the locus of divine disposal (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">محل التصريف<\/span>). The realm of wisdom is the realm of bodily forms (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الأشباح<\/span>), and the realm of power the realm of spirits (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الأرواح<\/span>). “You alone we worship” belongs to the people of the realm of wisdom; “from You alone we seek help” to the people of the realm of power. Hence Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī, may God be pleased with him, said: “‘You alone we worship’ is Sacred Law (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شريعة<\/span>); ‘from You alone we seek help’ is Reality (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حقيقة<\/span>). ‘You alone we worship’ is islām; ‘from You alone we seek help’ is iḥsān. ‘You alone we worship’ is worship (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبادة<\/span>); ‘from You alone we seek help’ is servanthood (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبودية<\/span>). ‘You alone we worship’ is distinction/separation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فرق<\/span>); ‘from You alone we seek help’ is gathering/unification (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جمع<\/span>).”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-065\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E61.2\">Or, if you wish: “‘You alone we worship’ belongs to those who act for God (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العمل لله<\/span>), the sincere; ‘from You alone we seek help’ belongs to those who act through God (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العمل بالله<\/span>), the people of tawḥīd. Acting for God brings reward; acting through God brings nearness. Acting for God brings realisation of worship; acting through God brings rectification of the will. Acting for God is the attribute of every worshipper; acting through God is the attribute of every seeker. Acting for God is standing by the rulings governing outward things; acting through God is standing by the rectification of inward consciences.” So said al-Qushayrī.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-066\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E61.3\">People are three classes with respect to witnessing power (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهود القدرة<\/span>) and wisdom (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/span>). One class is veiled by wisdom from witnessing power: these are the veiled among the heedless, who stop with “You alone we worship.” A second class is veiled by witnessing power from wisdom: these are the people of effacement (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الفناء<\/span>), who stop with “from You alone we seek help.” A third class is veiled neither by wisdom from power nor by power from wisdom: they give every possessor of a right his right and every possessor of a share his due. These are the perfected among the people of subsistence (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل البقاء<\/span>), who combine “You alone we worship” with “from You alone we seek help.” Success is through God.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"transition\" id=\"pair-067\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E61.4\">He then explained the greatest aim and the most important thing sought: asking for guidance (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهداية<\/span>) and divine enabling (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوفيق<\/span>) to the very essence of realisation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين التحقيق<\/span>). He said:<\/p><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"I:61 When the Real, glorious is His majesty, disclosed Himself from the realm of jabarūt ( عالم الجبروت ) to the realm of malakūt ( عالم الملكوت ), and praised Himself by Himself, He also disclosed Himself and descended from the realm of malakūt to the realm of mulk ( عالم الملك ) through His power ( القدرة ) and wisdom ( الحكمة ), in order to manifest the traces of His Names and Attributes. He manifested servanthood ( العبودية ) and concealed Lordship ( الربوبية ); He manifested wisdom and concealed power. He thus made the realm of wisdom ( عالم الحكمة ) address the realm of power ( عالم القدرة ), submit to it, worship, draw from it assistance and guidance, and seek protection from the path of error and misguidance. The realm of wisdom ( عالم الحكمة ) is the locus of legal-moral responsibility ( محل التكليف ), while the realm of power ( عالم القدرة ) is the locus of divine disposal ( محل التصريف ). The realm of wisdom is the realm of bodily forms ( عالم الأشباح ), and the realm of power the realm of spirits ( عالم الأرواح ). “You alone we worship” belongs to the people of the realm of wisdom; “from You alone we seek help” to the people of the realm of power. Hence Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī, may God be pleased with him, said: “‘You alone we worship’ is Sacred Law ( شريعة ); ‘from You alone we seek help’ is Reality ( حقيقة ). ‘You alone we worship’ is islām; ‘from You alone we seek help’ is iḥsān. ‘You alone we worship’ is worship ( عبادة ); ‘from You alone we seek help’ is servanthood ( عبودية ). ‘You alone we worship’ is distinction/separation ( فرق ); ‘from You alone we seek help’ is gathering/unification ( جمع ).” Or, if you wish: “‘You alone we worship’ belongs to those who act for God ( العمل لله ), the sincere; ‘from You alone we seek help’ belongs to those who act through God ( العمل بالله ), the people of tawḥīd. Acting for God brings reward; acting through God brings nearness. Acting for God brings realisation of worship; acting through God brings rectification of the will. Acting for God is the attribute of every worshipper; acting through God is the attribute of every seeker. Acting for God is standing by the rulings governing outward things; acting through God is standing by the rectification of inward consciences.” So said al-Qushayrī. People are three classes with respect to witnessing power ( شهود القدرة ) and wisdom ( الحكمة ). One class is veiled by wisdom from witnessing power: these are the veiled among the heedless, who stop with “You alone we worship.” A second class is veiled by witnessing power from wisdom: these are the people of effacement ( أهل الفناء ), who stop with “from You alone we seek help.” A third class is veiled neither by wisdom from power nor by power from wisdom: they give every possessor of a right his right and every possessor of a share his due. These are the perfected among the people of subsistence ( أهل البقاء ), who combine “You alone we worship” with “from You alone we seek help.” Success is through God. He then explained the greatest aim and the most important thing sought: asking for guidance ( الهداية ) and divine enabling ( التوفيق ) to the very essence of realisation ( عين التحقيق ). He said:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":11831,"arabic_characters":5839},{"id":"fatiha_4","q":"1:6","s":1,"surah":"Al-Fātiḥa","title":"Al-Fātiḥa 1:6 — commentary","source":"Cairo I:52–70","cairo":"I:61–64","source_page_first":61,"source_page_last":64,"ar":"اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ (6)\nقلت: الهدايةُ في الأصل: الدلالة بلطف، ولذلك تُستعمل في الخير، وقوله: فَاهْدُوهُمْ إِلى صِراطِ الْجَحِيمِ على التهكم، والفعل منه (هَدَى) بالفتح، وأصله أن يُعدى باللام، أو «إلى» ، فَعْومل هنا معاملة:\nوَاخْتارَ مُوسى قَوْمَهُ. والصراط لغة: الطريق، مشتق من سَرَط الطعامَ إذا ابتلعه، فكأنها تبتلع السابلةَ أي\nالمارَّة به، وَقُلِبَتْ السين صاداً لتطابق الطاء في الإطباق، وقد تُشَمُّ زاياً لقرب المَخرج، و (المستقيم) : الذي لا عوج فيه، والمراد به طريق الحق المُوصَّلة إلى الله.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: معلما لعباده كيف يطلبونه، وما ينبغي لهم أن يطلبوا، أي: قُولوا (اهدنا) أي: أَرشِدْنا إلى الطريق المستقيم، الموصلة إلى حضرة النعيم، والطريقُ المستقيم هو السيرُ على الشريعة المحمدية في الظاهر، والتبرِّي من الحول والقوة في الباطن، أو تقول: هو أن يكون ظاهرُك شريعةً وباطنك حقيقة، ظاهرك عبودية وباطنك حرية، الفرق على ظاهرك موجود والجمع في باطنك مشهود، وفي الحكم: «متى جَعَلَك في الظاهر ممتثلاً لأمره وفي الباطن مستسلماً لقهره، فقد أعظم المِنَّة عليك» .\nفالصراط المستقيم الذي أمرَنَا الحقُّ بطلبه هو: الجمع بين الشريعة والحقيقة، والمفهوم من قوله: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ، ولذلك وصلَه به، فكأن الحق- سبحانه- يقول: يا عبادي احمدوني ومجدوني وأفردوني بالقصد وخُصُّوني بالعبادة، وكونوا في ظاهركم مشتغلين بعبادتي، وفي باطنكم مستعينين بحولي وقوتي، أو كونوا في ظاهركم متأدبين بخدمتي، وفي باطنكم مشاهدين لقدرتي وعظمة ربوبيتي.\nوقال سيدنا علي- كَرَّمَ الله وجهه-: (الصراط المستقيم هنا القرآن) . وقال جابر رضي الله عنه: (هو الإسلام) يعني الحنيفية السمحاء، وقال سهل بن عبد الله: (هو طريق محمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم) . يعني اتباعَ ما جاء به. وحاصله ما تقدم من إصلاح الظاهر بالشريعة والباطن بالحقيقة، فهذا هو الطريق المستقيم الذي من سلكه كان من الواصلين المقربين مع النبيين والصدِّيقين.\nفإن قلتَ: إذا كان العبدُ ذاهباً على هذا المنهاج المستقيم، فكيف يطلب ما هو حاصل؟ فالجواب: أنه طلب التثبيت على ما هو حاصل، والإرشاد إلى ما هو ليس بحاصل، فأهل مقام الإسلام يطلبون الثبات على الإسلام، الذي هو حاصل، والترقي إلى مقام الإيمان الذي ليس بحاصل، على طريق الصوفية، الذين يخصون العمل الظاهر بمقام الإسلام، والعمل الباطن بمقام الإيمان، وأهلُ الإيمان يطلبون الثبات على الإيمان الذي هو حاصل، والترقي إلى مقام الإحسان الذي ليس بحاصل، وأهل مقام الإحسان يطلبون الثبات على الإحسان، والترقي إلى مالا نهاية له من كشوفات العرفان وَفَوْقَ كُلِّ ذِي عِلْمٍ عَلِيمٌ.\nوقال الشيخ أبو العباس المرسي رضي الله عنه: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ بالتثبيت فيما هو حاصل، والإرشاد فيما ليس بحاصل، ثم قال: عمومُ المؤمنين يقولون: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ أي: بالتثبيت فيما هو\nحاصل، والإرشاد لما ليس بحاصل، فإنه حصل لهم التوحيد وفاتَهُم درجات الصالحين، والصالحون يقولون:\nاهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ معناه: نسألك التثبيت فيما هو حاصل والإرشاد إلى ما ليس بحاصل، فإنهم حصل لهم الصلاح وفاتهم درجات الشهداء، والشهداءُ يقولون: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ أي بالتثبيت فيما هو حاصل والإرشاد إلى ما ليس بحاصل، فإنهم حصلت لهم الشهادة وفاتهم درجات الصديقين، والصديقون يقولون: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ أي: بالتثبيت فيما هو حاصل والإرشاد إلى ما ليس بحاصل، فإنهم حَصل لهم درجات الصديقين وفاتهم درجات القطب. والقطبُ يقول: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ بالتثبيت فيما هو حاصل والإرشاد إلى ما ليس بحاصل، فإنه حصل له رتبة القطبانية، وفاته علم ما إذا شاء الله أن يطلعه عليه أطلعه. هـ.\nوقال بعضهم: الهدايةُ إما للعين وإما للأثرِ الدالَّ على العين، ولا نهاية للأولى. قلت: فالأولى لأهل الشهود والعِيان، والثانية لأهل الدليل والبرهان، فالهداية للعين هي الدلالةُ على الله. والهداية للأثر هي الدلالة على العمل، «مَنْ دَلَّكَ على الله فقد نصحك، ومَن دلَّك على العمل فقد أتعبك» . وإنما كانت الأولى لا نهاية لها لأن الترقي بعد المعرفة لا نهاية له. بخلاف الدلالة على الأثر فنهايتها الوصول إلى العين، إن كان الدالُّ عارفاً بالطريق.\nقال البيضاوي: وهداية الله تتنوَّعُ أنواعاً لا يحصيها عد وَإِنْ تَعُدُّوا نِعْمَتَ اللَّهِ لا تُحْصُوها لكنها تنحصر في أجناس مترتبة:\nالأول: إِفاضَةُ الُقُوَى التي بها يتمكنُ المرء من الاهتداء إلى مصالحه، كالقوة العقلية والحواس الباطنة والمشاعر الظاهرة.\nالثاني: نَصْبُ الدلائل الفارقة بين الحق والباطل والصلاح والفساد، وإليه الإشارة بقوله: وَهَدَيْناهُ النَّجْدَيْنِ، وقال: فَهَدَيْناهُمْ فَاسْتَحَبُّوا الْعَمى عَلَى الْهُدى.\nالثالث الهدايةُ بإرسال الرسل وإنزال الكُتُب، وإياها عني بقوله: وَجَعَلْناهُمْ أَئِمَّةً يَهْدُونَ بِأَمْرِنا، وقوله:\nإِنَّ هذَا الْقُرْآنَ يَهْدِي لِلَّتِي هِيَ أَقْوَمُ.\nالرابع: أن يكشف عن قلوبهم السرائر ويُرِيَهُمْ الأشياءَ كما هي بالوحي والإلهام والمنامات الصادقة. وهذا يختص بِنَيْله الأنبياءُ والأولياءُ، وإياه عني بقوله: أُولئِكَ الَّذِينَ هَدَى اللَّهُ فَبِهُداهُمُ اقْتَدِهْ، وَالَّذِينَ جاهَدُوا فِينا لَنَهْدِيَنَّهُمْ سُبُلَنا.\nفالمطلوب: إما زيادةُ ما مُنحوه من الهدى والثباتُ عليه، أو حصولُ المراتب المترتبة عليه، فإذا قال العارفُ الواصل عَنَى بقوله: أرشدنا طريق السير فيك، لتمحُوَ عنا ظلماتِ أحوالنا، وتُمِيطَ غواشِيَ أبداننا، لنستضيء بنور قدسك فنراكَ بنورك. هـ.\nقلت: قوله الرابع ... الخ، في عبارته قَلَقٌ واختصار، والصواب أن يقول: الرابع- أن يكشف عن قلوبهم الظُّلَمَ والأغيار، ويُشرق عليها الأنوار والأسرار، ويُريهم الأشياء كما هي بالوحي والإلهام، وباستعمال الفكرة في عظمةِ الملك العلاَّم، حتى تستولي أنوارُ المعاني على حِسِّ الأواني، ثم يقول: وهذا قسم يختصّ بنيله الأنبياء والأولياء.\nوقوله: فإذا قال العارف.. الخ، الصواب أن يقول: فإذا قاله المريد السائر لأن الواصل انمحت عنه الظلماتُ كلها والغواشي وسائرُ الأكدار لأن الله تعالى غطَّى وصفه بوصفه ونعته بنعته، فلم يَبْقَ له وصفٌ ظُلماني. وأيضاً قوله: [أرشدنا إلى طريق السير] إنما يناسب السائر دون الواصل لأن الواصل ما بَقِيَ له إلا الترقي، ولا يُسمى في اصطلاح الصوفية [السير] إلا قبلَ الوصول. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم فسرّ الطريق المستقيم، فقال:","quran_ar":"اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ (6)","quran_en":"“Guide us upon the straight path.” [1:6]","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Guidance and the straight path: language and readings","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:61–62<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-069\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E61.6\">I say: Guidance (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهداية<\/span>, hidāya) originally means indicating or directing with gentleness (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الدلالة بلطف<\/span>), and for that reason it is normally used of what is good. “Guide them to the path of Hell” [37:23] is therefore ironic or derisive (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">على التهكم<\/span>). The verb is hadā (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هدى<\/span>). Its normal construction takes the preposition li- or ilā; here it governs a direct object without a preposition, by analogy with “Moses chose his people” [7:155]. Ṣirāṭ (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصراط<\/span>), in the language, means “road.” It is derived from saraṭa al-ṭaʿām, “he swallowed the food,” as though the road swallowed its travellers, those passing along it. The sīn was changed to ṣād to harmonise with the emphatic ṭāʾ (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإطباق<\/span>); it may also be pronounced with an admixture of zāy because their points of articulation are close. Mustaqīm (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المستقيم<\/span>) means “having no crookedness.” What is intended is the path of the Real (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طريق الحق<\/span>) that brings one to God.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:61–62 I say: Guidance ( الهداية , hidāya) originally means indicating or directing with gentleness ( الدلالة بلطف ), and for that reason it is normally used of what is good. “Guide them to the path of Hell” [37:23] is therefore ironic or derisive ( على التهكم ). The verb is hadā ( هدى ). Its normal construction takes the preposition li- or ilā; here it governs a direct object without a preposition, by analogy with “Moses chose his people” [7:155]. Ṣirāṭ ( الصراط ), in the language, means “road.” It is derived from saraṭa al-ṭaʿām, “he swallowed the food,” as though the road swallowed its travellers, those passing along it. The sīn was changed to ṣād to harmonise with the emphatic ṭāʾ ( الإطباق ); it may also be pronounced with an admixture of zāy because their points of articulation are close. Mustaqīm ( المستقيم ) means “having no crookedness.” What is intended is the path of the Real ( طريق الحق ) that brings one to God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The straight path: Sacred Law and Reality","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:62<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-070\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E62.0\">The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, teaching His servants how to seek Him and what they ought to seek: Say, “Guide us” - direct us to the straight path that brings one to the Presence of bliss (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حضرة النعيم<\/span>). The straight path is outwardly to travel according to the Muḥammadan Sacred Law (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة المحمدية<\/span>), and inwardly to divest oneself of any claim to power or strength of one's own (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التبرّي من الحول والقوة<\/span>). Or say: it is that your outward be Sacred Law (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شريعة<\/span>) and your inward Reality (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حقيقة<\/span>); your outward servanthood (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبودية<\/span>) and your inward freedom (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حرية<\/span>); distinction (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفرق<\/span>) present outwardly, and gathering (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجمع<\/span>) witnessed inwardly. In the Ḥikam: “When He makes you outwardly obedient to His command and inwardly surrendered to His overpowering might, He has bestowed an immense favour upon you.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-071\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E62.1\">The straight path that the Real commands us to seek is the conjunction of Sacred Law and Reality (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجمع بين الشريعة والحقيقة<\/span>), understood from “You alone we worship, and from You alone we seek help.” Hence He joins the petition for guidance directly to those words. It is as though the Real, glorified is He, says: “My servants, praise Me and glorify Me, make Me alone your aim, and single Me out in worship. Outwardly occupy yourselves with My worship; inwardly seek assistance through My power and strength. Or outwardly observe the courtesies of My service; inwardly witness My power and the majesty of My Lordship.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-072\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E62.2\">Our master ʿAlī, may God ennoble his countenance, said: “The straight path here is the Qurʾān.” Jābir, may God be pleased with him, said: “It is Islam,” meaning the generous, upright religion (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحنيفية السمحاء<\/span>). Sahl ibn ʿAbd Allāh said: “It is the path of Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace,” meaning following what he brought. The substance of these statements is what preceded: rectifying the outward through Sacred Law (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة<\/span>) and the inward through Reality (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة<\/span>). This is the straight path; whoever travels it is among those who arrive and are brought near (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواصلين المقربين<\/span>), together with the prophets and the utterly truthful.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:62 The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, teaching His servants how to seek Him and what they ought to seek: Say, “Guide us” - direct us to the straight path that brings one to the Presence of bliss ( حضرة النعيم ). The straight path is outwardly to travel according to the Muḥammadan Sacred Law ( الشريعة المحمدية ), and inwardly to divest oneself of any claim to power or strength of one's own ( التبرّي من الحول والقوة ). Or say: it is that your outward be Sacred Law ( شريعة ) and your inward Reality ( حقيقة ); your outward servanthood ( عبودية ) and your inward freedom ( حرية ); distinction ( الفرق ) present outwardly, and gathering ( الجمع ) witnessed inwardly. In the Ḥikam: “When He makes you outwardly obedient to His command and inwardly surrendered to His overpowering might, He has bestowed an immense favour upon you.” The straight path that the Real commands us to seek is the conjunction of Sacred Law and Reality ( الجمع بين الشريعة والحقيقة ), understood from “You alone we worship, and from You alone we seek help.” Hence He joins the petition for guidance directly to those words. It is as though the Real, glorified is He, says: “My servants, praise Me and glorify Me, make Me alone your aim, and single Me out in worship. Outwardly occupy yourselves with My worship; inwardly seek assistance through My power and strength. Or outwardly observe the courtesies of My service; inwardly witness My power and the majesty of My Lordship.” Our master ʿAlī, may God ennoble his countenance, said: “The straight path here is the Qurʾān.” Jābir, may God be pleased with him, said: “It is Islam,” meaning the generous, upright religion ( الحنيفية السمحاء ). Sahl ibn ʿAbd Allāh said: “It is the path of Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace,” meaning following what he brought. The substance of these statements is what preceded: rectifying the outward through Sacred Law ( الشريعة ) and the inward through Reality ( الحقيقة ). This is the straight path; whoever travels it is among those who arrive and are brought near ( الواصلين المقربين ), together with the prophets and the utterly truthful."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Why the guided continue to ask for guidance","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-073\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E62.3\">If you ask: when the servant is already proceeding upon this straight way, how can he ask for what he already possesses? The answer is that he asks for firmness in what he has attained and guidance to what he has not attained. The people of the station of islām ask for steadfastness in the islām already attained and ascent (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الترقي<\/span>) to the station of īmān not yet attained. This follows Sufi usage, which assigns outward action specifically to the station of islām and inward action to the station of īmān. The people of īmān ask for steadfastness in the īmān attained and ascent to the station of iḥsān not yet attained. The people of iḥsān ask for steadfastness in iḥsān and ascent into the endless unveilings of gnosis (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كشوفات العرفان<\/span>): “Above every possessor of knowledge is one more knowing” [12:76].<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:62–63<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-074\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E62.4\">Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī, may God be pleased with him, said: “‘Guide us upon the straight path’: by making us firm in what is attained, and directing us towards what is not attained.” Then he said: “The generality of believers say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: by making us firm in what is attained and directing us towards what is not attained. They have attained the affirmation of unity, but the degrees of the righteous have escaped them. The righteous say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: we ask You to make us firm in what is attained and direct us towards what is not attained. They have attained righteousness, but the degrees of the martyrs have escaped them. The martyrs say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: by making us firm in what is attained and directing us towards what is not attained. They have attained martyrdom, but the degrees of the utterly truthful have escaped them. The utterly truthful say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: by making us firm in what is attained and directing us towards what is not attained. They have attained the degrees of the utterly truthful, but the degrees of the Pole have escaped them. The Pole says, ‘Guide us upon the straight path’: by making him firm in what is attained and directing him towards what is not attained. He has attained the rank of the Pole, but there remains knowledge which, if God wills to acquaint him with it, He acquaints him with.” Here ends the quotation.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:63<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-075\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E63.0\">Someone said: “Guidance is either to the Reality itself (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العين<\/span>) or to the trace (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأثر<\/span>) that indicates the Reality; the first has no end.” I say: the first belongs to the people of witnessing and direct vision (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الشهود والعيان<\/span>), the second to the people of proof and demonstration (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الدليل والبرهان<\/span>). Guidance to the Reality itself is direction to God; guidance to the trace is direction to works. “Whoever directs you to God has counselled you sincerely; whoever directs you to works has wearied you.” The first has no end because ascent (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الترقي<\/span>) after gnosis (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/span>) has no end. Direction to the trace is otherwise: its end is arrival (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوصول<\/span>) at the Reality itself, provided the guide knows the path.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"If you ask: when the servant is already proceeding upon this straight way, how can he ask for what he already possesses? The answer is that he asks for firmness in what he has attained and guidance to what he has not attained. The people of the station of islām ask for steadfastness in the islām already attained and ascent ( الترقي ) to the station of īmān not yet attained. This follows Sufi usage, which assigns outward action specifically to the station of islām and inward action to the station of īmān. The people of īmān ask for steadfastness in the īmān attained and ascent to the station of iḥsān not yet attained. The people of iḥsān ask for steadfastness in iḥsān and ascent into the endless unveilings of gnosis ( كشوفات العرفان ): “Above every possessor of knowledge is one more knowing” [12:76]. I:62–63 Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī, may God be pleased with him, said: “‘Guide us upon the straight path’: by making us firm in what is attained, and directing us towards what is not attained.” Then he said: “The generality of believers say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: by making us firm in what is attained and directing us towards what is not attained. They have attained the affirmation of unity, but the degrees of the righteous have escaped them. The righteous say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: we ask You to make us firm in what is attained and direct us towards what is not attained. They have attained righteousness, but the degrees of the martyrs have escaped them. The martyrs say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: by making us firm in what is attained and directing us towards what is not attained. They have attained martyrdom, but the degrees of the utterly truthful have escaped them. The utterly truthful say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ meaning: by making us firm in what is attained and directing us towards what is not attained. They have attained the degrees of the utterly truthful, but the degrees of the Pole have escaped them. The Pole says, ‘Guide us upon the straight path’: by making him firm in what is attained and directing him towards what is not attained. He has attained the rank of the Pole, but there remains knowledge which, if God wills to acquaint him with it, He acquaints him with.” Here ends the quotation. I:63 Someone said: “Guidance is either to the Reality itself ( العين ) or to the trace ( الأثر ) that indicates the Reality; the first has no end.” I say: the first belongs to the people of witnessing and direct vision ( أهل الشهود والعيان ), the second to the people of proof and demonstration ( أهل الدليل والبرهان ). Guidance to the Reality itself is direction to God; guidance to the trace is direction to works. “Whoever directs you to God has counselled you sincerely; whoever directs you to works has wearied you.” The first has no end because ascent ( الترقي ) after gnosis ( المعرفة ) has no end. Direction to the trace is otherwise: its end is arrival ( الوصول ) at the Reality itself, provided the guide knows the path."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Al-Bayḍāwī’s four kinds of guidance","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-076\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E63.1\">Al-Bayḍāwī said: “God’s guidance takes forms beyond numbering: ‘If you count God’s blessing, you will not enumerate it’ [14:34]. Yet these fall within successively ordered kinds:<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-077\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E63.2\">“The first is the bestowal of the faculties through which a person is enabled to find his way to what benefits him, such as the intellectual faculty, the internal senses, and the external organs of perception.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-078\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E63.3\">“The second is the establishment of indications distinguishing truth from falsehood, and soundness from corruption. This is indicated by His saying, ‘We guided him upon the two high ways’ [90:10], and ‘We guided them, but they preferred blindness to guidance’ [41:17].<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-079\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E63.4\">“The third is guidance by sending messengers and sending down scriptures. This is meant by His saying, ‘We made them leaders, guiding by Our command’ [21:73], and ‘This Qurʾān guides towards that which is most upright’ [17:9].<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-080\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E63.5\">“The fourth is that He should uncover the mysteries from their hearts and show them things as they are, through revelation, inspiration, and true dreams. The attainment of this is particular to the prophets and the saints. This is meant by His saying, ‘Those are they whom God guided; so follow their guidance’ [6:90], and ‘Those who strive in Us, We shall surely guide them upon Our ways’ [29:69].<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:64<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-081\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E64.0\">“What is sought is therefore either an increase in the guidance already bestowed upon them and steadfastness in it, or the attainment of the degrees consequent upon it. When the gnostic who has arrived says it, he means by his words: ‘Direct us upon the path of journeying in You, that You may efface from us the darknesses of our states and remove the coverings of our bodies, so that we may be illumined by the light of Your holiness and see You through Your light.’” Here ends the quotation.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Bayḍāwī said: “God’s guidance takes forms beyond numbering: ‘If you count God’s blessing, you will not enumerate it’ [14:34]. Yet these fall within successively ordered kinds: “The first is the bestowal of the faculties through which a person is enabled to find his way to what benefits him, such as the intellectual faculty, the internal senses, and the external organs of perception. “The second is the establishment of indications distinguishing truth from falsehood, and soundness from corruption. This is indicated by His saying, ‘We guided him upon the two high ways’ [90:10], and ‘We guided them, but they preferred blindness to guidance’ [41:17]. “The third is guidance by sending messengers and sending down scriptures. This is meant by His saying, ‘We made them leaders, guiding by Our command’ [21:73], and ‘This Qurʾān guides towards that which is most upright’ [17:9]. “The fourth is that He should uncover the mysteries from their hearts and show them things as they are, through revelation, inspiration, and true dreams. The attainment of this is particular to the prophets and the saints. This is meant by His saying, ‘Those are they whom God guided; so follow their guidance’ [6:90], and ‘Those who strive in Us, We shall surely guide them upon Our ways’ [29:69]. I:64 “What is sought is therefore either an increase in the guidance already bestowed upon them and steadfastness in it, or the attainment of the degrees consequent upon it. When the gnostic who has arrived says it, he means by his words: ‘Direct us upon the path of journeying in You, that You may efface from us the darknesses of our states and remove the coverings of our bodies, so that we may be illumined by the light of Your holiness and see You through Your light.’” Here ends the quotation."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Ibn ʿAjība’s clarification: journeying, arrival and ascent","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-082\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E64.1\">I say: His wording under “the fourth” is awkward (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في عبارته قلق<\/span>) and overly compressed. The sounder expression would be: “The fourth is that He unveil from their hearts the darknesses and all other-than-God (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الظُّلَم والأغيار<\/span>), cause lights and mysteries (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأنوار والأسرار<\/span>) to shine upon them, and show them things as they are through revelation and inspiration, and through employing reflection upon the majesty of the all-knowing King, until the lights of the meanings (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أنوار المعاني<\/span>) dominate the sensible aspect of the vessels (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حسّ الأواني<\/span>).” Then one should say: “This is a kind whose attainment is particular to prophets and saints.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-083\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E64.2\">As for his words, “When the gnostic who has arrived says it...,” the sounder wording is: “When the disciple who is journeying (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريد السائر<\/span>) says it.” For from one who has arrived (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواصل<\/span>), all darknesses, coverings, and other turbidity have been effaced: God Most High has covered his description with His Description and his attribute with His Attribute, so no dark attribute remains to him. Moreover, “Direct us to the path of journeying (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طريق السير<\/span>)” suits the traveller (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السائر<\/span>), not one who has arrived. The one who has arrived has nothing left but ascent (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الترقي<\/span>); in Sufi technical usage (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اصطلاح الصوفية<\/span>), “journeying” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السير<\/span>) is used only for what precedes arrival (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوصول<\/span>). God Most High knows best.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"transition\" id=\"pair-084\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E64.3\">He then explained the straight path, saying:<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: His wording under “the fourth” is awkward ( في عبارته قلق ) and overly compressed. The sounder expression would be: “The fourth is that He unveil from their hearts the darknesses and all other-than-God ( الظُّلَم والأغيار ), cause lights and mysteries ( الأنوار والأسرار ) to shine upon them, and show them things as they are through revelation and inspiration, and through employing reflection upon the majesty of the all-knowing King, until the lights of the meanings ( أنوار المعاني ) dominate the sensible aspect of the vessels ( حسّ الأواني ).” Then one should say: “This is a kind whose attainment is particular to prophets and saints.” As for his words, “When the gnostic who has arrived says it...,” the sounder wording is: “When the disciple who is journeying ( المريد السائر ) says it.” For from one who has arrived ( الواصل ), all darknesses, coverings, and other turbidity have been effaced: God Most High has covered his description with His Description and his attribute with His Attribute, so no dark attribute remains to him. Moreover, “Direct us to the path of journeying ( طريق السير )” suits the traveller ( السائر ), not one who has arrived. The one who has arrived has nothing left but ascent ( الترقي ); in Sufi technical usage ( اصطلاح الصوفية ), “journeying” ( السير ) is used only for what precedes arrival ( الوصول ). God Most High knows best. He then explained the straight path, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":9301,"arabic_characters":5455},{"id":"fatiha_5","q":"1:7","s":1,"surah":"Al-Fātiḥa","title":"Al-Fātiḥa 1:7 — commentary","source":"Cairo I:52–70","cairo":"I:64–70","source_page_first":64,"source_page_last":70,"ar":"صِراطَ الَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا الضَّالِّينَ (7)\nقلت: (صراط) بدل من الأول- بدل الكل من الكل- وهو في حكم تكرير العامل من حيث إنه المقصود بالنسبة، وفائدته: التوكيد والتنصيص على أن طريق المسلمين هو المشهود عليه بالاستقامة، على آكد وجه وأبلغه لأنه جعله كالتفسير والبيان له، فكأنه من البيِّن الذي لا خفاء فيه، وأن الصراط المستقيم ما يكون طريق المؤمنين، وغَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ بدل من (الذين) على معنى أن المنعم عليهم هم الذين سَلِمُوا من الغضب والضلال.\nأو صفة له مُبيَّنة أو مقيدة على معنى أنهم جمعوا بين النعمة المطلقة، وهي نعمة الإيمان، وبين السلامة من الغضب والضلال، وذلك إنما يصح بأحد تأويلين: إجراء الموصول مجرى النكرة، إذ لم يُقصد به معهود كالمعرَّف في قوله:\nولَقَد أَمُرُ علُى اللئيم يَسُبنّي(1) ...\nأو يُجعل (غير) مَعْرِفةً لأنه أُضيف إلى مآلَهُ ضدٍّ واحد، وهو المنعمُ عليه، فيتعينُ تَعيُّن الحركة غير السكون، وإلا لزِم عليه نعت المعرفة بالنكرة. فتأملْهُ.\nوالغضبُ: ثَوَرانُ النفس إرادةَ الانتقام، فإذا أسند إلى الله تعالى أريد غايته وهو العقوبة، و (عليهم) نائب فاعل، و (لا) مَزيدة لتأكيد ما في (غير) من معنى النفي، فكأنه قال: ولا المغضوب عَلَيْهِم وَلاَ الضآلين، وقرأ عمر رضي الله عنه (وغير الضالين) والضلال: العدول عن الطريق السوي عمداً أو خطأً، وله عرض عَريضٌ والتفاوت بين أدناه وأقصاه كبير. قاله البيضاوي.\nوإنما أَسند النعمة إلى الله والغضبَ إلى المجهول تعليماً للأدب، مَّآ أَصابَكَ مِنْ حَسَنَةٍ فَمِنَ اللَّهِ ... الآية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في تفسير الطريق المستقيم: هو طريق الذين أنعمتُ عليهم بالهداية والاستقامة، والمعرفة العامة والخاصة، من النبيين والصديقين والشهداء والصالحين، والمُنعَم عليهم في الآية مطلق، يصدق بكل منعَم عليه بالمعرفة والاستقامة في دينه، كالصحابة وأضرابِهِمْ. وقيل: المراد بهم أصحاب سيدنا موسى عليه السلام قبل التحريف. وقيل: أصحاب سيدنا عيسى قبل التغيير. والتحقيق أنه عام.\nقال البيضاوي: ونِعَمُ الله وإن كانت لا تُحصى كما قال الله: وَإِنْ تَعُدُّوا نِعْمَةَ اللَّهِ لا تُحْصُوها تنحصر في جنسين: دنيوي وأخروي.\nفالأول: وهو الدنيوي- قسمان: موهبي وكَسْبِي، والموهبي قسمان: رُوحاني، كنفخ الروح فيه وإشراقه بالعقل وما يتبعه من القوي، كالفهم والفكر والنطق، وجسماني: كتخليق البدن بالقوة الحالة فيه والهيئات العارضة له من الصحة وكمال الأعضاء. والكسبي: كتزكية النفس عن الرذائل، وتحليتها بالأخلاق الحسنة والملكات الفاضلة، وتزيين البدن بالهيئات المطبوعة والحُلي المستحسنة، وحصول الجاه والمال.\nوالثاني: وهو الأُخروي-: أن يغفر له ما فَرَطَ منه ويرضى عنه ويبوئه في أعلى علِّيين، مع الملائكة المقربين أبد الآبدين، والمراد القسمُ الأخير، وما يكون وُصْلة إلى نيله من القسم الأول، وأما ما عدا ذلك فيشترك فيه المؤمن والكافر. هـ.\nقال ابن جُزَيّ: النعم التي يقع عليها الشكر ثلاثة أقسام، دنيوية: كالصحة والعافية والمال الحلال. ودينية:\nكالعلم والتقوى والمعرفة. وأخرويةٌ: كالثواب على العمل القليل بالعطاء الجزيل. وقال أيضاً: والناس في الشكر على مقامين: منهم مَن يشكر على النعم الواصلة إليه، الخاصة به، ومنهم مَن يشكر الله عن جميع خلقه على النعم الواصلة إلى جميعهم. والشكر على ثلاث درجات: فدرجة العوام: الشكر على النِّعم، ودرجة الخواص: الشكر على النِّعم والنقم وعلى كل حال، ودرجة خواص الخواص: أن يغيب عن رؤية النعمة بمشاهدة المُنعم. قال رجل\nلإبراهيم بن أدهم رضى الله عنه: الفقراء إذا أُعْطُوا شَكَرُوا وإذا مُنعوا صبروا، فقال إبراهيم: هذه أخلاقُ الكلاب، ولكن القومَ إذا مُنِعوا شكروا وإذا أُعْطُوا آثروا. هـ.\nثم احترس من الطريق غير المستقيمة، فقال: غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ أي: غير طريق الذين غضبت عليهم، فلا تهدنا إليها ولا تسلك بنا سبيلها، بل سلَّمنَا من مواردها. والمراد بهم: اليهود، كذا فسرها النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم، ويَصْدُقُ بحسب العموم على كل من غضب الله عليهم، وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ أي: ولا طريق الضالين، أي: التالفين عن الحق، وهم النصارى كما قال صلى الله عليه وسلّم. والتفسيران مأخوذان من كتاب الله تعالى. قال تعالى في شأن اليهود:\nفَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ عَلى غَضَبٍ، وقال في حق النصارى: قَدْ ضَلُّوا مِنْ قَبْلُ وَأَضَلُّوا كَثِيراً وَضَلُّوا عَنْ سَواءِ السَّبِيلِ.\nواعلم أن الحق- سبحانه- قسم خلقه على ثلاثة أقسام: قسم أعدَّهم للكرم والإحسان، ليظهر فيهم اسمه الكريم أو الرحيم، وهم المنعم عليهم بالإيمان والاستقامة. وقسم أعدَّهم للانتقام والغضب، ليُظهر فيهم اسمه المنتقم أو القهار، وهم المغضوب عليهم والضالون عن طريق الحق عقلاً أو عملاً، وهم الكفار، وقسم أعدَّهم الله للحِلْم والعفو، ليُظهر فيهم اسمه تعالى الحليم والعفو، وهم أهل العصيان من المؤمنين.\nفمن رَامَ أن يكونَ الوجودُ خالياً من هذه الأقسام الثلاثة، وأن يكون الناس كلهم سواء في الهداية أو ضدها، فهو جاهل بالله وبأسمائه إذ لا بد من ظهور آثار أسمائه في هذا الآدمي، من كرم وقهرية وحِلْم وغير ذلك. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: الطريق المستقيم التي أمرنا الحق بطلبها هي: طريق الوصول إلى الحضرة، التي هي العلم بالله على نعت الشهود والعيان، وهو مقام التوحيد الخاص، الذي هو أعلى درجات أهل التوحيد، وليس فوقه إلا مقامُ توحيد الأنبياء والرسل، ولا بد فيه من تربية على يد شيخ كامل عارف بطريق السير، قد سلك المقامات ذوقاً وكشفاً، وحاز مقام الفناء والبقاء، وجمع بين الجذب والسلوك لأن الطريق عويص، قليلٌ خُطَّارُهُ، كثيرٌ قُطَّاعُه، وشيطان هذه الطريق فَقِيهٌ بمقاماته ونوازِله، فلا بد فيه من دليل، وإلا ضلّ سالكها عن سواء السبيل، وإلى هذا المعنى أشار ابن البنا، حيث قال:\nوَإِنَّمَا القَوْمُ مُسَافِرُونَ ... لِحَضْرَةِ الْحَقِّ وَظَاعِنُونَ\nفَافْتَقَرُوا فِيهِ إلَى دَلِيل ... ذِي بَصَرٍ بالسَّيْرِ وَالْمَقِيلِ\nقَدْ سَلَكَ الطَّرِيقَ ثُمَّ عَادَ ... لِيُخْبِرَ الْقَوْمَ بِمَا اسْتَفَادَ\nوقال في لطائف المنن: (مَن لم يكن له أستاذ يصله بسلسلة الأتباع، ويكشف له عن قلبه القناع، فهو في هذا الشأن لَقيطٌ لا أب له، دعي لا نسب له، فإن يكن له نور فالغالب غلبة الحال عليه، والغالب عليه وقوفه مع ما يرد من الله إليه، لم تَرْضْهُ سياسةُ التأديب والتهذيب، ولم يقده زمام التربية والتدريب) ، فهذا الطريق الذي ذكرنا هو الذي يستشعره القارئ للفاتحة عند قوله: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ مع الترقي الذي ذكره الشيخ أبو العباس المرسي رضى الله عنه المتقدم، وإذا قرأ صِراطَ الَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ استشعر، أَيْ: أنعمتَ عليهم بالوصول والتمكين في معرفتك.\nوقال الورتجبي: اهدنا مُرَادَك مِنَّا لأن الصراط المستقيم ما أراد الحق من الخلق، من الصدق والإخلاص في عبوديته وخدمته. ثم، قال: وقيل: اهدنا هُدَى العِيَانِ بعد البيان، لنستقيم لك حسب إرادتِك. وقيل: اهدنا هُدَى مَنْ يكون منك مبدؤه ليكون إليك منتهاه. ثم قال: وقال بعضهم: اهدنا، أي: ثبِّتْنا على الطريق الذي لا اعوجاج فيه، وهو الإسلام، وهو الطريق المستقيم والمنهاج القويم صِراطَ الَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ أي: منازل الذين أنعمت عليهم بالمعرفة والمحبة وحسن الأدب في الخدمة. ثم قال: غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ يعني: المطرودين عن باب العبودية، وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ يعني المُفْلِسين عن نفائس المعرفة هـ.\nقلت: والأحسن أن يقال: غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ هم الذين أَوْقَفَهُمْ عن السير اتباعُ الحظوظ والشهوات، فأوقعهم في مَهَاوِي العصيان والمخالفات، وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ هم الذين حبسهم الجهل والتقليد، فلم تنفُذْ بصائرهم إلى خالص التوحيد، فنكصوا عن توحيد العيان إلى توحيد الدليل والبرهان، وهو ضلال عند أهل الشهود والعِيان، ولو بلغ في الصلاح غايةَ الإمكان.\nوقال في الإحياء: إذا قلت بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ فافْهَمْ أن الأمور كلها بالله، وأن المراد هاهنا المُسمَّى، وإذا كانت الأمورُ كلها بالله فلا جرَم أنَّ الحمد كله لله، ثم قال: وإذا قلت: الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ فأحضرْ في قلبك أنواعَ لطفه لتتفتحَ لك رحمتُه فينبعث به رجاؤُك، ثم استشعر من قلبك التعظيم والخوف من قولك: يَوْمِ الدِّينِ. ثم قال: ثم جَدَّد الإخلاص بقولك: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ. وجدَّد العجز والاحتياج والتبرِّيَ من الحوْل والقوة بقولك: وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ، ثم اطلب اسم حاجتك، وقل: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ الذي يسوقنا إلى جوارك ويُفضي بنا إلى مرضاتك، وزِدْهُ شرحاً وتفصيلاً وتأكيداً، واستشهد بالذين أفاض عليهم نعم الهداية من النبيين والصديقين والشهداء والصالحين، دون الذين غضب عليهم من الكفار والزائغين واليهود والنصارى والصابئين.\nهـ. ملخصاً.\nوقال القشيري: قوله تعالى: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ الأمر في هذه الآية مضمر، أي: قولوا: اهدنا.\nوالصراط المستقيم: طريق الحق، وهو ما عليه أهل التوحيد، أي: أرشدْنا إلى الحق لئلا نتكل على وسائط المعاملات، فيقعَ على وجه التوحيد غُبَارُ الظنون والحسابات لتكون دليلنا عليك. ثم قال: صِراطَ الَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ أي: الواصلين بك إليك، ثم قال: غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ بنسيان التوفيق والتَّعامِي عن رؤية التأييد، وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ عن شهودِ سابقِ الاختيار، وجريان تصاريف الأقدار. هـ.\n\nتتمات:\nالأولى: هذه السورة جمعت معانى القرآن كلّها، فكأنها نسخة مختصرة منه، ولذلك سميت أمّ القرآن، فالإلهيات حاصلة من قوله: الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ. الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ، والدار الآخرة من قوله: مَلِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ، والعبادات كلّها من الاعتقادات والأحكام الظاهرة التي تقتضيها الأوامر والنواهي، من قوله: إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ والمقامات وأسرار المعاملات الباطنة- تخلية وتحلية- من قوله: اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ والأنبياء وغيرهم من قوله: الَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ وذكر طوائف الكفار من قوله: غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ.\nوقال الشيخ ابن أبي جمرة رضى الله عنه فى بيان تضمنها لكتاب الله: إن لفظ (الحمد) يتضمن كل ما في كتاب الله من الحمد والشكر لأن الحمد أعمّ من الشكر، وأتى بالعام ليدل على الصفتين. ولفظة (الله) تدل على ما فى الكتاب العزيز من أسماء الترفيع والتعظيم لأنه قيل: أنه اسم الله الأعظم، ولفظ: رَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ يدل على ما فيه من أسماء الله، سبحانه، وعلى العوالم وعلى اختلافها وخالقها والمتصرف فيها. ولفظ: الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ يتضمن كل ما فى الكتاب من المغفرة والرحمة والإنعام والعفو والإفضال، ولفظ مَلِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ يدل على ما فيه من ذكر الآخرة وما فيه من الأهوال، ولفظ إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ يتضمن ما فيه من التعبّدات وإفراده بالألوهية، ولفظ إِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ يدل على ما فيه من طلب الاستعانة وذكر الاضطرار، ولفظ اهْدِنَا الصِّراطَ الْمُسْتَقِيمَ يتضمن ما فيه من طلب الهداية إلى سبيل الخير، ولفظ: صِراطَ الَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ يتضمن ما فيه من ذكر الخصوص والمرضىّ عنهم والمعفوّ عنهم وأهل السعادة، ولفظ غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ يتضمن ما فيه من أنواع الكفر والمخالفات ومساوئهم ومآلهم فاستحقّت أن تسمى أمّا. هـ.\nوعن عليّ- كرّم الله وجهه- قال: (شرح موسى عليه السّلام التوراة فى سبعين سفرا، ولو أذن لى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلّم لأوقرت على الفاتحة سبعين بعيرا) . قلت: قوله (سبعين) تقريبا، وإلا فهى قابلة لأكثر من ذلك، وتفصيل ذلك يطول، وقد ذكرنا أصول علومها فى شرحنا الكبير عليها. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالثانية: قال ابن جزىّ: قولنا: الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعالَمِينَ أفضل عند المحققين من (لا إله إلا الله) لوجهين:\nأحدهما: ما أخرج النّسائيّ: عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلّم «أنه مَن قال: لا إله إلا الله، كتبت له عشرون حسنة، ومن قال:\nالحمد لله رب العالمين، كتبت له ثلاثون حسنة» . والثاني: أن التوحيد الذي تقتضيه (لا إله إلا الله) ، حاصل فى قولك: (رب العالمين) وزادت بقولك: الحمد لله، وفيه من المعاني ما قدّمنا. وأما قوله صلى الله عليه وسلّم: «أفضل ما قلته أنا والنبيون من قبلى: لا إله إلا الله» فإنما ذلك للتوحيد الذي تقتضيه، وقد شاركتها (الحمد لله رب العالمين) فى ذلك وزادت عليها. وهذا لمؤمن حقّق إيمانه وطلب الثواب، وأما لمن دخل فى الإسلام فيتعيّن «لا إله إلا الله» . هـ.\nقلت: والتحقيق أن كل ما يدل على التوحيد من الألفاظ يكفى فى الدخول فى الإسلام، كما قال البنانىّ فى حاشيته.\nالثالثة: قراءة الفاتحة فى الصلاة واجبة عند مالك والشافعي خلافاً لأبي حنيفة، وقد ذكرنا فى الشرح الكبير منشأ الخلاف.\nالرابعة: التأمين عند ختم الفاتحة مطلوب للدعاء الذي فيها، قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلّم: «ما حسدتكم اليهود على شىء ما حسدتكم على السّلام والتّأمين» . رواه ابن ماجة. وقال أيضا صلى الله عليه وسلّم: «إن الله أعطانى خصالا ثلاثة:\nأعطانى صلاة الصّفوف وأعطانى التحية، وإنها لتحية أهل الجنة، وأعطانى التأمين، ولم يعطه أحدا من النبيين قبلى، إلا أن يكون الله أعطاه هارون، يدعو موسى ويؤمن هارون» رواه ابن خزيمة. وسمع عليه الصلاة والسلام رجلا يدعو ويلح فقال: «أوجب إن ختم» فقال بعض القوم: بأيّ شىء يختم؟ فقال: «يؤمن فإنه إن ختم بآمين فقد أوجب» . قال أبو زهير- راوى الحديث- فإن آمين مثل الطّابع على الصحيفة. ولعلّه مرفوع إلى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم، رواه أبو داود.\nولفظ آمِّينَ بالمدّ والقصر مخففا. وتشديد الميم لغة. قيل: هو اسم من أسماء الله تعالى.. وقيل معناه: اللهم استجب، أو كذلك فافعل، أو كذلك فليكن. قاله المنذرى فى الترغيب. قال البيضاوي: بنى على الفتح كأين لالتقاء الساكنين، وجاء مدّ ألفه وقصرها. قال:\nويرحم الله عبدا قال آمينا(1)\nوليس من القرآن اتفاقا، ولكن يسنّ ختم السورة به لقوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «علّمنى جبريل عليه السّلام آمين عند فراغى من قراءة الفاتحة» . وقال: إنّه كالختم على الكتاب.\nويقوله الإمام ويجهر به فى الجهرية، لما روى عن وائل بن حجر «أنه عليه الصلاة والسلام كان إذا قرأ: وَلَا الضَّالِّينَ قال: آمين، رفع بها صوته» وعن أبى حنيفة- رحمه الله- أنه لا يقوله. والمشهور عنه أنه يخفيه كما رواه عبد الله بن مُغفل وأنس. قلت: ومشهور مذهب مالك أن الإمام لا يقوله فى الجهرية.\nثم قال: والمأموم يؤمن معه لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «إذا قال الإمام ولا الضالين، فقولوا: آمين، فإن الملائكة تقول: آمين، فمن وافق تأمينه تأمين الملائكة غُفِر لَه ما تقدَم من ذنبه» . وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق. وصلّى الله على سيدنا ومولانا محمد عين الحق والتحقيق، وعلى آله وصحبه المطهرين بعده، أعلام الطريق، وسلّم تسليما.","quran_ar":"صِراطَ الَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ الْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا الضَّالِّينَ (7)","quran_en":"“The path of those whom You have blessed, not those upon whom wrath has fallen, nor those who are astray.” [1:7]","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Substitution, qualification and negation","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-086\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E64.5\">I say: the second “path” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صراط<\/span>) is a substitute (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل<\/span>) for the first - specifically, substitution of the whole for the whole (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل الكل من الكل<\/span>). It is, in effect, like repeating the grammatical governor (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العامل<\/span>), since the substitute is what is ultimately intended by the syntactic relation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المقصود بالنسبة<\/span>). Its function is confirmation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوكيد<\/span>) and explicit specification (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التنصيص<\/span>), in the strongest and most emphatic manner, that the path of the Muslims is the path attested to be straight. He makes the second phrase an explanation and clarification of the first, as though this were self-evident and without obscurity: the straight path is the path of the believers. “Not those upon whom wrath has fallen” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غير المغضوب عليهم<\/span>) may itself be a substitute (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل<\/span>) for “those” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الذين<\/span>), meaning that those blessed are precisely those kept safe from wrath and error. Or it may be an explanatory or restrictive adjective (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صفة مبيِّنة أو مقيِّدة<\/span>): they combine the unrestricted blessing - the blessing of faith - with safety from wrath and error. This grammatical analysis is sound only on one of two interpretations: either the relative expression (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الموصول<\/span>) is treated like an indefinite (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النكرة<\/span>), since no previously identified group (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معهود<\/span>) is intended, as with the formally definite expression in the poet's words, “Indeed, I pass by the base fellow who reviles me...”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-087\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E64.6\">Or ghayr (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غير<\/span>, “other than/not”) is treated as definite (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة<\/span>) because it is annexed to something having a single contrary - namely, the one blessed - and so it becomes determinate in the way “motion, other than rest” is determinate. Otherwise the construction would require a definite noun (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/span>) to be qualified by an indefinite adjective (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النكرة<\/span>). Reflect upon it.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:65<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-088\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E65.0\">Wrath (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغضب<\/span>) is the soul's agitation with the will to take vengeance. When attributed to God Most High, what is intended is its end-result (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غايته<\/span>): punishment. “Upon them” (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عليهم<\/span>) stands as the deputy subject (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نائب فاعل<\/span>) of the passive participle al-maghḍūb. The lā in “nor” is pleonastic/additional (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مزيدة<\/span>) for strengthening the negation already contained in ghayr, as though He had said: “neither those upon whom wrath has fallen nor those who are astray.” ʿUmar, may God be pleased with him, recited, “and other than those who are astray.” Error (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الضلال<\/span>) is turning aside from the even path, deliberately or mistakenly. It has a very broad extension, and the distance between its lowest and highest degrees is great. So said al-Bayḍāwī.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-089\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E65.1\">The bestowal of favour is ascribed to God, whereas wrath is expressed in the passive, to teach courtesy: “Whatever good befalls you is from God …” [4:79], and the rest of the verse.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the second “path” ( صراط ) is a substitute ( بدل ) for the first - specifically, substitution of the whole for the whole ( بدل الكل من الكل ). It is, in effect, like repeating the grammatical governor ( العامل ), since the substitute is what is ultimately intended by the syntactic relation ( المقصود بالنسبة ). Its function is confirmation ( التوكيد ) and explicit specification ( التنصيص ), in the strongest and most emphatic manner, that the path of the Muslims is the path attested to be straight. He makes the second phrase an explanation and clarification of the first, as though this were self-evident and without obscurity: the straight path is the path of the believers. “Not those upon whom wrath has fallen” ( غير المغضوب عليهم ) may itself be a substitute ( بدل ) for “those” ( الذين ), meaning that those blessed are precisely those kept safe from wrath and error. Or it may be an explanatory or restrictive adjective ( صفة مبيِّنة أو مقيِّدة ): they combine the unrestricted blessing - the blessing of faith - with safety from wrath and error. This grammatical analysis is sound only on one of two interpretations: either the relative expression ( الموصول ) is treated like an indefinite ( النكرة ), since no previously identified group ( معهود ) is intended, as with the formally definite expression in the poet's words, “Indeed, I pass by the base fellow who reviles me...” Or ghayr ( غير , “other than/not”) is treated as definite ( معرفة ) because it is annexed to something having a single contrary - namely, the one blessed - and so it becomes determinate in the way “motion, other than rest” is determinate. Otherwise the construction would require a definite noun ( المعرفة ) to be qualified by an indefinite adjective ( النكرة ). Reflect upon it. I:65 Wrath ( الغضب ) is the soul's agitation with the will to take vengeance. When attributed to God Most High, what is intended is its end-result ( غايته ): punishment. “Upon them” ( عليهم ) stands as the deputy subject ( نائب فاعل ) of the passive participle al-maghḍūb. The lā in “nor” is pleonastic/additional ( مزيدة ) for strengthening the negation already contained in ghayr, as though He had said: “neither those upon whom wrath has fallen nor those who are astray.” ʿUmar, may God be pleased with him, recited, “and other than those who are astray.” Error ( الضلال ) is turning aside from the even path, deliberately or mistakenly. It has a very broad extension, and the distance between its lowest and highest degrees is great. So said al-Bayḍāwī. The bestowal of favour is ascribed to God, whereas wrath is expressed in the passive, to teach courtesy: “Whatever good befalls you is from God …” [4:79], and the rest of the verse."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The recipients of divine favour","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-090\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E65.2\">The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, explaining the straight path: “It is the path of those upon whom I have bestowed favour through guidance, uprightness, and general and special gnosis: the prophets, the utterly truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous.” “Those upon whom favour has been bestowed” is unrestricted in the verse. It applies to everyone favoured with gnosis and uprightness in his religion, such as the Companions and their like. It has been said that the followers of our master Moses, peace be upon him, before the distortion are intended. It has also been said that the followers of our master Jesus before the alteration are intended. The sound determination is that its meaning is general.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says, explaining the straight path: “It is the path of those upon whom I have bestowed favour through guidance, uprightness, and general and special gnosis: the prophets, the utterly truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous.” “Those upon whom favour has been bestowed” is unrestricted in the verse. It applies to everyone favoured with gnosis and uprightness in his religion, such as the Companions and their like. It has been said that the followers of our master Moses, peace be upon him, before the distortion are intended. It has also been said that the followers of our master Jesus before the alteration are intended. The sound determination is that its meaning is general."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Kinds of blessings","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-091\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E65.3\">Al-Bayḍāwī said: “Although God’s blessings are innumerable, as God says, ‘If you count God’s blessing, you will not enumerate it’ [14:34], they fall into two kinds: worldly and otherworldly.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-092\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E65.4\">“The first, the worldly, has two divisions: bestowed and acquired. The bestowed has two divisions. One is spiritual: the breathing of the spirit into a person and his illumination by the intellect and the faculties consequent upon it, such as understanding, thought, and speech. The other is bodily: the formation of the body with the power inhering in it and the conditions that come upon it, such as health and completeness of its members. Acquired blessings include purifying the soul of vices, adorning it with good character and virtuous dispositions, embellishing the body with pleasing appearances and comely ornaments, and acquiring standing and wealth.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-093\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E65.5\">“The second, the otherworldly, is that He should forgive a person’s previous faults, be pleased with him, and give him a dwelling in the highest heights, with the angels brought near, for ever and ever. It is this last division that is intended, together with whatever in the first division serves as a means of attaining it. In other things, believers and unbelievers share.” Here ends the quotation.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Bayḍāwī said: “Although God’s blessings are innumerable, as God says, ‘If you count God’s blessing, you will not enumerate it’ [14:34], they fall into two kinds: worldly and otherworldly. “The first, the worldly, has two divisions: bestowed and acquired. The bestowed has two divisions. One is spiritual: the breathing of the spirit into a person and his illumination by the intellect and the faculties consequent upon it, such as understanding, thought, and speech. The other is bodily: the formation of the body with the power inhering in it and the conditions that come upon it, such as health and completeness of its members. Acquired blessings include purifying the soul of vices, adorning it with good character and virtuous dispositions, embellishing the body with pleasing appearances and comely ornaments, and acquiring standing and wealth. “The second, the otherworldly, is that He should forgive a person’s previous faults, be pleased with him, and give him a dwelling in the highest heights, with the angels brought near, for ever and ever. It is this last division that is intended, together with whatever in the first division serves as a means of attaining it. In other things, believers and unbelievers share.” Here ends the quotation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Degrees of thanksgiving","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:65–66<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-094\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E65.6\">Ibn Juzayy said: “The blessings for which thanksgiving (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشكر<\/span>) is rendered are of three kinds: worldly, such as health, well-being, and lawful wealth; religious, such as knowledge, reverent fear, and gnosis (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/span>); and otherworldly, such as abundant reward for little work.” He also said: “People occupy two stations in thanksgiving. Some give thanks for blessings that reach them personally and belong especially to them; others thank God on behalf of all His creatures for blessings reaching them all. Thanksgiving has three degrees: the common people give thanks for blessings; the elect give thanks for blessings and afflictions, in every state; the elect of the elect become absent from seeing the blessing through witnessing the Benefactor (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مشاهدة المنعم<\/span>).” A man said to Ibrāhīm ibn Adham, may God be pleased with him: “When the spiritual poor are given, they give thanks; when they are denied, they are patient.” Ibrāhīm replied: “Those are the manners of dogs! Our people give thanks when denied, and when given, prefer others to themselves.”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:65–66 Ibn Juzayy said: “The blessings for which thanksgiving ( الشكر ) is rendered are of three kinds: worldly, such as health, well-being, and lawful wealth; religious, such as knowledge, reverent fear, and gnosis ( المعرفة ); and otherworldly, such as abundant reward for little work.” He also said: “People occupy two stations in thanksgiving. Some give thanks for blessings that reach them personally and belong especially to them; others thank God on behalf of all His creatures for blessings reaching them all. Thanksgiving has three degrees: the common people give thanks for blessings; the elect give thanks for blessings and afflictions, in every state; the elect of the elect become absent from seeing the blessing through witnessing the Benefactor ( مشاهدة المنعم ).” A man said to Ibrāhīm ibn Adham, may God be pleased with him: “When the spiritual poor are given, they give thanks; when they are denied, they are patient.” Ibrāhīm replied: “Those are the manners of dogs! Our people give thanks when denied, and when given, prefer others to themselves.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Wrath and straying","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:66<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-095\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E66.0\">He then guarded against the path that is not straight, saying, “not those upon whom wrath has fallen”: that is, not the path of those with whom You are angry. Do not guide us to it or lead us along its way, but keep us safe from the places to which it leads. The Jews are intended; thus the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, explained it. In its general application it holds of all those with whom God is angry. “Nor those who are astray”: that is, nor the path of those who are astray, who have lost the truth. These are the Christians, as he, God bless him and grant him peace, said. Both explanations are taken from the Book of God Most High. He says concerning the Jews, “They incurred wrath upon wrath” [2:90], and concerning the Christians, “They went astray before, led many astray, and strayed from the even way” [5:77].<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:66 He then guarded against the path that is not straight, saying, “not those upon whom wrath has fallen”: that is, not the path of those with whom You are angry. Do not guide us to it or lead us along its way, but keep us safe from the places to which it leads. The Jews are intended; thus the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, explained it. In its general application it holds of all those with whom God is angry. “Nor those who are astray”: that is, nor the path of those who are astray, who have lost the truth. These are the Christians, as he, God bless him and grant him peace, said. Both explanations are taken from the Book of God Most High. He says concerning the Jews, “They incurred wrath upon wrath” [2:90], and concerning the Christians, “They went astray before, led many astray, and strayed from the even way” [5:77]."},{"type":"theology","title":"The divine Names and the three classes of creation","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-096\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E66.1\">Know that the Real, glorified is He, has divided His creatures into three classes. One class He prepared for generosity and beneficence, to manifest in them His Name the Generous or the Ever-Merciful. These are those favoured with faith and uprightness. Another class He prepared for retribution and wrath, to manifest in them His Name the Avenger or the Overpowering. These are those upon whom wrath has fallen and who stray from the path of truth in understanding or in action: the unbelievers. A third class God prepared for forbearance and pardon, to manifest in them His Names the Forbearing and the Pardoner. These are the disobedient among the believers.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-097\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E66.2\">Whoever wishes existence to be free of these three classes, and all people to be alike in guidance or in its opposite, is ignorant of God and His Names. For the effects of His Names must appear in the human being: generosity, overpowering might, forbearance, and the rest. And God Most High knows best.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Know that the Real, glorified is He, has divided His creatures into three classes. One class He prepared for generosity and beneficence, to manifest in them His Name the Generous or the Ever-Merciful. These are those favoured with faith and uprightness. Another class He prepared for retribution and wrath, to manifest in them His Name the Avenger or the Overpowering. These are those upon whom wrath has fallen and who stray from the path of truth in understanding or in action: the unbelievers. A third class God prepared for forbearance and pardon, to manifest in them His Names the Forbearing and the Pardoner. These are the disobedient among the believers. Whoever wishes existence to be free of these three classes, and all people to be alike in guidance or in its opposite, is ignorant of God and His Names. For the effects of His Names must appear in the human being: generosity, overpowering might, forbearance, and the rest. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The path of arrival and spiritual guidance","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-099\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E66.3\">The straight path that the Real commands us to seek is the path of arrival (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طريق الوصول<\/span>) at the Presence (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحضرة<\/span>): knowledge of God in the mode of witnessing and direct vision (<span class=\"arinline\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلم بالله على نعت الشهود والعيان<\/span>). This is the station of the special tawḥīd (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام التوحيد الخاص<\/span>), the highest degree of the people of tawḥīd; above it is only the tawḥīd of the prophets and messengers. It necessarily requires spiritual formation at the hands of a perfected shaykh who knows the path of journeying (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طريق السير<\/span>), has traversed the stations through tasting (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذوقاً<\/span>) and unveiling (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كشفاً<\/span>), has attained the stations of effacement and subsistence (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء والبقاء<\/span>), and has combined attraction and wayfaring (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجذب والسلوك<\/span>). The path is difficult, its travellers few and its highwaymen many; the devil of this path is learned in its stations and the occurrences that arise in them (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نوازله<\/span>). A guide is therefore indispensable, otherwise the traveller will stray from the even way. Ibn al-Bannāʾ alluded to this meaning:<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-100\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E66.4\">The folk are travellers,<br/>journeying toward the Presence of the Real.<br/>Upon this way they therefore need a guide,<br/>one with insight into travel and the resting-place,<br/>who has travelled the path and then returned<br/>to tell the people what he gained.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:67<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-101\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E67.0\">In Laṭāʾif al-minan it is said: “Whoever has no master to connect him with the chain of following (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سلسلة الأتباع<\/span>) and lift the covering from his heart is, in this affair, a foundling without a father and a claimant without lineage. If he possesses light, the usual case is that the spiritual state overwhelms him, and that he stops with whatever comes to him from God. The governance of discipline and refinement (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سياسة التأديب والتهذيب<\/span>) has not trained him, nor have the reins of formation and exercise (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التربية والتدريب<\/span>) led him.” This path is what the reciter of al-Fātiḥa should inwardly call to mind at “Guide us upon the straight path,” together with the ascent (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الترقي<\/span>) mentioned earlier by Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī. At “the path of those whom You have blessed,” he should inwardly apprehend: those whom You blessed with arrival (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوصول<\/span>) and firm establishment (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التمكين<\/span>) in gnosis of You.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"The straight path that the Real commands us to seek is the path of arrival ( طريق الوصول ) at the Presence ( الحضرة ): knowledge of God in the mode of witnessing and direct vision ( العلم بالله على نعت الشهود والعيان ). This is the station of the special tawḥīd ( مقام التوحيد الخاص ), the highest degree of the people of tawḥīd; above it is only the tawḥīd of the prophets and messengers. It necessarily requires spiritual formation at the hands of a perfected shaykh who knows the path of journeying ( طريق السير ), has traversed the stations through tasting ( ذوقاً ) and unveiling ( كشفاً ), has attained the stations of effacement and subsistence ( الفناء والبقاء ), and has combined attraction and wayfaring ( الجذب والسلوك ). The path is difficult, its travellers few and its highwaymen many; the devil of this path is learned in its stations and the occurrences that arise in them ( نوازله ). A guide is therefore indispensable, otherwise the traveller will stray from the even way. Ibn al-Bannāʾ alluded to this meaning: The folk are travellers, journeying toward the Presence of the Real. Upon this way they therefore need a guide, one with insight into travel and the resting-place, who has travelled the path and then returned to tell the people what he gained. I:67 In Laṭāʾif al-minan it is said: “Whoever has no master to connect him with the chain of following ( سلسلة الأتباع ) and lift the covering from his heart is, in this affair, a foundling without a father and a claimant without lineage. If he possesses light, the usual case is that the spiritual state overwhelms him, and that he stops with whatever comes to him from God. The governance of discipline and refinement ( سياسة التأديب والتهذيب ) has not trained him, nor have the reins of formation and exercise ( التربية والتدريب ) led him.” This path is what the reciter of al-Fātiḥa should inwardly call to mind at “Guide us upon the straight path,” together with the ascent ( الترقي ) mentioned earlier by Shaykh Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Mursī. At “the path of those whom You have blessed,” he should inwardly apprehend: those whom You blessed with arrival ( الوصول ) and firm establishment ( التمكين ) in gnosis of You."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Guidance, deprivation and fulfilment","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-102\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E67.1\">Al-Wartajbī said: “Guide us to what You desire of us; the straight path is what the Real desires from creation: truthfulness and sincerity in His servanthood (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عبوديته<\/span>) and service.” Then: “It has been said: ‘Guide us with the guidance of direct vision (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هدى العيان<\/span>) after clear exposition (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البيان<\/span>), so that we may stand straight before You according to Your will.’ It has also been said: ‘Guide us with the guidance of one whose beginning is from You, so that his end may be unto You.’” He then quotes someone who said: “‘Guide us’ means: make us firm upon the path in which there is no crookedness, namely Islam, the straight path and upright way. ‘The path of those whom You have blessed’: the stations of those whom You blessed with gnosis, love, and beautiful courtesy in service.” He then said: “‘Not those upon whom wrath has fallen’: those expelled from the gate of servanthood. ‘Nor those who are astray’: those bankrupt of the precious treasures of gnosis.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-103\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E67.2\">I say: it is better to say that “not those upon whom wrath has fallen” are those whom following their egoic shares and appetites (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحظوظ والشهوات<\/span>) has halted from journeying (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السير<\/span>), casting them into the abysses of disobedience and opposition. “Nor those who are astray” are those whom ignorance and imitation (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجهل والتقليد<\/span>) have imprisoned, so that their inner sights (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بصائرهم<\/span>) do not penetrate to pure tawḥīd. They have recoiled from the tawḥīd of direct vision (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">توحيد العيان<\/span>) to the tawḥīd of proof and demonstration (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">توحيد الدليل والبرهان<\/span>). Among the people of witnessing and direct vision (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الشهود والعيان<\/span>), this is a kind of error, even if a person reaches the utmost possible degree of righteousness.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Wartajbī said: “Guide us to what You desire of us; the straight path is what the Real desires from creation: truthfulness and sincerity in His servanthood ( عبوديته ) and service.” Then: “It has been said: ‘Guide us with the guidance of direct vision ( هدى العيان ) after clear exposition ( البيان ), so that we may stand straight before You according to Your will.’ It has also been said: ‘Guide us with the guidance of one whose beginning is from You, so that his end may be unto You.’” He then quotes someone who said: “‘Guide us’ means: make us firm upon the path in which there is no crookedness, namely Islam, the straight path and upright way. ‘The path of those whom You have blessed’: the stations of those whom You blessed with gnosis, love, and beautiful courtesy in service.” He then said: “‘Not those upon whom wrath has fallen’: those expelled from the gate of servanthood. ‘Nor those who are astray’: those bankrupt of the precious treasures of gnosis.” I say: it is better to say that “not those upon whom wrath has fallen” are those whom following their egoic shares and appetites ( الحظوظ والشهوات ) has halted from journeying ( السير ), casting them into the abysses of disobedience and opposition. “Nor those who are astray” are those whom ignorance and imitation ( الجهل والتقليد ) have imprisoned, so that their inner sights ( بصائرهم ) do not penetrate to pure tawḥīd. They have recoiled from the tawḥīd of direct vision ( توحيد العيان ) to the tawḥīd of proof and demonstration ( توحيد الدليل والبرهان ). Among the people of witnessing and direct vision ( أهل الشهود والعيان ), this is a kind of error, even if a person reaches the utmost possible degree of righteousness."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Inward attentiveness when reciting al-Fātiḥa","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-104\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E67.3\">In the Iḥyāʾ it is said: “When you say, ‘In the Name of God, the All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful,’ understand that all affairs are through God, and that what is intended here is the One named. If all affairs are through God, it necessarily follows that all praise belongs to God.” Then he said: “When you say, ‘The All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful,’ bring the forms of His gentleness into your heart, so that His mercy may open to you and your hope may be stirred by it. Then let your heart feel magnification and fear at your words, ‘the Day of Recompense.’” Then he said: “Renew sincerity with your saying, ‘You alone we worship,’ and renew helplessness, need, and the disclaiming of strength and power with your saying, ‘and You alone we ask for help.’ Then ask for your need by name, and say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ which leads us to Your proximity and brings us to Your good pleasure. Add explanation, detail, and confirmation to it, and invoke the testimony of those upon whom He poured the blessings of guidance: the prophets, the utterly truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous, as distinct from those upon whom His wrath fell among the unbelievers, the deviating, the Jews, the Christians, and the Sabians.” Here ends the abridged quotation.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:68<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-105\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E68.0\">Al-Qushayrī said: “In ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ the imperative is implicit (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأمر مضمر<\/span>): that is, ‘Say: Guide us.’ The straight path is the path of the Real, the way of the people of tawḥīd. That is: ‘Direct us to the Real, lest we rely upon the intermediary means of our dealings (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وسائط المعاملات<\/span>) and the dust of suppositions and reckonings fall upon the face of tawḥīd; be Yourself our guide to You.’ ‘The path of those whom You have blessed’ means those who arrive through You unto You. ‘Not those upon whom wrath has fallen’: through forgetting divine enabling (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوفيق<\/span>) and deliberately blinding themselves to divine support (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التأييد<\/span>). ‘Nor those who are astray’: from witnessing the antecedence of divine choice (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سابق الاختيار<\/span>) and the unfolding dispositions of the decrees (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تصاريف الأقدار<\/span>).”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"In the Iḥyāʾ it is said: “When you say, ‘In the Name of God, the All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful,’ understand that all affairs are through God, and that what is intended here is the One named. If all affairs are through God, it necessarily follows that all praise belongs to God.” Then he said: “When you say, ‘The All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful,’ bring the forms of His gentleness into your heart, so that His mercy may open to you and your hope may be stirred by it. Then let your heart feel magnification and fear at your words, ‘the Day of Recompense.’” Then he said: “Renew sincerity with your saying, ‘You alone we worship,’ and renew helplessness, need, and the disclaiming of strength and power with your saying, ‘and You alone we ask for help.’ Then ask for your need by name, and say, ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ which leads us to Your proximity and brings us to Your good pleasure. Add explanation, detail, and confirmation to it, and invoke the testimony of those upon whom He poured the blessings of guidance: the prophets, the utterly truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous, as distinct from those upon whom His wrath fell among the unbelievers, the deviating, the Jews, the Christians, and the Sabians.” Here ends the abridged quotation. I:68 Al-Qushayrī said: “In ‘Guide us upon the straight path,’ the imperative is implicit ( الأمر مضمر ): that is, ‘Say: Guide us.’ The straight path is the path of the Real, the way of the people of tawḥīd. That is: ‘Direct us to the Real, lest we rely upon the intermediary means of our dealings ( وسائط المعاملات ) and the dust of suppositions and reckonings fall upon the face of tawḥīd; be Yourself our guide to You.’ ‘The path of those whom You have blessed’ means those who arrive through You unto You. ‘Not those upon whom wrath has fallen’: through forgetting divine enabling ( التوفيق ) and deliberately blinding themselves to divine support ( التأييد ). ‘Nor those who are astray’: from witnessing the antecedence of divine choice ( سابق الاختيار ) and the unfolding dispositions of the decrees ( تصاريف الأقدار ).”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"1. Al-Fātiḥa as a summary of the Qurʾān","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-107\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E68.1\">This sūrah gathers all the meanings of the Qurʾān, as though it were an abridged copy of it. For this reason it is called the Mother of the Qurʾān. Matters concerning divinity are contained in His saying, “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds, the All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful”; the abode of the hereafter in His saying, “King of the Day of Recompense”; all forms of worship, comprising beliefs and the outward ordinances entailed by commands and prohibitions, in His saying, “You alone we worship”; the spiritual stations and the mysteries of inward dealings, through purification and adornment [of the soul], in His saying, “Guide us upon the straight path”; the prophets and others in His saying, “those upon whom You have bestowed favour”; and the mention of the classes of unbelievers in His saying, “not those upon whom wrath has fallen, nor those who are astray.”<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-108\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E68.2\">Shaykh Ibn Abī Jamra, may God be pleased with him, said, explaining how it comprises the Book of God: “The word ‘praise’ includes everything in the Book of God relating to praise and thanksgiving, for praise is more general than thanksgiving: the more general term was used to indicate both attributes. The word ‘God’ indicates what is in the Mighty Book of Names of exaltation and magnification, since it has been said to be God’s Greatest Name. The phrase ‘Lord of the worlds’ indicates what it contains of the Names of God, glorified is He, and of the worlds, their differences, their Creator, and the One who disposes their affairs. ‘The All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful’ includes everything in the Book relating to forgiveness, mercy, the bestowal of blessings, pardon, and munificence. ‘King of the Day of Recompense’ indicates its mention of the hereafter and its terrors. ‘You alone we worship’ includes its acts of worship and the singling of Him out as God. ‘You alone we ask for help’ indicates its requests for assistance and its mention of utter need. ‘Guide us upon the straight path’ includes its requests for guidance upon the way of good. ‘The path of those upon whom You have bestowed favour’ includes its mention of the elect, those with whom God is pleased, those who are pardoned, and the people of felicity. ‘Not those upon whom wrath has fallen’ includes its kinds of unbelief and transgression, the evil qualities of those concerned, and their final end. It therefore deserves to be called a Mother.” Here ends the quotation.<\/p><\/div><span class=\"orig-page\">I:69<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-109\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E69.0\">It is related from ʿAlī, may God ennoble his countenance, that he said: “Moses, peace be upon him, expounded the Torah in seventy volumes. If the Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, gave me leave, I would load seventy camels with an exposition of al-Fātiḥa.” I say: his mention of “seventy” is approximate, for it admits of more than that. The detail would be lengthy. We have mentioned the foundations of its sciences in our greater commentary upon it. And God Most High knows best.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"This sūrah gathers all the meanings of the Qurʾān, as though it were an abridged copy of it. For this reason it is called the Mother of the Qurʾān. Matters concerning divinity are contained in His saying, “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds, the All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful”; the abode of the hereafter in His saying, “King of the Day of Recompense”; all forms of worship, comprising beliefs and the outward ordinances entailed by commands and prohibitions, in His saying, “You alone we worship”; the spiritual stations and the mysteries of inward dealings, through purification and adornment [of the soul], in His saying, “Guide us upon the straight path”; the prophets and others in His saying, “those upon whom You have bestowed favour”; and the mention of the classes of unbelievers in His saying, “not those upon whom wrath has fallen, nor those who are astray.” Shaykh Ibn Abī Jamra, may God be pleased with him, said, explaining how it comprises the Book of God: “The word ‘praise’ includes everything in the Book of God relating to praise and thanksgiving, for praise is more general than thanksgiving: the more general term was used to indicate both attributes. The word ‘God’ indicates what is in the Mighty Book of Names of exaltation and magnification, since it has been said to be God’s Greatest Name. The phrase ‘Lord of the worlds’ indicates what it contains of the Names of God, glorified is He, and of the worlds, their differences, their Creator, and the One who disposes their affairs. ‘The All-Merciful, the Ever-Merciful’ includes everything in the Book relating to forgiveness, mercy, the bestowal of blessings, pardon, and munificence. ‘King of the Day of Recompense’ indicates its mention of the hereafter and its terrors. ‘You alone we worship’ includes its acts of worship and the singling of Him out as God. ‘You alone we ask for help’ indicates its requests for assistance and its mention of utter need. ‘Guide us upon the straight path’ includes its requests for guidance upon the way of good. ‘The path of those upon whom You have bestowed favour’ includes its mention of the elect, those with whom God is pleased, those who are pardoned, and the people of felicity. ‘Not those upon whom wrath has fallen’ includes its kinds of unbelief and transgression, the evil qualities of those concerned, and their final end. It therefore deserves to be called a Mother.” Here ends the quotation. I:69 It is related from ʿAlī, may God ennoble his countenance, that he said: “Moses, peace be upon him, expounded the Torah in seventy volumes. If the Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, gave me leave, I would load seventy camels with an exposition of al-Fātiḥa.” I say: his mention of “seventy” is approximate, for it admits of more than that. The detail would be lengthy. We have mentioned the foundations of its sciences in our greater commentary upon it. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"theology","title":"2. Praise, tawḥīd and entering Islam","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-110\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E69.1\">Ibn Juzayy said: “According to the discerning scholars, our saying, ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,’ is superior to ‘There is no god but God,’ for two reasons. The first is what al-Nasāʾī related from the Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace: ‘Whoever says, “There is no god but God,” has twenty good deeds recorded for him, and whoever says, “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,” has thirty good deeds recorded for him.’ The second is that the affirmation of unity entailed by ‘There is no god but God’ is contained in your saying, ‘Lord of the worlds,’ while the phrase adds your words, ‘Praise belongs to God,’ with the meanings we have already explained. As for his saying, God bless him and grant him peace, ‘The best thing that I and the prophets before me have said is: There is no god but God,’ this is because of the affirmation of unity which it entails. ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds’ shares that affirmation and adds something to it. This applies to a believer who has made his faith firm and seeks reward. For one entering Islam, however, ‘There is no god but God’ is specifically required.” Here ends the quotation.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-111\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E69.2\">I say: The sound determination is that any expression indicating the affirmation of unity suffices for entering Islam, as al-Bannānī said in his gloss.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"Ibn Juzayy said: “According to the discerning scholars, our saying, ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,’ is superior to ‘There is no god but God,’ for two reasons. The first is what al-Nasāʾī related from the Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace: ‘Whoever says, “There is no god but God,” has twenty good deeds recorded for him, and whoever says, “Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds,” has thirty good deeds recorded for him.’ The second is that the affirmation of unity entailed by ‘There is no god but God’ is contained in your saying, ‘Lord of the worlds,’ while the phrase adds your words, ‘Praise belongs to God,’ with the meanings we have already explained. As for his saying, God bless him and grant him peace, ‘The best thing that I and the prophets before me have said is: There is no god but God,’ this is because of the affirmation of unity which it entails. ‘Praise belongs to God, Lord of the worlds’ shares that affirmation and adds something to it. This applies to a believer who has made his faith firm and seeks reward. For one entering Islam, however, ‘There is no god but God’ is specifically required.” Here ends the quotation. I say: The sound determination is that any expression indicating the affirmation of unity suffices for entering Islam, as al-Bannānī said in his gloss."},{"type":"legal","title":"3. Reciting al-Fātiḥa in prayer","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-112\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E69.3\">Reciting al-Fātiḥa in prayer is obligatory according to Mālik and al-Shāfiʿī, contrary to Abū Ḥanīfa. We have explained the origin of the disagreement in the greater commentary.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"Reciting al-Fātiḥa in prayer is obligatory according to Mālik and al-Shāfiʿī, contrary to Abū Ḥanīfa. We have explained the origin of the disagreement in the greater commentary."},{"type":"exposition","title":"4. Saying Āmīn: reports and practice","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-113\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E69.4\">Saying Āmīn upon completing al-Fātiḥa is called for because of the supplication it contains. The Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, said: “The Jews have envied you for nothing as they have envied you for the greeting of peace and for saying Āmīn.” Ibn Mājah related it. He also said, God bless him and grant him peace: “God has granted me three distinctions: He granted me prayer in rows; He granted me the greeting, which is indeed the greeting of the people of Paradise; and He granted me the saying of Āmīn. He gave it to none of the prophets before me, unless God gave it to Aaron: Moses would supplicate and Aaron would say Āmīn.” Ibn Khuzayma related it.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-114\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E69.5\">He, blessings and peace be upon him, heard a man supplicating insistently and said: “He has made it assured, if he seals it.” Some of those present asked, “With what should he seal it?” He replied: “He should say Āmīn; if he seals it with Āmīn, he has made it assured.” Abū Zuhayr, the transmitter, said: “Āmīn is like the seal (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطابع<\/span>) upon the sheet.” Ibn ʿAjība adds that this last statement may itself be traceable to the Prophet. Abū Dāwūd related the report.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":true,"text":"Saying Āmīn upon completing al-Fātiḥa is called for because of the supplication it contains. The Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, said: “The Jews have envied you for nothing as they have envied you for the greeting of peace and for saying Āmīn.” Ibn Mājah related it. He also said, God bless him and grant him peace: “God has granted me three distinctions: He granted me prayer in rows; He granted me the greeting, which is indeed the greeting of the people of Paradise; and He granted me the saying of Āmīn. He gave it to none of the prophets before me, unless God gave it to Aaron: Moses would supplicate and Aaron would say Āmīn.” Ibn Khuzayma related it. He, blessings and peace be upon him, heard a man supplicating insistently and said: “He has made it assured, if he seals it.” Some of those present asked, “With what should he seal it?” He replied: “He should say Āmīn; if he seals it with Āmīn, he has made it assured.” Abū Zuhayr, the transmitter, said: “Āmīn is like the seal ( الطابع ) upon the sheet.” Ibn ʿAjība adds that this last statement may itself be traceable to the Prophet. Abū Dāwūd related the report."},{"type":"language","title":"Āmīn: pronunciation, grammar and meaning","html":"<div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-115\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E69.6\">Āmīn may be pronounced with its initial vowel long or short (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بالمد والقصر<\/span>), with an ungeminated mīm; gemination of the mīm is also an attested linguistic form. It has been said to be one of the Names of God Most High. It has also been explained as “O God, answer,” “thus, do [it],” or “thus, let it be.” Al-Mundhirī mentions this in al-Targhīb. Al-Bayḍāwī said: “It is indeclinable with final fatḥa (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبني على الفتح<\/span>), like ayna, because of the meeting of two quiescent consonants (<span class=\"arinline short\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لالتقاء الساكنين<\/span>). Its alif is transmitted both long and short.” The poet said: “May God have mercy upon a servant who says Āmīn.”<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"Āmīn may be pronounced with its initial vowel long or short ( بالمد والقصر ), with an ungeminated mīm; gemination of the mīm is also an attested linguistic form. It has been said to be one of the Names of God Most High. It has also been explained as “O God, answer,” “thus, do [it],” or “thus, let it be.” Al-Mundhirī mentions this in al-Targhīb. Al-Bayḍāwī said: “It is indeclinable with final fatḥa ( مبني على الفتح ), like ayna, because of the meeting of two quiescent consonants ( لالتقاء الساكنين ). Its alif is transmitted both long and short.” The poet said: “May God have mercy upon a servant who says Āmīn.”"},{"type":"legal","title":"Āmīn in congregational prayer","html":"<span class=\"orig-page\">I:70<\/span><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-116\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E70.0\">“By agreement, it is not part of the Qurʾān. It is, however, a sunna to conclude the sūrah with it, because of his saying, blessings and peace be upon him: ‘Gabriel, peace be upon him, taught me Āmīn when I finished reciting al-Fātiḥa.’ He said that it is like the seal upon a letter.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-117\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E70.1\">“The imam says it, and says it aloud in prayers recited aloud, because of the report from Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr that when he, blessings and peace be upon him, recited ‘nor those who are astray,’ he would say Āmīn, raising his voice with it. It is related from Abū Ḥanīfa, may God have mercy upon him, that the imam does not say it. The well-known position related from him is that the imam says it quietly, as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mughaffal and Anas reported.” I say: the well-known position of Mālik’s school is that the imam does not say it in prayers recited aloud.<\/p><\/div><div class=\"commentary\" id=\"pair-118\"><p class=\"enp\" data-source-id=\"E70.2\">Then he said: “The person praying behind the imam says Āmīn with him, because of his saying, blessings and peace be upon him: ‘When the imam says, “nor those who are astray,” say Āmīn, for the angels say Āmīn. Whoever’s saying of Āmīn coincides with the angels’ saying of Āmīn will have his previous sins forgiven.’” Success is through God, and He guides to the even way. May God bless our master and patron Muḥammad, the very reality of truth and realisation, and his family and his Companions, purified after him, the standards of the path, and grant them abundant peace.<\/p><\/div>","authorial":false,"text":"I:70 “By agreement, it is not part of the Qurʾān. It is, however, a sunna to conclude the sūrah with it, because of his saying, blessings and peace be upon him: ‘Gabriel, peace be upon him, taught me Āmīn when I finished reciting al-Fātiḥa.’ He said that it is like the seal upon a letter. “The imam says it, and says it aloud in prayers recited aloud, because of the report from Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr that when he, blessings and peace be upon him, recited ‘nor those who are astray,’ he would say Āmīn, raising his voice with it. It is related from Abū Ḥanīfa, may God have mercy upon him, that the imam does not say it. The well-known position related from him is that the imam says it quietly, as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mughaffal and Anas reported.” I say: the well-known position of Mālik’s school is that the imam does not say it in prayers recited aloud. Then he said: “The person praying behind the imam says Āmīn with him, because of his saying, blessings and peace be upon him: ‘When the imam says, “nor those who are astray,” say Āmīn, for the angels say Āmīn. Whoever’s saying of Āmīn coincides with the angels’ saying of Āmīn will have his previous sins forgiven.’” Success is through God, and He guides to the even way. May God bless our master and patron Muḥammad, the very reality of truth and realisation, and his family and his Companions, purified after him, the standards of the path, and grant them abundant peace."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C64.1","page":64,"ar":"هذا شطر بيت، وتمامه: (فمضيت ثمة قلت لا يعنيني).","en":"This is one hemistich; its continuation is: “I passed on, then said: it is no concern of mine.”","anchor":"ولقد أمر على اللئيم يسبني"},{"id":"C69.1","page":69,"ar":"هذا شطر بيت، أوله: (يا رب لا تسلبني حبها أبدا...). ونسبه ابن منظور في اللسان إلى عمر بن أبي ربيعة. قلت: وقد أغفل الشيخ المفسر ذكر مثال القصر. وهو كما في أنوار التنزيل ولسان العرب:\nتباعد مني فطحل، إذ سألته\nأمين فزاد الله ما بيننا بعدا","en":"This is one hemistich. Its beginning is: “O Lord, never deprive me of love for her …” Ibn Manẓūr attributes it in the Lisān to ʿUmar ibn Abī Rabīʿa. I [the editor] say: the shaykh who wrote this commentary has omitted an example of the short form. It appears as follows in Anwār al-tanzīl and Lisān al-ʿArab: “Fuṭḥul drew away from me when I asked him; / Amīn—so may God increase the distance between us.”","anchor":"ويرحم الله عبدا قال آمينا"}],"english_characters":21155,"arabic_characters":12359},{"id":"intro2","q":"intro2","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"Al-Baqara: introduction and excellence","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 71–71","cairo":"I:71","source_page_first":71,"source_page_last":71,"ar":"سورة البقرة\nقال سيدنا علي- كرّم الله وجهه-: (أول سورة نزلت بالمدينة سورة البقرة)(1). وفيها ستة آلاف ومائة وإحدى وعشرون كلمة، ومائتان وست وثمانون آية، وقيل: سبع وثمانون. قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلّم: «لكلّ شيء سنام، وإنّ سنام القرآن سورة البقرة. من قرأها فى بيته نهارا لم يدخله شيطان ثلاثة أيام، ومن قرأها فى بيته ليلا لم يدخله شيطان ثلاث ليال، وفيها سيّدة آي القرآن، وهى آية الكرسي» . وإنما كانت سنام القرآن، أي ذروته لأنها اشتملت على جملة ما فيه من أحوال الإيمان وفروع الإسلام.\nوقال صلى الله عليه وسلّم: «أعطيت سورة البقرة من الذكر الأول، وأعطيت طه والطواسين من ألواح موسى، وأعطيت فاتحة الكتاب، وخواتيم البقرة من تحت العرش» .\nثم افتتح السورة برموز رمز بها بينه وبين حبيبه، فقال:","quran_ar":"","quran_en":"","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Revelation, extent and excellence of the sūrah","html":"<p>Our master ʿAlī, may God ennoble his countenance, said: “The first sūrah revealed in Medina was Sūrat al-Baqara<a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/CP11\" title=\"Cairo print critical note CP11\">[CP11]<\/a>.” It contains six thousand, one hundred and twenty-one words, and two hundred and eighty-six verses—or, it is said, two hundred and eighty-seven.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-5\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B001]<\/a> <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Source comparison:<\/strong> Al-Thaʿlabī, <i>al-Kashf wa-l-bayān<\/i>, I:135, independently transmits this sentence through ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī from ʿIkrima. His preceding heading mentions ʿAlī in connection with Kūfan verse enumeration, whereas the chain for the first Medinan sūrah ends with ʿIkrima. This directly inspected parallel supports the Cairo editor’s existing caution about the attribution. The English preserves Ibn ʿAjība’s attribution to ʿAlī; the source comparison establishes an attribution discrepancy rather than authenticating the reported revelation chronology.<\/span><\/p><p>The Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, said: “Everything has a summit, and the summit of the Qurʾān is Sūrat al-Baqara. Whoever recites it in his house by day, no devil will enter it for three days; whoever recites it in his house by night, no devil will enter it for three nights. Within it is the foremost verse of the Qurʾān: the Throne Verse.” It is the summit of the Qurʾān—that is, its highest point—because it embraces a compendium of the Qurʾān’s teachings concerning the states of faith and the branches of Islam.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-6\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B002]<\/a><\/p><p>He also said, God bless him and grant him peace: “I was given Sūrat al-Baqara from the First Remembrance; Ṭā Hā and the Ṭawāsīn from the tablets of Moses; and the Opening of the Book and the concluding verses of al-Baqara from beneath the Throne.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-7\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B003]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Our master ʿAlī, may God ennoble his countenance, said: “The first sūrah revealed in Medina was Sūrat al-Baqara [CP11] .” It contains six thousand, one hundred and twenty-one words, and two hundred and eighty-six verses—or, it is said, two hundred and eighty-seven. [B001] Source comparison: Al-Thaʿlabī, al-Kashf wa-l-bayān , I:135, independently transmits this sentence through ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī from ʿIkrima. His preceding heading mentions ʿAlī in connection with Kūfan verse enumeration, whereas the chain for the first Medinan sūrah ends with ʿIkrima. This directly inspected parallel supports the Cairo editor’s existing caution about the attribution. The English preserves Ibn ʿAjība’s attribution to ʿAlī; the source comparison establishes an attribution discrepancy rather than authenticating the reported revelation chronology. The Messenger of God, God bless him and grant him peace, said: “Everything has a summit, and the summit of the Qurʾān is Sūrat al-Baqara. Whoever recites it in his house by day, no devil will enter it for three days; whoever recites it in his house by night, no devil will enter it for three nights. Within it is the foremost verse of the Qurʾān: the Throne Verse.” It is the summit of the Qurʾān—that is, its highest point—because it embraces a compendium of the Qurʾān’s teachings concerning the states of faith and the branches of Islam. [B002] He also said, God bless him and grant him peace: “I was given Sūrat al-Baqara from the First Remembrance; Ṭā Hā and the Ṭawāsīn from the tablets of Moses; and the Opening of the Book and the concluding verses of al-Baqara from beneath the Throne.” [B003]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then opens the sūrah with symbols exchanged between Himself and His Beloved, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then opens the sūrah with symbols exchanged between Himself and His Beloved, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C71.1","page":71,"ar":"ذكره السيوطي في الدر المنثور ١/٤٦ عن عكرمة وعزاه لأبي داود في الناسخ والمنسوخ. ولم أقف عليه منسوبا إلى سيدنا علي- كرم الله وجهه-.","en":"Al-Suyūṭī mentions it in al-Durr al-manthūr, 1:46, from ʿIkrima, and attributes it to Abū Dāwūd in al-Nāsikh wa-l-mansūkh. I have not found it attributed to our master ʿAlī—may God ennoble his countenance.","anchor":"أول سورة نزلت بالمدينة سورة البقرة"}],"english_characters":1728,"arabic_characters":684},{"id":"q2-1","q":"2:1","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:1 · The opening letters and the descent of revelation","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 71–72","cairo":"I:71","source_page_first":71,"source_page_last":71,"ar":"الم (1)\nوقد حارت العقول في رموز الحكماء، فكيف بالأنبياء؟ فكيف بالمرسلين؟ فكيف بسيد المرسلين؟، فكيف يطمع أحد في إدراك حقائق رموز رب العالمين؟! قال الصديق رضى الله عنه: (في كل كتاب سر وسر، القرآن فواتح السور) . هـ.\nفمعرفة أسرار هذه الحروف لا يقف عليها إلا الصفوة من أكابر الأولياء. وكل واحد يلمع له على قدر صفاء شربه.\nوأقرب ما فيها أنها أشياء أقسم الله بها لشرفها. فقيل: إنها مختصرة من أسمائه تعالى، فالألف من الله، واللام من اللطيف، والميم من مهيمن أو مجيد. وقيل: من أسماء نبيه صلى الله عليه وسلّم فالميم مختصرة إما من المصطفى، ويدل عليه زيادة الصاد فى المص، أو من المرسل، ويدل عليه زيادة الراء فى المر. والر مختصرة من الرسول. فكأن الحق تعالى يقول: يا أيها المصطفى، أو يا أيها الرسول","quran_ar":"بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمنِ الرَّحِيمِ\nالم (1)","quran_en":"In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful.\n1. Alif. Lām. Mīm.","segments":[{"type":"spiritual","title":"The mysteries of the opening letters","html":"<p>If intellects are bewildered by the symbols of sages, what then of prophets? What of messengers? What of the master of the messengers? How, then, could anyone aspire to grasp the realities of the symbols of the Lord of the worlds? Al-Ṣiddīq, may God be pleased with him, said: “Every book has a secret, and the secret of the Qurʾān is the opening of its sūrahs.” End of quotation.<\/p><p>Knowledge of the mysteries of these letters is accessible only to the elect among the greatest saints (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأولياء<\/bdi>). A gleam of it appears to each according to the purity of his draught (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صفاء شربه<\/bdi>).<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-8\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B004]<\/a><\/p><p>The most plausible explanation is that they are things by which God swears because of their nobility. Thus it has been said that they are abbreviated from His Names: the alif from Allāh, the lām from al-Laṭīf, and the mīm from Muhaymin or Majīd. Others say they are abbreviated from the names of His Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace: the mīm from <i>al-Muṣṭafā<\/i>, as the additional ṣād in <i>Alif Lām Mīm Ṣād<\/i> indicates, or from <i>al-Mursal<\/i>, as the additional rāʾ in <i>Alif Lām Mīm Rāʾ<\/i> indicates. <i>Alif Lām Rāʾ<\/i> is abbreviated from <i>al-Rasūl<\/i>. It is as though the Real Most High were saying, “O Chosen One,” or “O Messenger: ‘That is the Book, in which there is no doubt,’” or “‘A Book sent down to you,’” or the like. The fact that the address is directed to him, God bless him and grant him peace, after these symbols supports this interpretation.<\/p><p><i>Kāf Hā Yā ʿAyn Ṣād<\/i> is abbreviated from al-Kāfī, al-Hādī, al-Walī, al-ʿĀlim and al-Ṣādiq; <i>Ṭā Hā<\/i> from ṭāhir, “pure”; <i>Ṭā Sīn<\/i> from “O Pure One, O Master,” with “O Muḥammad” added in <i>Ṭā Sīn Mīm<\/i>; and so forth.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"If intellects are bewildered by the symbols of sages, what then of prophets? What of messengers? What of the master of the messengers? How, then, could anyone aspire to grasp the realities of the symbols of the Lord of the worlds? Al-Ṣiddīq, may God be pleased with him, said: “Every book has a secret, and the secret of the Qurʾān is the opening of its sūrahs.” End of quotation. Knowledge of the mysteries of these letters is accessible only to the elect among the greatest saints ( الأولياء ). A gleam of it appears to each according to the purity of his draught ( صفاء شربه ). [B004] The most plausible explanation is that they are things by which God swears because of their nobility. Thus it has been said that they are abbreviated from His Names: the alif from Allāh, the lām from al-Laṭīf, and the mīm from Muhaymin or Majīd. Others say they are abbreviated from the names of His Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace: the mīm from al-Muṣṭafā , as the additional ṣād in Alif Lām Mīm Ṣād indicates, or from al-Mursal , as the additional rāʾ in Alif Lām Mīm Rāʾ indicates. Alif Lām Rāʾ is abbreviated from al-Rasūl . It is as though the Real Most High were saying, “O Chosen One,” or “O Messenger: ‘That is the Book, in which there is no doubt,’” or “‘A Book sent down to you,’” or the like. The fact that the address is directed to him, God bless him and grant him peace, after these symbols supports this interpretation. Kāf Hā Yā ʿAyn Ṣād is abbreviated from al-Kāfī, al-Hādī, al-Walī, al-ʿĀlim and al-Ṣādiq; Ṭā Hā from ṭāhir, “pure”; Ṭā Sīn from “O Pure One, O Master,” with “O Muḥammad” added in Ṭā Sīn Mīm ; and so forth."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Letters, divine Names and the realms of manifestation","html":"<p>According to the people of allusion, the Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “Alif: devote your innermost secret (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سرّك<\/bdi>) exclusively to Me, as the alif stands apart from the other letters. Lām: make your limbs pliant in My worship. Mīm: abide with Me through the effacement of your outlines and attributes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">محو رسومك وصفاتك<\/bdi>), and I shall adorn you with the purity of intimacy and nearness to Me.” This is reported by al-Thaʿlabī.<\/p><p>I say: more evidently, these letters point to the three realms (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العوالم الثلاثة<\/bdi>). Alif signifies the oneness of the Essence in the realm of jabarūt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الجبروت<\/bdi>); lām, the appearance of its mysteries in the realm of malakūt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الملكوت<\/bdi>); mīm, the pervasion of its sustaining influxes in the realm of raḥamūt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الرحموت<\/bdi>); and ṣād, the manifestation of its governance in the realm of mulk (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الملك<\/bdi>).<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-9\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B005]<\/a><\/p><p>Each of these symbolic letters indicates the appearance of the Essence’s effect in the realm of witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عالم الشهادة<\/bdi>). Alif points to the pervasion of oneness in the manifestations of created things; lām, to the overflowing of the lights of malakūt from the ocean of jabarūt; and mīm, to the King’s governance in the realm of mulk. It is as though the Real Most High were saying: “This Book which you recite, O Muḥammad, flows from the ocean of jabarūt into the realm of malakūt, and from malakūt into raḥamūt. The Trustworthy Spirit then brought it down to the realm of mulk and witnessing. One ought not, therefore, to doubt it.” Hence He follows these letters with His words: “That is the Book, in which there is no doubt.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"According to the people of allusion, the Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “Alif: devote your innermost secret ( سرّك ) exclusively to Me, as the alif stands apart from the other letters. Lām: make your limbs pliant in My worship. Mīm: abide with Me through the effacement of your outlines and attributes ( محو رسومك وصفاتك ), and I shall adorn you with the purity of intimacy and nearness to Me.” This is reported by al-Thaʿlabī. I say: more evidently, these letters point to the three realms ( العوالم الثلاثة ). Alif signifies the oneness of the Essence in the realm of jabarūt ( عالم الجبروت ); lām, the appearance of its mysteries in the realm of malakūt ( عالم الملكوت ); mīm, the pervasion of its sustaining influxes in the realm of raḥamūt ( عالم الرحموت ); and ṣād, the manifestation of its governance in the realm of mulk ( عالم الملك ). [B005] Each of these symbolic letters indicates the appearance of the Essence’s effect in the realm of witnessing ( عالم الشهادة ). Alif points to the pervasion of oneness in the manifestations of created things; lām, to the overflowing of the lights of malakūt from the ocean of jabarūt; and mīm, to the King’s governance in the realm of mulk. It is as though the Real Most High were saying: “This Book which you recite, O Muḥammad, flows from the ocean of jabarūt into the realm of malakūt, and from malakūt into raḥamūt. The Trustworthy Spirit then brought it down to the realm of mulk and witnessing. One ought not, therefore, to doubt it.” Hence He follows these letters with His words: “That is the Book, in which there is no doubt.”"},{"type":"language","title":"Doubt and tranquillity","html":"<p>I say: <i>rayb<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الريب<\/bdi>) is the heart’s movement and agitation through doubts and imaginings. Its opposite is tranquillity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطمأنينة<\/bdi>), through abiding rest in the Real.<\/p><p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “O chosen Messenger and elect Prophet, that Book which We have sent down to you from the jabarūt of Our holiness and the malakūt of Our might—there is no doubt that it is from Us. Whoever doubts it or attributes it to another has merited distance from the precinct of Our mercy and incurred the severity of Our retribution. Whoever realizes that it is from Us and believes in the one who has brought it from Us has merited entry into the Presence of Our holiness, until he hears from Us and speaks through Us: ‘When I love him, I am his; through Me he hears, through Me he sees, and through Me he speaks …’”—the hadith.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-10\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B006]<\/a><\/p><p>Thus he becomes one of the utterly truthful brought near (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصديقين المقربين<\/bdi>), with the prophets and messengers, and reaches the summit of the degrees of those who are mindful of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتقين<\/bdi>), who are guided by the clear Qurʾān’s guidance. He points to this in His words:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: rayb ( الريب ) is the heart’s movement and agitation through doubts and imaginings. Its opposite is tranquillity ( الطمأنينة ), through abiding rest in the Real. The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “O chosen Messenger and elect Prophet, that Book which We have sent down to you from the jabarūt of Our holiness and the malakūt of Our might—there is no doubt that it is from Us. Whoever doubts it or attributes it to another has merited distance from the precinct of Our mercy and incurred the severity of Our retribution. Whoever realizes that it is from Us and believes in the one who has brought it from Us has merited entry into the Presence of Our holiness, until he hears from Us and speaks through Us: ‘When I love him, I am his; through Me he hears, through Me he sees, and through Me he speaks …’”—the hadith. [B006] Thus he becomes one of the utterly truthful brought near ( الصديقين المقربين ), with the prophets and messengers, and reaches the summit of the degrees of those who are mindful of God ( المتقين ), who are guided by the clear Qurʾān’s guidance. He points to this in His words:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4340,"arabic_characters":682},{"id":"q2-2","q":"2:2","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:2 · Guidance, God-consciousness and the degrees of recitation","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 72–73","cairo":"I:71–73","source_page_first":71,"source_page_last":73,"ar":"ذلِكَ الْكِتابُ لا رَيْبَ فِيهِ أوهذا كِتابٌ أُنْزِلَ إِلَيْكَ أو غير ذلك، ويدل على هذا توجيه الخطاب إليه صلى الله عليه وسلّم بعد هذه الرموز. وكهيعص مختصرة من الكافي والهادي والولي والعالم والصادق، وطه من طاهر، وطس من يا طاهر يا سيد، ويا محمد في طسم، إلى غير ذلك.\nوعند أهل الإشارة يقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ألف: أفْرِدْ سِرَّك إِلّيَّ، انفراد الألف عن سائر الحروف، واللام: لَيِّنْ جوارحك لعبادتي، والميم: أقم معي بمحو رسومك وصفاتك، أزينك بصفاء الأنس والقرب مني. قاله الثعلبي.\nقلت: والأظهر أنها حروف تشير للعوالم الثلاثة، فالألف لوحدة الذات في عالم الجبروت، واللام لظهور أسرارها في عالم الملكوت، والميم لسريان أمدادها في عالم الرحموت، والصاد لظهور تصرفها في عالم الملك.\nوكل حرف من هذه الرموز يدل على ظهور أثر الذات في عالم الشهادة، فالألف يشير إلى سريان الوحدة في مظاهر الأكوان، واللام: يشير إلى فيضان أنوار الملكوت من بحر الجبروت، والميم يشير إلى تصرف الملك في عالم الملك، وكأن الحق تعالى يقول: هذا الكتاب الذي تتلو يا محمد- هو فائض من بحر الجبروت إلى عالم الملكوت، ومن عالَم الملكوت إلى الرحموت، ثم نَزَلَ به الروح الأمين إلى عالم الملك والشهادة، فلا ينبغي أن يرتاب فيه، ولذلك رتب عليه قوله تعالى:\nذلِكَ الْكِتابُ لا رَيْبَ فِيهِ ...\nقلت: الريب: تحرُّك القلب واضطرابه بالشكوك والأوهام، وتقابله الطمأنينة بالسكون إلى الحق على الدوام.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أيها الرسول المصطفى والنبيّ المجتبى ذلِكَ الْكِتابُ الذي أنزلناه عليك من جبروت قدسنا وملكوت عزِّنا لا رَيْبَ فِيهِ أنه من عندنا. فمن ارتاب فيه، أو نسبه إلى غيرنا، فقد استحق البعد من ساحة رحمتنا، وحلّت عليه شدائد نقمتنا، ومن تحقق به أنه من لدنا، وآمن بمن جاء به من عندنا، فقد استحق دخول حضرة قدسنا حتى يسمع منا ويتكلم بنا، فإذا أحببته كنت له، فبي يسمع، وبي يبصر، وبي يتكلم ... الحديث.\nفيكون من الصديقين المقربين مع النبيين والمرسلين، وكان في ذروة درجات المتقين، الذين يهتدون بهدي القرآن المبين، كما أشار إلى ذلك بقوله:\n\n... هُدىً لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (2)\nقلت: (هدى) خبر عن مبتدأ مضمر، أو مبتدأ بتقديم الخبر. أي: هو هاد للمتقين، أو فيه الهدى لهم. والهدى:\nهو الإرشاد والبيان، ومعناه: الدلالة الموصلة إلى الحق. والمتقي: من جعل بينه وبين مقت الله وقاية، وله ثلاث درجات:\nحفظ الجوارح من المخالفات، وحفظ القلوب من المساوئ والهفوات، وحفظ السرائر من الوقوف مع المحسوسات،\nفالأولى لمقام الإسلام، وإليه توجه الخطاب بقوله: فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ مَا اسْتَطَعْتُمْ، والثانية لمقام الإيمان، وإليه توجه الخطاب بقوله: فَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ يا أُولِي الْأَلْبابِ، والثالثة لمقام الإحسان، وإليه توجه الخطاب بقوله: اتَّقُوا اللَّهَ حَقَّ تُقاتِهِ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ذلِكَ الْكِتابُ الذي لا يقرب ساحتَه شكٍّ ولا ارتياب، هو عين الهداية لأهل التقى من ذوي الألباب، فلا يزالون يَتَرَقَّوْنَ به في المقامات والأحوال حتى يسمعوه من الكبير المتعال، بلا واسطة تبليغ ولا إرسال، قد انمحت في حقهم الرسوم والأشكال، وهذه غاية الهداية، وتحقيق سابق العناية.\nقال جعفر الصادق: (والله لقد تجلّى الله تعالى لخلقه في كلامه ولكن لا يشعرون) وقال أيضاً- وقد سألوه عن حالة لحقَته في الصلاة حتى خَرّ مغشيًّا عليه، فلما سُرِّيَ عنه، قيل له في ذلك فقال-: (ما زلت أردد الآية على قلبي حتى سمعتها من المتكلم بها، فلم يثبت جسمي لمعاينة قدرته) .\n\nفدرجات القراءة ثلاث:\nأدناها: أن يقرأ العبد كأنه يقرأ على الله تعالى واقفاً بين يديه، وهو ناظر له ومستمع منه، فيكون حاله السؤال والتملُّق والتضرّع والابتهال.\nوالثانية: أن يشهد بقلبه كأن الله تعالى يُخاطبه بألفاظه، ويُناجيه بإنعامه وإحسانه، فمقامه الحياء والتعظيم، والإصغاء والفهم.\nوالثالثة: أن يرى في الكلام المتكلِّم، فلا ينظر إلى نفسه ولا إلى قراءته، بل يكون فانيّا عن نفسه، غائباً في شهود ربه، لم يبق له عن نفسه إخبار ولا مع غير الله قرار.\nفالأولى لأهل الفناء في الأفعال، والثانية لأهل الفناء في الصفات، والثالثة لأهل الفناء في شهود الذات، رضى الله عنهم، وحشرنا على منهاجهم.. آمين.\nثم وصف المتقين، الذين خصوا بهداية كتابه المبين، بثلاثة أوصاف، فقال:","quran_ar":"ذلِكَ الْكِتابُ لا رَيْبَ فِيهِ هُدىً لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (2)","quran_en":"2. That is the Book, in which there is no doubt: a guidance for those who are mindful of God.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The grammar of guidance and the degrees of taqwā","html":"<p>I say: “guidance” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هدى<\/bdi>) is either the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>) of an implicit subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ مضمر<\/bdi>), or itself the subject with its predicate placed before it: “It is a guide for those mindful of God,” or “Within it is guidance for them.” Guidance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهدى<\/bdi>) is direction and clear explanation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإرشاد والبيان<\/bdi>); its meaning is an indication that brings one to the Real (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الدلالة الموصلة إلى الحق<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>The person mindful of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتقي<\/bdi>) is one who places a safeguard (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وقاية<\/bdi>) between himself and God’s displeasure. This has three degrees: guarding the limbs against disobedience; guarding hearts against defects and lapses; and guarding the innermost secrets against stopping with sensible things.<\/p><p>The first belongs to the station of Islam (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإسلام<\/bdi>), to which the address “Be mindful of God as far as you are able” [64:16] is directed. The second belongs to the station of faith (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإيمان<\/bdi>), to which “Be mindful of God, O possessors of understanding” [65:10] is directed. The third belongs to the station of excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإحسان<\/bdi>), to which “Be mindful of God as He ought to be regarded” [3:102] is directed.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “guidance” ( هدى ) is either the predicate ( خبر ) of an implicit subject ( مبتدأ مضمر ), or itself the subject with its predicate placed before it: “It is a guide for those mindful of God,” or “Within it is guidance for them.” Guidance ( الهدى ) is direction and clear explanation ( الإرشاد والبيان ); its meaning is an indication that brings one to the Real ( الدلالة الموصلة إلى الحق ). The person mindful of God ( المتقي ) is one who places a safeguard ( وقاية ) between himself and God’s displeasure. This has three degrees: guarding the limbs against disobedience; guarding hearts against defects and lapses; and guarding the innermost secrets against stopping with sensible things. The first belongs to the station of Islam ( مقام الإسلام ), to which the address “Be mindful of God as far as you are able” [64:16] is directed. The second belongs to the station of faith ( مقام الإيمان ), to which “Be mindful of God, O possessors of understanding” [65:10] is directed. The third belongs to the station of excellence ( مقام الإحسان ), to which “Be mindful of God as He ought to be regarded” [3:102] is directed."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"From guidance to hearing the Speaker","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “That Book, whose precinct neither doubt nor uncertainty approaches, is guidance itself for the God-conscious possessed of understanding.” Through it they continue to ascend in stations and states (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المقامات والأحوال<\/bdi>) until they hear it from the Great, the Most High, without an intermediary conveying or transmitting it. Outlines and forms (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرسوم والأشكال<\/bdi>) have been effaced for them. This is guidance’s utmost attainment and the realization of antecedent providential care (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سابق العناية<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq said: “By God, God Most High has disclosed Himself to His creatures in His speech, but they do not perceive it.” He also said, when asked about a state that overtook him in prayer until he fell unconscious, and then recovered: “I continued repeating the verse upon my heart until I heard it from the One who speaks it. My body could not withstand the direct vision of His power.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: “That Book, whose precinct neither doubt nor uncertainty approaches, is guidance itself for the God-conscious possessed of understanding.” Through it they continue to ascend in stations and states ( المقامات والأحوال ) until they hear it from the Great, the Most High, without an intermediary conveying or transmitting it. Outlines and forms ( الرسوم والأشكال ) have been effaced for them. This is guidance’s utmost attainment and the realization of antecedent providential care ( سابق العناية ). Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq said: “By God, God Most High has disclosed Himself to His creatures in His speech, but they do not perceive it.” He also said, when asked about a state that overtook him in prayer until he fell unconscious, and then recovered: “I continued repeating the verse upon my heart until I heard it from the One who speaks it. My body could not withstand the direct vision of His power.”"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The three degrees of recitation","html":"<p>The degrees of recitation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">درجات القراءة<\/bdi>) are three. The lowest is for the servant to recite as though he were reciting before God Most High, standing in His presence while He looks upon him and listens to him. His state is then one of petition, humble entreaty, supplication and imploring.<\/p><p>The second is to witness in his heart as though God Most High were addressing him with His words and speaking intimately to him through His bounty and beneficence. His station is modesty, magnification, attentive listening and understanding.<\/p><p>The third is to behold the Speaker in the speech, looking neither to himself nor to his recitation. Rather, he is effaced from himself (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فانيًا عن نفسه<\/bdi>), absent in witnessing his Lord: no report of himself remains to him, nor any resting with other than God.<\/p><p>The first belongs to the people of effacement in acts (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء في الأفعال<\/bdi>); the second to the people of effacement in attributes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء في الصفات<\/bdi>); and the third to the people of effacement in witnessing the Essence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفناء في شهود الذات<\/bdi>). May God be pleased with them and gather us upon their way. Amen.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The degrees of recitation ( درجات القراءة ) are three. The lowest is for the servant to recite as though he were reciting before God Most High, standing in His presence while He looks upon him and listens to him. His state is then one of petition, humble entreaty, supplication and imploring. The second is to witness in his heart as though God Most High were addressing him with His words and speaking intimately to him through His bounty and beneficence. His station is modesty, magnification, attentive listening and understanding. The third is to behold the Speaker in the speech, looking neither to himself nor to his recitation. Rather, he is effaced from himself ( فانيًا عن نفسه ), absent in witnessing his Lord: no report of himself remains to him, nor any resting with other than God. The first belongs to the people of effacement in acts ( الفناء في الأفعال ); the second to the people of effacement in attributes ( الفناء في الصفات ); and the third to the people of effacement in witnessing the Essence ( الفناء في شهود الذات ). May God be pleased with them and gather us upon their way. Amen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then describes those mindful of God, singled out for the guidance of His clear Book, by three qualities, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then describes those mindful of God, singled out for the guidance of His clear Book, by three qualities, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3277,"arabic_characters":3586}];
